export const ROZDZIALY = [
  {"nr":"1","tytul":"Makietka at a glance","czesc":"Basics","ikona":"ksiazka","sekcje":[{"id":"1.1","tytul":"What you can do in Makietka","html":"<p>Makietka is an editor for print publications: books, calendars, planners, notebooks, invitations, labels — page by page, like on a layout mock-up. At the end you get a PDF file ready for the print shop. It has what a multi-page publication needs:</p><ul class=\"punkty\"><li><b>sections</b> with different formats — e.g. a cover with a spine calculated from the page count and paper (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"10.6\">Cover with a spine</a>) and the body (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"10.4\">Section settings</a>),</li><li><b>spreads</b> with mirrored margins, like in a book (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"10.5\">Spreads</a>),</li><li><b>masters</b> — backgrounds, footers and page numbers drawn once for many pages (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"11\">Master pages</a>),</li><li><b>text styles</b> — one look for all headings or captions (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"5.4\">Styles</a>),</li><li><b>a print-ready file</b> with bleed, crop marks and embedded fonts, also PDF/X-4 in CMYK (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"21\">Export to PDF and PNG</a>).</li></ul><p>Makietka's strong point is <b>data</b>. You can repeat a page for every row of a table and insert the values from that row into it: that's how you make a 365-day calendar, a planner for the whole year or a hundred personalised invitations from one design (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"14\">Data tables</a> – <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"17\">Appearance from data</a>).</p><figure><img src=\"img/instrukcja/projekty.png\" alt=\"Your projects\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Your projects</figcaption></figure>"},{"id":"1.2","tytul":"How a project is built","html":"<p>One <b>project</b> is one publication. A project consists of:</p><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Sections</b></td><td>Parts of the publication with their own format, margins and numbering, e.g. “Cover” and “Body”.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Pages</b></td><td>The pages of each section, in print order. Elements sit on the pages.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Elements</b></td><td>Texts, shapes, lines, images, tables and groups.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Masters</b></td><td>Elements shared by many pages — they lie under the page content or above it.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Styles</b></td><td>Saved text looks.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Data tables</b></td><td>Sheets with data for fields in text and for repeating pages.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Images</b></td><td>All the project's photos and graphics, in one window.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The project saves itself, to your account (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"2.4\">Saving</a>).</p>"}]},
  {"nr":"2","tytul":"Projects","czesc":"Basics","ikona":"ksiazka","wstep":"A project is one publication: here you create, open, copy and save projects.","sekcje":[{"id":"2.1","tytul":"Project list","html":"<p>Clicking a card opens the project in the editor — on the page you last edited on this device. When you don't have any projects yet, the list offers <span class=\"ui\">Create project</span>, <span class=\"ui\">From a template</span> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"10.10\">Ready-made sections (document templates)</a>) and <span class=\"ui\">Import from file</span>.</p><ul class=\"punkty\"><li>The <b>cloud</b> next to the name tells you whether the changes are in your account: with a tick — saved, with a clock — waiting to be sent, crossed out — no internet (the changes are on the device and will be sent automatically).</li><li>The <b>Shared</b> badge — the project has a preview link (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"23\">Sharing via link</a>). Clicking the badge opens the link window.</li><li>Projects shared with you by others have a badge with the owner's name and the note “Can edit” or “Can view”. The <span class=\"ui\">All</span>, <span class=\"ui\">Mine</span> and <span class=\"ui\">Shared with me</span> filters appear when you have such projects.</li><li><span class=\"ui\">Invitations</span> with a number — invitations to other people's projects waiting for your answer (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"24.3\">Accepting an invitation</a>).</li></ul><figure><img src=\"img/instrukcja/projekty.png\" alt=\"Project list\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Project list</figcaption></figure>"},{"id":"2.2","tytul":"New project","html":"<p><span class=\"ui\">New project</span> asks for a name, a page format (A4, A5, A6, B5, square or <b>Custom</b> in mm), orientation and:</p><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What it sets</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Bleed</b></td><td>A strip beyond the page edge that the print shop trims off — usually 3 mm (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"12.1\">Lines on the page</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Margins</b></td><td>The safe area for texts, in mm.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Page layout</b></td><td><b>Single pages</b> or <b>Spreads (left + right)</b>, like in a book.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>You can change all of this later in the section settings (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"10.4\">Section settings</a>).</p><p><b>Start from</b>: <b>Blank page</b> or <b>Template</b>. <b>Template</b> shows, right in this window, templates of <b>whole projects</b> — sets of sections with settings, ready for print and for the 3D mock-up:</p><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Template</th><th>Sections</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Hardcover book</b></td><td>hard cover, front endpaper, book block, back endpaper</td></tr><tr><td><b>Hardcover book with dust jacket</b></td><td>as above plus a dust jacket with flaps</td></tr><tr><td><b>Paperback book</b></td><td>soft glued cover, book block</td></tr><tr><td><b>Saddle-stitched booklet</b></td><td>saddle-stitched cover, body (multiple of 4)</td></tr><tr><td><b>Hardcover weekly planner</b>, <b>Paperback daily planner</b></td><td>cover, (endpapers), calendar in the book block</td></tr><tr><td><b>Hardcover photo album</b></td><td>hard cover, endpapers, spreads for photos</td></tr><tr><td><b>Wall calendar with backing board</b></td><td>12 months with a cover, backing (cardboard)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Click a template: you'll see the first pages of each section and choose the <b>Format</b>, <b>Block pages</b> and <b>Page multiple (signature)</b> — 16 pages by default; the number of block pages is always a multiple of it. <b>Colors</b> — brand or palette. <span class=\"ui\">Create a project with this template</span> creates the whole project. The format and layout fields are hidden then — the template provides them.</p><p><b>Section</b> templates (calendar, planner, invitation, chapter…) are something else: you insert them into an existing project in the editor (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"10.10\">Ready-made sections (document templates)</a>).</p><figure><img src=\"img/instrukcja/nowy-projekt.png\" alt=\"New project\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>New project</figcaption></figure>"},{"id":"2.3","tytul":"Project menu","html":"<p><span class=\"ui\">⋯</span> on the project card: <span class=\"ui\">Preview</span> (straight away, without the editor, <span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"19\">Preview</a>), <span class=\"ui\">Rename</span>, <span class=\"ui\">Duplicate</span> (e.g. a calendar for the next year), <span class=\"ui\">Export to file (.makietka)</span> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"2.6\">Project file</a>), <span class=\"ui\">Collaboration…</span> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"24\">Collaboration</a>) and <span class=\"ui\">Delete project</span>. In someone else's project, instead of duplicating and deleting you get <span class=\"ui\">Make my own copy</span> and <span class=\"ui\">Leave project</span> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"24.6\">Leaving a project and making a copy</a>).</p><figure><img src=\"img/instrukcja/menu.png\" alt=\"Project menu\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Project menu</figcaption></figure>"},{"id":"2.4","tytul":"Saving","html":"<p>There's no “Save” button — every change saves itself, first on the device and a moment later to your account. Without internet, changes wait on the device and are sent automatically when the connection is back. The save status is shown by the cloud next to the project name (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"2.1\">Project list</a>). A change made right before closing the tab is kept too: it's saved on the device and reaches your account the next time you open the project.</p>"},{"id":"2.5","tytul":"Several devices and undo","html":"<p>You can have the same project open on several devices or in several tabs. A change made on one appears on the other after a moment, without refreshing.</p><p>A new version of Makietka switches on by itself, right away, when there's internet (in an open tab within about 2 minutes) – also in an open project: your changes are saved first, and then the project reopens in the same place (with the undo history). While you're typing text, it waits until you finish (at most 20 seconds). Without internet, the version saved on the device works. The version number is shown in <b>Settings</b>, below the list of sections.</p><p>When a project was changed in two places at once, the app <b>merges the changes element by element</b>: if you move a photo on one device and fix a heading on the other, both changes are kept. It only asks when the same element was changed in both places: the “The same thing was changed on two devices” window shows each such element with a choice of <b>From this device</b> or <b>From the account</b> (the account version is selected). <span class=\"ui\">Use the account version everywhere</span> picks it for all of them, <span class=\"ui\">Apply</span> confirms.</p><p><span class=\"ui\">Undo</span> (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd>) and <span class=\"ui\">Redo</span> (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd>) cover the whole project: elements, pages, sections, masters, styles, data and series. The history goes back 80 steps and stays after you close the project — on the same device. Undo only reverses changes made on this device; changes by other people and AI stay.</p>"},{"id":"2.6","tytul":"Project file","html":"<ul class=\"punkty\"><li><span class=\"ui\">Export to file (.makietka)</span> (<span class=\"ui\">⋯</span> menu on the card) saves the whole project, with images and fonts, to a <code>.makietka</code> file — it's a backup or a way to hand the project over.</li><li><span class=\"ui\">Import from file</span> next to <span class=\"ui\">New project</span> loads a project from a <code>.makietka</code> file (on a narrow screen it's just an arrow icon). The same way you can open a project from <b>InDesign</b> (<code>.idml</code> – in InDesign: File → Save As → InDesign Markup) and from <b>Scribus</b> (<code>.sla</code>): you get a new project with pages (a section for each page format, bleed and margins from the file), text frames with formatting (linked frames still flow text), shapes in color (CMYK converted to RGB) and images stored in the file. Images that are only linked in InDesign or Scribus can be loaded together with the project in a ZIP file (in InDesign: File → Package; in Scribus: File → Collect for Output) – without them, grey boxes with the file name stand in their place. Data merge fields from InDesign and Affinity (<code>&lt;&lt;imie&gt;&gt;</code>) and from Word and Publisher (<code>«imie»</code>) become Makietka fields (<code>{imie}</code>) – all that's left is to load the data (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"14\">Data tables</a>). This also works with <b>Microsoft Publisher</b> files (<code>.pub</code>, versions 2002–2019 – Microsoft retired Publisher in October 2026): pages in the file's format, text frames in their places with font, size, bold, italic, underline and alignment, linked frames as flowing text, master page elements under the content, photos (JPEG, PNG, GIF), colored frame backgrounds and simple shapes. Images in the old Windows formats (WMF, EMF) can't be displayed by the browser – grey boxes stand in their place. Older files (Publisher 98/2000) must first be saved in a newer version. After loading, the <b>“What came over from…”</b> window tells you what was simplified: a gradient replaced with a single color, shadow and other effects skipped, a rotated image inserted without rotation, an IDML table as text with tabs, text on a path and a Scribus table as a grey box, missing images, paragraph styles as plain formatting. <span class=\"ui\">Show</span> opens the page with the first such element. Simplified elements are marked and appear in the <b>Preflight check</b> as “From import: …” – the <span class=\"ui\">Checked</span> button removes the mark once the element is fixed. Formatting of parts of text (e.g. a bold <code>&lt;&lt;imie&gt;&gt;</code> field) is kept for merge fields too.</li><li>The app installed on a computer opens <code>.makietka</code> files with a double-click (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"28.4\">Installing the app</a>).</li></ul><p><b>Text from and to other programs.</b> In the editor, the <b>⋯</b> menu:</p><ul class=\"punkty\"><li><span class=\"ui\">Insert text from file (Word, ODT, Markdown)…</span> — a <code>.docx</code> (Word), <code>.odt</code> (LibreOffice, OpenOffice), <code>.md</code> (Markdown) or <code>.txt</code> file goes into one frame on the current page, within the margins, with <b>Add pages</b> turned on: the text spreads itself over as many pages as needed. Headings stay headings (larger, bold), chapters (heading 1) start on a new page, and bold, italic and underlined parts of text are kept. Tables come in as rows with tabs; images and footnotes from the file have to be inserted separately — the app will tell you how many there were.</li><li><span class=\"ui\">Save text as Word (.docx)</span> and <span class=\"ui\">Save as e-book (EPUB)</span> — just the page content in reading order (without the cover), with headings and formatting of parts of text; the e-book has a table of contents and each chapter in a separate file. The page layout and images stay in Makietka.</li><li><span class=\"ui\">Save for Scribus (.sla)</span> — the whole project with its page layout for the free layout program Scribus (1.5 and 1.6): pages in print order, with bleed and margins, series expanded and data filled in. Texts are ordinary Scribus text frames (font, size, color, bold, italic, underline, alignment, paragraphs) — you can keep editing them; rectangles, ellipses, polygons, lines and paths are shapes with colors; photos, tables, calendars, groups and elements with a shadow or transparency come in as images (300 dpi) stored in the file itself. Fonts must be installed on the system — the app tells you which ones the file uses. Die-cut lines don't transfer (they're in the file for the cutting plotter).</li><li><span class=\"ui\">Save for InDesign (.idml)</span> — the same in IDML format, which opens in Adobe InDesign (CS4 and newer), as well as Affinity Publisher and QuarkXPress: text frames with formatting, shapes with colors, the rest as images embedded in the file. Each page is a separate document page (not joined into spreads); margins and bleed are kept.</li><li><span class=\"ui\">Save for Word with page layout (.docx)</span> — project pages as Word document pages: text frames (text boxes) with font, color, alignment and line spacing, shapes and images in their places, page background; tables, calendars and elements with effects as images. LibreOffice (Writer) opens such a file too.</li><li><span class=\"ui\">Save pages as SVG (Inkscape, Illustrator)</span> — each page as an SVG file in millimetres (ZIP) – for Inkscape, Illustrator, Corel, Affinity or LibreOffice Draw.</li><li><b>What transfers and what doesn't.</b> Before downloading the file for Scribus, InDesign and Word, the <b>“What will transfer to…”</b> window lists what goes over unchanged, what <b>turns into an image</b> (looks the same but can't be edited: tables, calendars, charts, codes, groups, elements with a shadow, transparency, gradient, dashed line, text on an arc), what gets <b>simplified</b> (series expanded into finished pages, masters merged into the pages, styles as plain formatting, linked frames split, Pantone as RGB), what we <b>can't transfer</b> (links, footnotes, drop caps, die-cut lines) and what you need to <b>do in the program</b> (install fonts, turn on hyphenation). Each item lists page numbers, and <span class=\"ui\">Show</span> closes the window without downloading and selects the first such element – you can fix it and save again. <span class=\"ui\">Download</span> saves the file.</li><li><span class=\"ui\">Series for another program (template + data)…</span> — mail merge outside Makietka. The project pages appear once (without repeating), with fields in place of the data, and alongside them the data from the series table. You can choose: - <b>InDesign / Affinity Publisher</b> – an IDML template with <code>&lt;&lt;imie&gt;&gt;</code> tags, <code>dane.csv</code> and an <code>obrazy/</code> folder (the image column has a name with <code>@</code>, e.g. <code>@zdjecie</code>, and paths to the files – that's how “Data Merge” reads them); - <b>Scribus</b> – an SLA template with <code>%VAR_imie%</code> variables for the ScribusGenerator plugin and <code>dane.csv</code>; - <b>Word / Publisher</b> – text in DOCX with real merge fields and <code>dane.xlsx</code> (Mailings → Select Recipients → Use an Existing List). Document fields (<code>{page}</code>, <code>{section}</code>…) are already calculated. The <code>CZYTAJ-MNIE.txt</code> file in the package explains step by step what to do next, and at the end it has the same description of “what transferred and how”. The other way round: text from Word with merge fields (Insert text from file) comes in with <code>{imie}</code> fields.</li></ul>"}]},
  {"nr":"3","tytul":"The editor screen","czesc":"Editor","ikona":"olowek","wstep":"Where everything is in the editor — bars, panels and menus.","sekcje":[{"id":"3.1","tytul":"Screen layout","html":"<p>A project opens in the editor. The screen has five parts:</p><ul class=\"punkty\"><li><b>top bar</b> — navigation, undo, project windows and the <span class=\"ui\">⋯</span> menu (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"3.2\">Top bar</a>, <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"3.3\">The ⋯ menu</a>),</li><li><b>pages panel</b> on the left — page thumbnails grouped into sections (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"10.1\">Page panel</a>),</li><li><b>the page</b> in the middle, with the insert bar above it (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"3.4\">Insert bar</a>),</li><li><b>right column</b> — the Properties and Layers tabs (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"3.5\">Properties and layers</a>, <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"9\">Layers</a>),</li><li><b>bottom-right corner</b> — guides, warnings, the view menu and zoom (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"3.6\">Bottom-right corner and the view menu</a>).</li></ul><p>Hover the mouse over a button to see its name and keyboard shortcut.</p><figure><img src=\"img/instrukcja/edytor.png\" alt=\"Editor\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Editor</figcaption></figure>"},{"id":"3.2","tytul":"Top bar","html":"<figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-pasek-gorny.png\" alt=\"Top bar\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Top bar</figcaption></figure><p>From the left: <span class=\"ui\">Projects</span>, the <b>project name</b> (click it to rename) with the save status (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"2.4\">Saving</a>), <span class=\"ui\">Undo</span> and <span class=\"ui\">Redo</span> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"2.5\">Several devices and undo</a>), <span class=\"ui\">Masters</span> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"11\">Master pages</a>), <span class=\"ui\">Data</span> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"14\">Data tables</a>), <span class=\"ui\">Preview</span> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"19\">Preview</a>), the section <b>format</b>, e.g. “A5” — opens the section settings (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"10.4\">Section settings</a>), <span class=\"ui\">Comments</span> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"24.7\">Comments</a>), the magnifier <span class=\"ui\">Find and replace</span> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"5.15\">Find and replace</a>) and <span class=\"ui\">⋯</span> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"3.3\">The ⋯ menu</a>).</p><p>Labels next to the icons appear when there is room for them — you can change this in the view menu: <b>Top bar labels</b> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"3.6\">Bottom-right corner and the view menu</a>). On a narrow phone, <span class=\"ui\">Masters</span>, <span class=\"ui\">Data</span>, <span class=\"ui\">Comments</span> and the magnifier are in the <span class=\"ui\">⋯</span> menu.</p>"},{"id":"3.3","tytul":"The ⋯ menu","html":"<figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-menu-wiecej.png\" alt=\"The ⋯ menu\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>The ⋯ menu</figcaption></figure><p>The items are grouped:</p><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Items</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Search and notes</td><td><span class=\"ui\">Find and replace…</span> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"5.15\">Find and replace</a>), <span class=\"ui\">Comments</span> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"24.7\">Comments</a>).</td></tr><tr><td>Project resources</td><td><span class=\"ui\">Project images…</span> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"7.5\">The Project images window</a>), <span class=\"ui\">Project fonts…</span> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"5.8\">Fonts</a>), <span class=\"ui\">Project colors…</span> — project and section colors, extra colors, finishes (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"22.1\">Spot colors</a>, <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"22.5\">Project colors and color printing — for the whole project or for a section</a>); on a phone also <span class=\"ui\">Master pages…</span> and <span class=\"ui\">Data…</span>.</td></tr><tr><td>Format and brand</td><td><span class=\"ui\">Change project format…</span> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"10.9\">Changing the format with scaling</a>), <span class=\"ui\">Apply brand to project…</span> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"27.9\">Brand kit</a>).</td></tr><tr><td>Print and export</td><td><span class=\"ui\">Preflight check</span> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"20\">Preflight check</a>), <span class=\"ui\">Export to PDF…</span> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"21\">Export to PDF and PNG</a>), <span class=\"ui\">Print at home…</span> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"21.8\">Print at home</a>), <span class=\"ui\">Download current page as PNG</span> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"21.5\">Page as PNG</a>).</td></tr><tr><td>Other</td><td><span class=\"ui\">Collaboration…</span> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"24\">Collaboration</a>) and the theme switch (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"27.4\">Theme</a>).</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},{"id":"3.4","tytul":"Insert bar","html":"<p>The bar above the page inserts new elements (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"4.1\">Inserting</a>).</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-wstaw.png\" alt=\"Insert bar\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Insert bar</figcaption></figure><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Button</th><th>Key</th><th>What it inserts</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span class=\"ui\">Text</span></td><td><kbd>T</kbd></td><td>A text frame (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"5\">Text</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ui\">Shapes ▾</span></td><td><kbd>R</kbd> <kbd>E</kbd> <kbd>P</kbd> <kbd>L</kbd> <kbd>B</kbd> <kbd>N</kbd></td><td>A menu: <b>Rectangle</b>, <b>Ellipse</b> (or a circle), <b>Polygon or star</b> (you pick the shape first), <b>Line</b> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"6.1\">Rectangle, ellipse and polygon</a>, <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"6.2\">Line</a>), <b>Freehand drawing</b> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"6.3\">Freehand drawing</a>) and <b>Pen</b> – a shape made of points and curves (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"6.4\">Pen and point editing</a>). While drawing or the pen is active, the button shows its icon – clicking it ends the tool.</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ui\">Image</span></td><td>—</td><td>A photo or a graphic (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"7.1\">Inserting an image</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ui\">AI ▾</span></td><td>—</td><td>The AI menu: an image from a description, fixing the picture in a frame, improving and translating text, a data column (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"5.17\">AI text: fixing and translating</a>, <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"7.11\">AI image</a>, <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"14.6\">Fill a column with AI</a>); in a comic also the script and the panels (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"10.13\">Comic</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ui\">Table</span></td><td>—</td><td>A table with rows and columns (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"8\">Table</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ui\">Library</span></td><td>—</td><td>Ready-made elements: shapes, frames, ornaments, planners, calendars, charts and more (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"4.8\">Library</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ui\">Comment</span></td><td>—</td><td>A comment pinned to a spot on the page or to the selected element (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"24.7\">Comments</a>).</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>You can turn on labels under the icons in the view menu: <b>Tool labels</b> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"3.6\">Bottom-right corner and the view menu</a>).</p><p>The shape keyboard shortcuts work without opening the menu. A chat with the AI opens with a separate <span class=\"ui\">AI assistant</span> button on the top bar, next to <span class=\"ui\">Preview</span> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"25.6\">AI Assistant in Makietka (own key)</a>).</p><p><b>On a phone</b> <span class=\"ui\">Comment</span> and <span class=\"ui\">Copy style</span> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"4.13\">Copy style (format painter)</a>) are under <span class=\"ui\">⋯</span> at the end of the bar, and <span class=\"ui\">AI assistant</span> – in the <span class=\"ui\">AI ▾</span> menu. While one of the tools under <span class=\"ui\">⋯</span> is active, <span class=\"ui\">⋯</span> shows its icon; tap it to end the tool.</p><figure class=\"kadr telefon\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-wstaw-telefon.png\" alt=\"The insert bar ⋯ menu on a phone\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>The insert bar ⋯ menu on a phone</figcaption></figure>"},{"id":"3.5","tytul":"Properties and layers","html":"<p>The right column has two tabs at the top: <span class=\"ui\">Properties</span> and <span class=\"ui\">Layers</span> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"9\">Layers</a>); switch between them by clicking or with <kbd>F7</kbd>. The properties header tells you what the settings apply to:</p><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Header</th><th>When</th><th>What you set</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Selected element</b> · type (e.g. “Text”)</td><td>An element is selected.</td><td>The look and position of that element.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Tool settings</b> · name (e.g. “Drawing”)</td><td>A tool is on, e.g. freehand drawing. The header then has an accent-colored background.</td><td>How the next elements will look — “This is how the next lines will look.”</td></tr><tr><td><b>Nothing is selected</b> · “Page”</td><td>Nothing is selected.</td><td>Settings of the open page (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"10.3\">Page properties</a>).</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>With an element selected, the header has <span class=\"ui\">Copy to clipboard</span>, <span class=\"ui\">Duplicate</span>, <span class=\"ui\">Copy style</span> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"4.13\">Copy style (format painter)</a>), <span class=\"ui\">Save to library</span> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"4.8\">Library</a>) and <span class=\"ui\">Delete</span>. <b>Warnings</b> open from the triangle button next to the zoom (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"12.2\">Warnings</a>).</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-rysowanie.png\" alt=\"Tool settings: drawing\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Tool settings: drawing</figcaption></figure><p>On a phone, these panels open from the buttons at the bottom of the screen (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"28.2\">Editor on a phone</a>).</p><figure><img src=\"img/instrukcja/wlasciwosci.png\" alt=\"Properties of the selected text\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Properties of the selected text</figcaption></figure>"},{"id":"3.6","tytul":"Bottom-right corner and the view menu","html":"<figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-widok.png\" alt=\"Bottom-right corner\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Bottom-right corner</figcaption></figure><ul class=\"punkty\"><li><b>two sheets</b> — only in a section with spreads: the whole spread or a single page (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"10.5\">Spreads</a>),</li><li>the <b>guides</b> button (<kbd>W</kbd>) — shows or hides margins, bleed, spine and guides (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"12.1\">Lines on the page</a>),</li><li>the <b>Warnings</b> button (triangle, <kbd>F8</kbd>) — the number of issues on the page; opens the warnings window (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"12.2\">Warnings</a>),</li><li><span class=\"ui\">View</span> (grid icon) — the view menu,</li><li><span class=\"ui\">−</span>, percentage, <span class=\"ui\">+</span> — zoom (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"12.6\">Zoom</a>).</li></ul><p>The view menu:</p><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Grid</b>, <b>Snap to grid</b>, <b>Grid step</b></td><td>A grid at the given step in mm (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"12.3\">Grid</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Baseline grid</b></td><td>Baselines from the section settings (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"5.13\">Baseline grid</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Snap to elements and show distances</b></td><td>Pink lines and distances in mm while moving (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"12.5\">Snapping and distances</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Rulers</b></td><td>Rulers in mm above the page and on the left (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"12.4\">Rulers and guides</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Lock guides</b>, <span class=\"ui\">Remove guides from this page</span></td><td>Protecting and clearing guides (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"12.4\">Rulers and guides</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Show one page in spreads</b></td><td>Same as the two-sheets button (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"10.5\">Spreads</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Check spelling</b></td><td>A wavy red line under words not in the dictionary and the <b>Spelling</b> part in the text panel (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"5.16\">Spell check</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Show print finishes</b></td><td>The look of varnish, foil, embossing and white on the page; off — the normal view (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"22.3\">Print finish view</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Tool labels</b></td><td>Labels under the insert bar icons.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Top bar labels</b></td><td><span class=\"ui\">Auto</span> — when they fit, <span class=\"ui\">Always</span>, <span class=\"ui\">Never</span>.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>View settings apply only to this device.</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-menu-widoku.png\" alt=\"View menu\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>View menu</figcaption></figure>"}]},
  {"nr":"4","tytul":"Elements on the page","czesc":"Editor","ikona":"olowek","wstep":"Everything on a page is an element: here is what all of them have in common — selecting, moving, groups, ready-made elements from the Library.","sekcje":[{"id":"4.1","tytul":"Inserting","html":"<p>Click a tool on the insert bar (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"3.4\">Insert bar</a>) — the element appears in the middle of the view and is selected. You can also drag an image from your disk onto the page or paste it from the clipboard (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>V</kbd>).</p><p>Master elements (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"11\">Master pages</a>) are visible on the page but can't be selected here — you change them in the master.</p><figure><img src=\"img/instrukcja/wstawianie.png\" alt=\"Inserted text\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Inserted text</figcaption></figure>"},{"id":"4.2","tytul":"Selecting","html":"<ul class=\"punkty\"><li><b>A click</b> selects an element.</li><li><kbd>Shift</kbd> + click adds an element to the selection or removes it.</li><li><b>Dragging a box</b> from an empty spot on the page selects the elements it touches.</li><li><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>A</kbd> selects all elements on the page.</li><li>To select an element lying under another one, use the layers list (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"9\">Layers</a>).</li><li><b>Double-click</b>: in text — typing, in a group — entering the group, in a table cell — typing in the cell, in a pen shape — editing points, in an image — cropping.</li><li><kbd>Esc</kbd> clears the selection.</li></ul>"},{"id":"4.3","tytul":"Moving, size and rotation","html":"<ul class=\"punkty\"><li><b>Drag</b> an element to move it. Arrow keys move it by 1 mm, with <kbd>Shift</kbd> — by 10 mm.</li><li><b>The circles at the corners and sides</b> change the size, <b>the circle above the element</b> rotates it.</li><li><kbd>Shift</kbd> while dragging — only horizontally or vertically; while rotating — in 15° steps.</li><li><kbd>Alt</kbd> while dragging — you drag a copy.</li></ul><p>You can type exact values in the properties, in the <b>Position and size (mm)</b> part:</p><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>X</b>, <b>Y</b></td><td>The top-left corner of the element, in mm from the top-left corner of the page (without bleed).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Width</b>, <b>Height</b></td><td>Size in mm. The padlock between them keeps the proportions.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Rotation</b></td><td>Angle in degrees.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>With several elements selected, the numbers refer to their shared bounding box.</p>"},{"id":"4.4","tytul":"Aligning and even spacing","html":"<p>The <b>Align</b> part in the properties:</p><ul class=\"punkty\"><li><b>one element</b> — aligns to the page margins: left, center, right, top, vertical center, bottom,</li><li><b>several elements</b> — aligns them to each other,</li><li><b>three or more</b> — additionally <span class=\"ui\">Distribute horizontally</span> and <span class=\"ui\">Distribute vertically</span>: equal spacing between elements.</li></ul><p>While moving, an element “snaps” to the edges and centers of other elements, and arrows show distances in mm (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"12.5\">Snapping and distances</a>).</p><figure><img src=\"img/instrukcja/wyrownanie.png\" alt=\"Aligning several elements\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Aligning several elements</figcaption></figure>"},{"id":"4.5","tytul":"Groups","html":"<p>Select several elements and choose <span class=\"ui\">Group</span> (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>G</kbd>). A group moves, scales and rotates as one element. <span class=\"ui\">Ungroup</span> (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>G</kbd>) splits it back apart.</p><p><b>An element of a group without ungrouping:</b> double-click the group — it opens (orange dashed frame) and the element under the cursor is selected. You can move it, change it in the properties or type in it. Clicking another element of the group selects that one, <kbd>Shift</kbd> adds more. <kbd>Esc</kbd>, a click outside the group or <span class=\"ui\">Back to the whole group</span> closes it.</p><ul class=\"punkty\"><li>In an open group, <span class=\"ui\">Duplicate</span> leaves the copy in the group, and <span class=\"ui\">Delete</span> deletes only the selected element.</li><li>A group inside a group opens with another double-click; <kbd>Esc</kbd> goes back up one level.</li></ul>"},{"id":"4.6","tytul":"Order, locking and the clipboard","html":"<ul class=\"punkty\"><li>The <b>Order</b> part: <span class=\"ui\">Bring to front</span>, <span class=\"ui\">Higher</span>, <span class=\"ui\">Lower</span>, <span class=\"ui\">Send to back</span>. You can do the same by dragging in the layers list (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"9\">Layers</a>).</li><li>The <b>Other</b> part: - <b>Opacity</b> — the element's transparency in %, - <b>Lock position and size</b> — you won't move the element by accident; you can still change color and text, - <b>Mute warnings for this object</b> — e.g. for a background that deliberately extends into the bleed, - <b>Name</b> — the element's name in the layers list.</li><li><span class=\"ui\">Copy to clipboard</span> and <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>V</kbd> on another page paste the element in the same spot. The page properties also have <span class=\"ui\">Paste copied elements</span>.</li><li><b>Between projects</b>: the copied element waits in this device's clipboard — open another project and paste (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>V</kbd> or <span class=\"ui\">Paste copied elements</span>). The element's images go to the images of the new project. The clipboard survives switching to another project, reloading and a second tab.</li><li><b>Pages and sections</b> to another project: page menu (⋯ on the thumbnail) → <span class=\"ui\">Copy to clipboard (also to another project)</span>, section menu → <span class=\"ui\">Copy section to clipboard (also to another project)</span>. In the other project: page menu → <span class=\"ui\">Paste pages from clipboard</span> (after this page, in its section) or section menu → <span class=\"ui\">Paste section from clipboard</span> (after this section). Together with the pages come their images, masters, series with data tables and text styles. The menu item tells you what is on the clipboard and from which project. You can't paste a cover into a project that already has one — paste just the pages instead.</li></ul>"},{"id":"4.7","tytul":"Shadow","html":"<p>The <b>Shadow</b> part works for every element:</p><p><b>Drop shadow under the element</b> turns the shadow on. You can set its color, <b>Strength</b>, offset <b>Right</b> and <b>Down</b> in mm (negative — left or up) and the edge <b>Blur</b> in mm.</p>"},{"id":"4.8","tytul":"Library","html":"<p>The Library holds ready-made elements to insert: shapes, frames, ornaments, planners, calendars, charts and your own elements. Open it with <span class=\"ui\">Library</span> on the insert bar.</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-biblioteka.png\" alt=\"Library next to the page\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Library next to the page</figcaption></figure><ol class=\"kroki\"><li>Choose a category at the top or type a word into the <b>Search</b> field – search looks through all categories at once.</li><li>Click an element – it lands in the middle of the current page. On a computer you can also drag a tile onto the page: the element lands where you drop it.</li><li>Set the rest in the properties panel, as with any element.</li></ol><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>What's in it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>My elements</b></td><td>Elements you saved – in your account, in every project (see below).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Ready-made sections</b></td><td>Whole documents: calendars, planners, notebooks, invitations, diplomas, business cards, flyers, menus, book chapters… A tile opens the ready-made sections window with that template (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"10.10\">Ready-made sections (document templates)</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Icons</b></td><td>260 line icons in one style, in groups: <b>Contact</b>, <b>Time and calendar</b>, <b>Weather</b>, <b>Food</b>, <b>Travel</b>, <b>School and learning</b>, <b>Faith</b> (cross, dove, fish, candle, praying hands, Bible, chalice, rosary…), <b>Health</b>, <b>Finance</b>, <b>Home</b>, <b>Nature</b>, <b>Technology</b>, <b>Arrows</b>. Change the color and line weight in the <b>Stroke</b> part; when you enlarge an icon, the line grows with it.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Illustrations</b></td><td>Line drawings to recolor: mountains, forest, sailboat, lighthouse, house with a tree, church on a hill, coffee and a book, candle with a book, monstera, twig, cactus, birds, butterfly, cat, sheaf of grain, grapevine, dove with a twig, hands with a heart, cross on a hill, moon, city, bicycle, gift.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Shapes</b></td><td>Over 200 shapes in groups: <b>Basic</b> (circle, square, ovals, triangles, arcs, ring, cube, cylinder…), <b>Polygons</b> (also rounded, honeycomb), <b>Stars and badges</b> (stars, seals, medal, gear, promo badges), <b>Hearts and nature</b> (hearts, flowers, leaves, clouds, mountains, wave, flame, rainbow, animals…), <b>Symbols and icons</b> (house, phone, crown, trophy, padlock, person, laptop, key, puzzle piece, map…), <b>Occasions</b> (gift, balloon, Christmas tree, bauble, cake, Easter egg, snowman…), <b>Characters</b> (plus, minus, percent, question mark, check mark, checkboxes…).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Arrows and speech bubbles</b></td><td>Arrows in every direction, diagonal, curved, elbowed, arched, wavy, swap, branching; chevrons; speech bubbles from every side, round, oval, thought, cloud, shout, two bubbles.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Frames</b></td><td>Over 60 frames in groups: <b>Simple</b>, <b>Rounded</b>, <b>Double and dashed</b> (dashed, dotted, dash-dot – stroke only), <b>Decorative</b>, <b>With room for a title</b>, <b>Shaped</b> (heart, oval, circle, cloud, octagon, arch, star…), <b>For photos</b> (fields shaped like a circle, heart, arch, cloud… – insert an image into them, plus a print, a slide, a film frame), <b>Papers and notes</b>, <b>Page borders</b> (inserted over the whole page right away).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Ornaments</b></td><td>Ribbons and banners, corners (single and sets of four), rosettes and ornaments (mandala, compass rose, fleur-de-lis, medallion…).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Lines and dividers</b></td><td>Solid, thin, thick, dashed, dotted, dash-dot, double and vertical lines; decorative dividers (wave, zigzag, with a dot, circle, star, flower, diamonds, swirls, loops, leaves…). You can change the type and weight later in the <b>Line</b> part (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"6.2\">Line</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Labels and badges</b></td><td>Labels: rounded, with cut corners, oval, outlined, wavy, scalloped, jar label, heraldic, price tag, tag, badge, stamp, postage stamp, plaque — put text in the middle.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Occasions</b></td><td>Matching sets for <b>Christmas</b>, <b>Easter</b>, <b>birthday</b>, <b>wedding</b>, <b>baptism</b>, <b>First Communion</b> and <b>school</b>: a greeting header, a frame, a border and a ready card.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Backgrounds and patterns</b></td><td><b>Full-page patterns</b> (stripes, dots, polka dots, grid, plaid, herringbone, waves, zigzag, diamonds, stars, flowers, honeycomb, scales, triangles, crosses, circles, comic halftone, speed lines), <b>Paper textures</b> (linen, cardboard, kraft paper, handmade, lined with a margin, graph paper), <b>Gradients</b> and <b>Blobs and watercolor</b>. Patterns, textures and gradients are inserted over the whole page including the bleed, beneath the content; a pattern is one vector element on the background – change its colors after opening the group (double-click). With a shape selected, a pattern can fill it (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"6.5\">Fill</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Calendars</b></td><td>A month calendar: plain, with room for notes, with week numbers (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"4.9\">Month calendar</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Planner</b></td><td>Ready tables for planners and notebooks: to-do list, habit tracker, weekly plan, daily plan (hours), lined notes, weekday grid, shopping list, goals, budget, contacts, school timetable; <b>Plans</b>: yearly calendar (12 months on one page), monthly plan, week in columns and with priorities, meal plan; <b>Trackers</b>: mood, water, reading, prayer journal; <b>Lists and budget</b>: wish list, monthly budget; plus note grids: squares, dots, lines (every 5 mm), graph paper, isometric grid. Checkboxes are in <b>Shapes → Characters</b>.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Printable forms</b></td><td>Date and signature, letter boxes, data fields, a tear-off strip with scissors, checkboxes, a 1–5 rating scale, a remarks field, a coupon, consent with signature; an order table and an attendance list (ordinary tables).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Charts</b></td><td>Bar, horizontal bar, line, pie and donut charts from a data table (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"4.10\">Chart</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Infographics</b></td><td>Horizontal and vertical timeline, process steps and arrows, numbers and statistics, comparison, tree, pyramid, cycle, progress bars, star rating, funnel, mind map, 2 × 2 matrix.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Photo collages</b></td><td>Ready layouts with 2–9 photo fields (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"4.11\">Photo collage</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Texts</b></td><td>Ready text on an arc, in a circle (seal), a wavy banner, a flag, a bulging title (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"5.14\">Text on an arc, circle and path; warping</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Headings and titles</b></td><td>Title with subtitle, title between lines, chapter number, drop cap with a paragraph, quote with author, stamps, ribbon with text, title on a bar, handwritten, in two typefaces, with a label, in corners, page header, title with a date.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Content blocks</b></td><td><b>Note</b>, <b>Tip</b>, <b>Fun fact</b> and <b>Important</b> boxes, pull quote, verse, list with icons and numbered list, sidebar, masthead, author note (with a photo field), contact, question and answer, “Remember”.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Page layouts</b></td><td>Whole pages for the content area: title page, chapter opening, 2 × 2 gallery, quote page, notes page, diploma, announcement, day page.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Text styles</b></td><td>The project's paragraph styles: a click applies the style to the selected text, and with nothing selected it inserts new text in that style (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"5.4\">Styles</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Brand</b></td><td>The logo from the brand kit, <span class=\"ui\">Apply brand to project…</span> and <span class=\"ui\">Edit brand kit…</span> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"27.9\">Brand kit</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Codes</b></td><td>A QR code and an EAN / ISBN barcode (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"4.12\">QR code and barcode</a>).</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Icons, illustrations, headings, blocks, infographics, forms, layouts and occasion sets are ordinary texts, shapes and lines combined into one group – you move them together, and after a double-click (open group, <span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"4.5\">Groups</a>) you change each text, color or shape separately. They take colors and fonts from the brand kit (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"27.9\">Brand kit</a>) if you have one; the whole insert is a single undo step. On a computer, the categories take up at most three rows – scroll that area to see the rest.</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-biblioteka-dzialy.png\" alt=\"A heading, a “Tip” block, a timeline and a verse from the Library\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>A heading, a “Tip” block, a timeline and a verse from the Library</figcaption></figure><p>A shape from the library is an ordinary shape: you can change its color, stroke and points (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"6.4\">Pen and point editing</a>), and a closed shape can be a photo frame (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"7.7\">Image in a shape</a>). Long categories have group headings (e.g. Basic, Polygons); when searching there are no headings, and results come from all categories. A template from the <b>Planner</b> category is an ordinary table (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"8\">Table</a>): you type into the cells, add rows and columns, change lines and colors. To repeat a planner on many pages, insert it in a master (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"11\">Master pages</a>) or on a series page (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"16\">Series</a>).</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-biblioteka-planer.png\" alt=\"The Planner category in the Library\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>The Planner category in the Library</figcaption></figure><p><b>My elements.</b> Save a logo, a company footer or a favorite frame once and insert it in every project:</p><ol class=\"kroki\"><li>Select an element or several elements (e.g. a logo with a caption).</li><li>Click <span class=\"ui\">Save to library</span> in the panel header (with several selected — in the <b>Selection</b> part) or in <span class=\"ui\">⋯</span> next to the element in the layers list.</li><li>Type a name. The element goes to the <b>My elements</b> category.</li></ol><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-moje-elementy.png\" alt=\"My elements in the Library\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>My elements in the Library</figcaption></figure><ul class=\"punkty\"><li>Elements are stored in your account, so you see them in every project and on every device.</li><li>An inserted element is a copy — removing it from the Library doesn't change the pages where it already is.</li><li>The pencil in the tile's corner renames it, the bin removes the element from the Library.</li><li>You can't save master elements or text you're currently typing in — finish typing first.</li></ul>"},{"id":"4.9","tytul":"Month calendar","html":"<p>A grid of days for one month with the month name and weekdays. Sundays and Polish holidays – including movable ones: Easter, Easter Monday, Pentecost, Corpus Christi – are highlighted in color.</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-kalendarz.png\" alt=\"A month calendar and its settings\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>A month calendar and its settings</figcaption></figure><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>What it changes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Month</b>, <b>Year</b></td><td>The month shown.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Month from data column</b></td><td>A different month on each series page – from a column with values like “2027-03”, “marzec 2027” or “3” (the year then comes from <b>Year from column</b>). This is how you make a 12-month calendar from one template page (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"16\">Series</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Week starts on</b></td><td>Monday or Sunday.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Day names</b></td><td>Full, short or just letters.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Days of other months</b></td><td>Grayed out, like ordinary days, or hidden.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Month name above the days</b>, <b>Week numbers</b></td><td>An extra row and column.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Space for notes</b></td><td>Date in the corner, taller cells to write in.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Polish holidays</b>, <b>Holiday name under the date</b></td><td>Highlighting holidays and their names in the cells.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Holidays from the data table</b></td><td>Your own days (birthdays, name days, deadlines) from a table: a column with the date (“2027-05-26”, “26.05.2027” or “26.05” – every year) and a column with the name.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Calendar colors</b></td><td>Days, names, Sundays, Saturdays, holidays, days of other months.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>You change lines, row backgrounds, the font and alignment as in a table (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"8.5\">Lines and backgrounds</a>). The app lays out the dates – you don't type in the calendar cells.</p>"},{"id":"4.10","tytul":"Chart","html":"<p>A chart draws numbers from a project data table (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"14\">Data tables</a>) and updates itself when you change the data.</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-wykres.png\" alt=\"A bar chart and its settings\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>A bar chart and its settings</figcaption></figure><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>What it changes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Type</b></td><td>Bar, horizontal bar, line, pie, donut.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Data table</b>, <b>Labels from column</b>, <b>Numbers from columns</b></td><td>Where the chart takes its data from; each selected number column is a separate series (separate color).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Rows</b></td><td>All, selected (from–to) or <b>series page row</b> – on each repeated page, a chart from that page's row.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Title</b>, <b>Values on bars and slices</b>, <b>Axis</b>, <b>Grid lines</b>, <b>Legend</b></td><td>What is visible on the chart.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Width</b>, <b>Height</b>, <b>Font</b>, <b>Font size</b>, <b>Colors</b></td><td>The look. The handles change the chart's size, not the text size.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Numbers in cells can have a decimal comma, spaces and a % sign.</p>"},{"id":"4.11","tytul":"Photo collage","html":"<p>A collage is a ready layout of photo fields. Drop a photo from your computer onto a field – it goes into that field. You can also double-click the collage, choose a field and insert an image from the project in the <b>Image in shape</b> part (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"7.7\">Image in a shape</a>).</p><p>In the collage panel you can change the <b>Layout</b> (fields are added or removed, photos in the first fields stay), <b>Spacing between photos</b>, <b>Corner rounding</b>, <b>Width</b> and <b>Height</b>.</p>"},{"id":"4.12","tytul":"QR code and barcode","html":"<p>Insert a code from <span class=\"ui\">Library</span>, the <b>Codes</b> category. Set the rest in the <b>Code</b> part of the panel:</p><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Type</b></td><td><b>QR code</b> or <b>EAN-13 / ISBN barcode</b> (e.g. the ISBN on a book cover).</td></tr><tr><td><b>What it contains</b> (QR)</td><td><b>Website address</b>, <b>Text</b>, <b>Email</b>, <b>Phone</b> or <b>Wi-Fi network</b>.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Error correction</b> (QR)</td><td>How much of the code can be covered: from <b>Low (7%)</b> — the smallest code — to <b>Highest (30%)</b>, e.g. under a logo.</td></tr><tr><td><b>EAN or ISBN number</b></td><td>The check digit is added automatically; you can type an ISBN with hyphens.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Size</b></td><td><span class=\"ui\">Nominal size (100%)</span> sets the EAN code to 37.29 × 25.93 mm.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Code color</b>, <b>Background</b>, <b>Quiet zone</b></td><td>The code must be dark on a light background, with an empty band around it.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>A data field in the content — e.g. <code>{adres}</code> or <code>{isbn}</code> — gives a different code on each series page (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"15\">Fields in text</a>).</p><p>If the code is too small, too light or has wrong content, the panel and <b>Preflight check</b> will remind you.</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-kod.png\" alt=\"A QR code and its settings\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>A QR code and its settings</figcaption></figure>"},{"id":"4.13","tytul":"Copy style (format painter)","html":"<p>Transfer the look of one element to another with one click — without retyping colors and weights.</p><p><span class=\"ui\">Copy style</span> (brush) is in the properties panel header, in <span class=\"ui\">⋯</span> next to the element in the layers list, and on a phone also under <span class=\"ui\">⋯</span> on the insert bar (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"3.4\">Insert bar</a>).</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-malarz.png\" alt=\"Copy style in the panel header\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Copy style in the panel header</figcaption></figure><ol class=\"kroki\"><li>Select an element that already looks right.</li><li>Click <span class=\"ui\">Copy style</span> (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Alt</kbd>+<kbd>C</kbd>).</li><li>Click the element that should get this look.</li></ol><ul class=\"punkty\"><li><b>Double-click</b> <span class=\"ui\">Copy style</span> — continuous mode: you click one element after another until you press <kbd>Esc</kbd>.</li><li><b>Part of a text:</b> in the text you're typing, select words and click <span class=\"ui\">Copy style</span> — only the selected part gets the look. You can copy the look from a part of a text the same way.</li></ul><p>Everything to do with the look is transferred — colors, gradients, stroke, font, spacing, photo filters, opacity, shadow, print finishes — and the content stays. Tables, codes, charts and calendars have their own settings and don't take on the look.</p>"},{"id":"4.14","tytul":"Speech bubble","html":"<p>A speech bubble is one element: a text frame with a bubble around it. Insert it from <span class=\"ui\">Library</span>, category <b>Speech bubbles</b>. You type in it like in an ordinary text frame – the bubble grows with the text.</p><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Look</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>speech</b></td><td>Oval with a tail.</td></tr><tr><td><b>thought</b></td><td>Cloud with small circles instead of a tail.</td></tr><tr><td><b>shout</b></td><td>Burst with spikes.</td></tr><tr><td><b>whisper</b></td><td>Oval with a dashed outline.</td></tr><tr><td><b>narration</b></td><td>Rectangular box without a tail.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class=\"punkty\"><li>Drag the orange handle on the page to move the tip of the tail – put it at the character's mouth. The tail moves together with the bubble.</li><li>In the <b>Speech bubble</b> part of the panel you change the <b>Type</b>, turn the <b>Tail</b> on or off, and set <b>Fill</b>, <b>Outline</b> and <b>Outline width</b>. Font, size and text colour work as in ordinary text (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"5\">Text</a>).</li><li>The bubble comes out in the PDF as vectors, just like on the page.</li></ul>"}]},
  {"nr":"5","tytul":"Text","czesc":"Editor","ikona":"olowek","wstep":"Text frames, their look, styles and typesetting — from a single caption to the text of a book flowing across many pages.","sekcje":[{"id":"5.1","tytul":"Typing","html":"<p>Insert text with the <span class=\"ui\">Text</span> button (<kbd>T</kbd>) and start typing right away. Double-click existing text to type in it; <kbd>Esc</kbd> ends typing. <kbd>Enter</kbd> starts a new paragraph.</p><p>Change the frame width with the side handles — the text reflows, the letters don't stretch. The frame height grows with the text unless you set a fixed height (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"5.6\">Text frame</a>).</p><figure><img src=\"img/instrukcja/wstawianie.png\" alt=\"Text on the page\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Text on the page</figcaption></figure>"},{"id":"5.2","tytul":"Text look","html":"<p>Properties of the selected text:</p><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Style</b></td><td>A saved text look (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"5.4\">Styles</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Font</b></td><td>The typeface. In the list, start typing a name to find it (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"5.8\">Fonts</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Size</b></td><td>In points (pt). The circle on the right is the <b>text color</b>.</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ui\">B</span> <span class=\"ui\">I</span> <span class=\"ui\">U</span> <span class=\"ui\">S</span></td><td>Bold, italic, underline, strikethrough. Grayed out when the font has no such variant.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Alignment</b></td><td>Left, center, right, justified.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Line spacing</b></td><td>Space between lines; 1 is single.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Letter spacing</b></td><td>Spreads letters apart; a negative value tightens the text.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Letter case</b></td><td><span class=\"ui\">AA</span> — all caps, <span class=\"ui\">aa</span> — all lowercase, <span class=\"ui\">Aᴀ</span> — small caps: this is only the look, the content stays as typed. <span class=\"ui\">Aa</span> — “sentence case”: changes the content itself.</td></tr><tr><td><b>List</b></td><td>Bullets, dashes or numbers before each paragraph (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"5.5\">Lists</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Letter outline</b></td><td>Color and <b>Letter stroke weight</b> — an outline around each letter.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure><img src=\"img/instrukcja/wlasciwosci.png\" alt=\"Text properties\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Text properties</figcaption></figure>"},{"id":"5.3","tytul":"Part of a text","html":"<p>Double-click the text, select a part and change it with the same settings — the panel shows “You're changing only the selected text.” This is how you make one word bold or change the color of one line. The look of a part containing a field (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"15\">Fields in text</a>) carries over to the inserted value.</p>"},{"id":"5.4","tytul":"Styles","html":"<p>A <b>style</b> is a saved text look, e.g. “Title”, “Caption”, “Bubble”. Apply it to all texts that should look the same.</p><ul class=\"punkty\"><li>The <b>Style</b> list in the properties applies a style to the selected text.</li><li><span class=\"ui\">New style</span> saves the look of the selected text under a new name.</li><li>When you change a text that has a style, <span class=\"ui\">Update style</span> appears — it changes all texts with that style in the whole project — and <span class=\"ui\">Restore</span> — it goes back to the style's look.</li><li>A style also saves the <b>Paragraph composition</b> settings (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"5.9\">Paragraph composition</a>) — e.g. hyphenation for the whole text of a book.</li></ul><p>A <b>character style</b> is a saved look of a part of a text, e.g. “Emphasis” or “Proper name”.</p><ol class=\"kroki\"><li>Select part of a text (double-click the text and select the words).</li><li>Set its look (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"5.3\">Part of a text</a>), then in the <b>Text style</b> part choose <span class=\"ui\">New character style</span>.</li><li>Select other parts and choose the style from the <b>Character style</b> list. With nothing selected, the whole text gets the style.</li></ol><p><span class=\"ui\">Update style</span> moves the look of the selection into the style — all parts with that style in the project change. Deleting a character style doesn't change the look of the parts.</p>"},{"id":"5.5","tytul":"Lists","html":"<p>The <b>List</b> list in the text properties: <b>Bullets</b>, <b>Dashes</b> or <b>Numbers</b>. The list mark stands before each paragraph, and the following lines of the paragraph line up under the first. In text flowing through several frames, numbering continues. Shortcuts: <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>8</kbd> (bullets) and <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>7</kbd> (numbers).</p>"},{"id":"5.6","tytul":"Text frame","html":"<p>The <b>Text frame</b> part is useful when the text should take a fixed space — e.g. texts from data of varying length (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"17.6\">Text of varying length</a>):</p><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Fixed frame height</b></td><td>The frame doesn't grow with the text. Type the height in mm or change it with the top or bottom handle.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Vertical alignment</b></td><td>Text at the top, middle or bottom of the frame.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Shrink text when it doesn't fit</b></td><td>The font gets smaller until the text fits — but not below the <b>minimum size</b>.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Text that still doesn't fit is cut off at the bottom of the frame and goes on the warnings list.</p><figure><img src=\"img/instrukcja/ramka-tekstu.png\" alt=\"A fixed-height frame\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>A fixed-height frame</figcaption></figure>"},{"id":"5.7","tytul":"Columns and text across several frames","html":"<p>Longer text can flow through several frames: whatever doesn't fit in the first one moves on to the next. The <b>Text flow</b> part:</p><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Button</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span class=\"ui\">Split into columns</span></td><td>Turns the frame into 2, 3 or 4 columns side by side.</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ui\">Add continuation frame</span></td><td>A new frame next to it (or below it) that the rest of the text flows into.</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ui\">Continued on next page</span></td><td>A frame on the next page — with a new page if needed.</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ui\">Link text frames</span></td><td>Links two selected frames.</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ui\">Unlink frames</span></td><td>Each frame keeps its own part of the text.</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ui\">Previous frame</span>, <span class=\"ui\">Next frame</span></td><td>Go to the neighboring frame, also on another page.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>You type in any frame, and the text reflows as you go. The text look (font, size, list) is shared by the linked frames. A purple line shows where the text from the selected frame flows.</p><p><b>Long text across many pages (a book).</b> Turn on <b>Add pages until all the text fits</b> — when the text doesn't fit in the last frame, new pages are added after its page (in the same section, with its masters) with a frame in the same place and size. When the text gets shorter, empty added pages at the end disappear. Pages are added after you finish typing (Esc or a click outside), after pasting and after changing the text look.</p><p><b>A paragraph on a new page</b> (e.g. a chapter): put the cursor in the paragraph and turn on <b>Start this paragraph on a new page</b> — the paragraph and everything after it moves to the next frame. You choose this for each paragraph separately: a chapter can start on a new page or keep flowing. With page adding on, the next frame is created automatically.</p><figure><img src=\"img/instrukcja/uklad.png\" alt=\"Columns, lists and a table\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Columns, lists and a table</figcaption></figure>"},{"id":"5.8","tytul":"Fonts","html":"<p>You can choose from 55 font families with Polish characters that may be used in commercial print: sans-serif for body text (e.g. Inter, Lato, Roboto), condensed for headings (Oswald, Bebas Neue), serif (Lora, Merriweather, Playfair Display, EB Garamond), handwritten and decorative (Caveat, Pacifico, Dancing Script), comic (Bangers for shouts, Comic Neue and Pangolin for dialogue) and typewriter fonts. Most also have Ukrainian letters (Cyrillic) – e.g. Inter, Roboto, Lora and Pangolin; when a font lacks them, the PDF export tells you (such text goes in as an image).</p><p><span class=\"ui\">⋯</span> → <span class=\"ui\">Project fonts…</span> shows samples, gives you font files to download (e.g. for the print shop) and lets you upload <b>your own font</b> (TTF, OTF, WOFF, WOFF2; each variant separately) — also from the font list: <span class=\"ui\">+ Upload your own font…</span>. An uploaded font stays in your account, under “My fonts”.</p><p class=\"ramka-instrukcji wskazowka\"><b>Tip.</b> For print, it's best to upload your own font as TTF or OTF, with a separate file for bold and italic. Other files go into the PDF as outlines — the text will be sharp, but you won't be able to select it.</p><figure><img src=\"img/instrukcja/czcionki.png\" alt=\"Fonts window\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Fonts window</figcaption></figure><p>At the top of the <b>Font</b> list is <b>Used in project</b> — fonts from the texts of all pages, masters and styles, most frequent first, with the number of uses (e.g. “Inter · 5×”). The full list is below.</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-czcionki-uzyte.png\" alt=\"Used in project in the font list\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Used in project in the font list</figcaption></figure><p>If you have a brand kit (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"27.9\">Brand kit</a>), above them is the <b>Brand</b> group — the brand's heading font and body font.</p>"},{"id":"5.9","tytul":"Paragraph composition","html":"<p>The collapsible <b>Paragraph composition</b> part in the text properties decides how the text breaks into lines. You can save the same settings in a style (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"5.4\">Styles</a>).</p><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Polish typesetting rules</b></td><td>On in new texts. A line doesn't break after an abbreviation (np., tj., ul., dr…), after an initial (J. Kowalski), between a number and a unit, a date or further digits (5 kg, 12 maja, 10 000), and a dash doesn't start a line — as in the Polish standards PN-83/P-55366 and PN-70/P-55036. With <b>Hyphenate</b>: at most 3 hyphenated lines in a row, at least 2 letters before the hyphen and 3 after it, no hyphenation of a paragraph's last word or of capitalized words (a word longer than the line is always hyphenated). In justified text, spaces may also tighten slightly (up to 20 %), so there are fewer lines with gaps. In text flowing through frames, a title (a short paragraph in larger or bold type) isn't left alone at the end of a frame — it moves on with the next paragraph, and the frames on the left and right page of a spread end at the same height (column balancing: when widows or a title shortened one, the other is shorter too). Under the switch there are notes about places that break the rules and a <b>Fix characters</b> button: curly quotes „…” instead of straight ones, an en dash (–) instead of a hyphen between spaces, an ellipsis (…) instead of three dots, no double spaces and no spaces before a comma — formatting of parts of the text stays.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Break the whole paragraph at once</b></td><td>On in new texts. The lines of a paragraph are chosen together, not one by one — like the “paragraph composer” in DTP programs. The program weighs all possible break points and picks the layout with the most even word spacing and as few hyphenated words as possible; the Polish typesetting rules still apply. You see it best in justified text: instead of one line with big gaps, the spacing is spread over several lines. A paragraph that wraps around another element, has a drop cap or tabs is broken line by line.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Hyphenate</b></td><td>Long words are hyphenated at the end of a line according to Polish rules — justified text then has no gaps. Next to it you set the shortest word to hyphenate and how many letters stay before and after the hyphen.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Conjunctions at line end</b></td><td>Single-letter <b>a, i, o, u, w, z</b> (or also two-letter ones: do, na, że…) move to the next line together with the following word, as in Polish typesetting.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Widows and orphans</b></td><td>Text flowing through several frames (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"5.7\">Columns and text across several frames</a>) doesn't leave a lone line of a paragraph at the bottom or top of a frame.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Align to baseline grid</b></td><td>Lines sit on the section's grid lines (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"5.13\">Baseline grid</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Don't wrap around other elements</b></td><td>The text lies over elements with text wrap instead of flowing around them (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"5.12\">Text wrap</a>) — e.g. a caption on a photo.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-sklad-akapitu.png\" alt=\"Paragraph composition in the text properties\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Paragraph composition in the text properties</figcaption></figure><p><b>Typesetting warnings.</b> Text that breaks the Polish rules (a conjunction or abbreviation at the end of a line, a number separated from its unit, a dash at the start of a line, more than 3 hyphenated lines in a row, a hyphenated last word of a paragraph, a very short last line of a paragraph, a loose line, a straight quote, three dots, double spaces…) shows up in the editor warnings and in the preflight check (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"20.1\">List of issues</a>) with an example of the spot. To silence a warning for intentional text, use the <b>Mute</b> button.</p>"},{"id":"5.10","tytul":"Drop cap","html":"<p>A drop cap is an enlarged first letter of a paragraph spanning several lines. In the <b>Paragraph composition</b> part, choose <b>Drop cap</b>: <b>2 lines</b> … <b>5 lines</b>. The letter extends from the top edge of the first line to the baseline of the last one, and the text wraps around it. The <b>Drop cap font</b> and <b>Drop cap color</b> can differ from the text.</p><p>The drop cap goes on the first paragraph of the text. In text flowing through several frames — only in the first frame.</p><figure><img src=\"img/instrukcja/sklad.png\" alt=\"Drop cap, hyphenation, dotted tab stops and text wrapping around a circle\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Drop cap, hyphenation, dotted tab stops and text wrapping around a circle</figcaption></figure>"},{"id":"5.11","tytul":"Tab stops","html":"<p>The <kbd>Tab</kbd> key in the text you're typing inserts a tab: the text after it jumps to the nearest position. Without set positions, tab stops are every 12.5 mm.</p><p>In the <b>Paragraph composition</b> part, <span class=\"ui\">Add tab stop</span> adds a position. Each one has:</p><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Tab stop N</b></td><td>Position in mm from the left edge of the frame.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Alignment</b></td><td><b>left</b>, <b>center</b>, <b>right</b> or <b>decimal comma</b> (numbers with a comma line up one under another).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Fill</b></td><td>Empty, dots, dashes or a line before the text — e.g. a table of contents “Chapter 1 . . . . . 12”.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-tabulatory.png\" alt=\"A right tab stop with dots\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>A right tab stop with dots</figcaption></figure>"},{"id":"5.12","tytul":"Text wrap","html":"<p>A photo, shape or group can push text away. Select the element and in the <b>Other</b> part choose <b>Text wrap</b>:</p><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Value</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>text wraps around the element's frame</b></td><td>Lines end before the element's rectangle (or start after it).</td></tr><tr><td><b>text wraps around the shape</b></td><td>For an ellipse, polygon and pen shape — lines follow its edge.</td></tr><tr><td><b>text jumps below the element</b></td><td>There is no text next to the element; it continues below it.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Text offset from element</b> is the free space around the element (2 mm by default). Text wrap affects all texts on the page — also while you're moving the element. The text picks the side of the element where it has more room.</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-oblewanie.png\" alt=\"Text wrap in the Other part\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Text wrap in the Other part</figcaption></figure>"},{"id":"5.13","tytul":"Baseline grid","html":"<p>A baseline grid puts the lines of all columns and pages at the same height — as in books where the lines on both sides of a sheet match when held up to the light.</p><ol class=\"kroki\"><li>In the section settings (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"10.4\">Section settings</a>), turn on <b>Baseline grid in this section</b>: <b>Spacing</b> (usually the line spacing of the body text) and <b>First line</b> (mm from the top edge of the page, usually the top margin).</li><li>In the view menu (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"3.6\">Bottom-right corner and the view menu</a>), <b>Baseline grid</b> shows these lines on the page.</li><li>For texts that should sit on the grid, turn on <b>Align to baseline grid</b> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"5.9\">Paragraph composition</a>) — preferably in the body text style.</li></ol>"},{"id":"5.14","tytul":"Text on an arc, circle and path; warping","html":"<p>Select the text and in the <b>Text shape</b> part of the panel choose a <b>Shape</b>. You'll also find ready examples in <span class=\"ui\">Library</span>, in the <b>Texts</b> category.</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-ksztalt-tekstu.png\" alt=\"Text on an arc, in a circle and warped\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Text on an arc, in a circle and warped</figcaption></figure><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Shape</th><th>What it does and what you set</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Arc</b></td><td>Text along an arc. <b>Bend</b> in %: positive – arc up, negative – down.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Circle</b>, <b>Ellipse</b></td><td>Text all the way around, e.g. a seal: radius, start, letters facing out or in.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Along a path</b></td><td>Text along a line of any shape on the page (drawn with the pen or from the Library) — choose it in <b>Line from a shape on the page</b>.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Warp</b></td><td>Letters warped like an arc, bulge, wave, flag or taper; <b>Bend strength</b> in %.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class=\"punkty\"><li>With an arc and warps, the ◆ handle under the text bends it more or less.</li><li>Double-click the text to edit it — while you type, it's shown straight.</li><li>Text on a shape is a single line; font, color, styles and <code>{…}</code> fields work as usual.</li><li><b>Straight</b> brings back ordinary text.</li></ul>"},{"id":"5.15","tytul":"Find and replace","html":"<p>The magnifier on the top bar or <span class=\"ui\">⋯</span> → <span class=\"ui\">Find and replace…</span> (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>F</kbd>; <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>H</kbd> — opens straight with the replace field).</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-znajdz.png\" alt=\"Find and replace next to the page\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Find and replace next to the page</figcaption></figure><ol class=\"kroki\"><li>Type the text to find. The list shows each match: the page or master and a bit of the surrounding text.</li><li>Click a match (or <kbd>Enter</kbd> in the field, <kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Enter</kbd> — backwards) — the editor opens that page and selects the found text.</li><li>To replace, type the new text in the second field and click <span class=\"ui\">Replace</span> (the selected match) or <span class=\"ui\">Replace all</span>.</li></ol><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Match case</b></td><td>“Calendar” won't find “calendar”.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Whole words</b></td><td>“cat” won't find “cats”.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Also in data</b></td><td>Also searches the cells of data tables (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"14\">Data tables</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><b>In style names</b></td><td>Also searches the names of paragraph and character styles (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"5.4\">Styles</a>).</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class=\"punkty\"><li>Search covers all pages and masters, including texts in groups.</li><li>Fields in braces, e.g. <code>{miesiac}</code>, stay untouched — unless you type a brace in the search text.</li><li>The look of parts of the text (e.g. a bold word) stays in place after replacing.</li><li>You can undo <span class=\"ui\">Replace all</span> with a single <span class=\"ui\">Undo</span>.</li></ul>"},{"id":"5.16","tytul":"Spell check","html":"<p>Words not in the Polish dictionary are underlined with a wavy red line — only in the editor, never in the PDF.</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-pisownia-dymek.png\" alt=\"Suggestions for an underlined word\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Suggestions for an underlined word</figcaption></figure><ol class=\"kroki\"><li>Double-click the text and click (on a phone, tap) the underlined word — or right-click it. A bubble with suggestions and <span class=\"ui\">Add to dictionary</span> appears next to the word.</li><li>Click a suggestion (e.g. „wcześniej”) — the word changes in that spot.</li><li>Add a proper name the dictionary doesn't know (e.g. a company name) with the <span class=\"ui\">Add to dictionary</span> button. Your dictionary is stored in your account, so it works in every project.</li></ol><p>All suspicious words of the selected text are also listed in the panel, in the <b>Spelling</b> part — there a suggestion changes the word in all places in that text.</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-pisownia.png\" alt=\"The “Spelling” part in the text panel\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>The “Spelling” part in the text panel</figcaption></figure><ul class=\"punkty\"><li><code>{…}</code> fields, numbers, words with digits, web addresses and emails are skipped.</li><li>You can turn spell checking off in the view menu: <b>Check spelling</b>.</li></ul>"},{"id":"5.17","tytul":"AI text: fixing and translating","html":"<p>AI can fix, shorten or translate a text – in every project. Google Gemini writes it with your key (how to get a key – <span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"7.11\">AI image</a>); one change costs a fraction of a grosz.</p><ol class=\"kroki\"><li>Select a text frame – you change the whole text. Or double-click into the text and select a part – you change only that part.</li><li>Click <span class=\"ui\">AI ▾</span> on the insert bar → <b>Improve or translate text…</b>.</li><li>Click what to do: <span class=\"ui\">Shorten</span>, <span class=\"ui\">Expand</span>, <span class=\"ui\">Simpler</span>, <span class=\"ui\">More formal</span>, <span class=\"ui\">Fix mistakes</span>, <span class=\"ui\">To Polish</span>, <span class=\"ui\">To English</span> or <span class=\"ui\">To Ukrainian</span>.</li><li>After a moment the result is in the <b>Result (you can edit it)</b> field. Edit it if you like, or click another button to try something else.</li><li><span class=\"ui\">Replace</span> puts the result in place of the text. <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd> undoes.</li></ol><p>Fields in braces, e.g. <code>{name}</code>, stay unchanged.</p><p><b>Translating a whole project.</b> For many texts at once (e.g. a calendar in three languages) keep the texts in a data table and translate the column: the <b>✦</b> button next to the column name in the <b>Data</b> window (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"14.6\">Fill a column with AI</a>), e.g. “translate the opis column into English”. Other changes in many places at once can be done by the AI Assistant (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"25.6\">AI Assistant in Makietka (own key)</a>).</p>"}]},
  {"nr":"6","tytul":"Shapes, lines and colors","czesc":"Editor","ikona":"olowek","wstep":"Shapes, lines, drawing and colors: fills, gradients and strokes.","sekcje":[{"id":"6.1","tytul":"Rectangle, ellipse and polygon","html":"<p><span class=\"ui\">Shapes ▾</span> → <b>Rectangle</b>, <b>Ellipse</b> and <b>Polygon or star</b> insert shapes with a fill and a stroke (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"6.5\">Fill</a>, <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"6.6\">Stroke</a>). <b>Polygon or star</b> first shows a choice: triangle, diamond, pentagon, hexagon and octagon, or a star with 4, 5, 6, 8 or 12 points. The <kbd>P</kbd> key inserts a triangle right away. The <b>Shape</b> section:</p><ul class=\"punkty\"><li>rectangle — corner <b>Rounding</b> in mm,</li><li>polygon — the same ready-made shapes as when inserting (triangle … star 12), <b>Number of sides</b> (3–24) and the <b>Star</b> switch; changing it keeps the position and size.</li></ul>"},{"id":"6.2","tytul":"Line","html":"<p><span class=\"ui\">Shapes ▾</span> → <b>Line</b> inserts a line segment. The <b>Line</b> section: <b>Color</b>, <b>Weight</b> in mm and <b>Type</b> — solid, dashed, dotted or dash – dot (the dashes grow with the weight). With <kbd>Shift</kbd> the line is horizontal, vertical or at 45°. A freehand line and a pen shape have the same section, and a closed pen shape also has <b>Fill</b>. Ready-made lines (thin, thick, dashed, double, vertical, decorative) are in the Library, in the <b>Lines and separators</b> category (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"4.8\">Library</a>).</p>"},{"id":"6.3","tytul":"Freehand drawing","html":"<p><span class=\"ui\">Shapes ▾</span> → <b>Freehand drawing</b> (<kbd>B</kbd>): drag across the page like a pencil — hand tremor is smoothed out. Each stroke is a separate element. The mode stays on, so you can draw several strokes.</p><p>You set the color and weight of the next lines in the properties, in the <b>Brush</b> section (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"3.5\">Properties and layers</a>). <span class=\"ui\">Finish drawing</span> or <kbd>Esc</kbd> ends drawing.</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-rysowanie.png\" alt=\"Drawing: brush color and weight\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Drawing: brush color and weight</figcaption></figure>"},{"id":"6.4","tytul":"Pen and point editing","html":"<p><span class=\"ui\">Shapes ▾</span> → <b>Pen</b> (<kbd>N</kbd>) creates a shape from points:</p><ul class=\"punkty\"><li>a click places a corner point, a click and drag — a point with a curve,</li><li>clicking the first point closes the shape,</li><li><kbd>Enter</kbd> or a double-click finishes an open shape,</li><li><kbd>Backspace</kbd> undoes the last point, <kbd>Esc</kbd> discards the shape you started.</li></ul><p><b>Point editing:</b> double-click the shape or choose <span class=\"ui\">Edit points</span>. Drag the points, and use the handles of the selected point to change the curves. Double-clicking the line adds a point, <kbd>Delete</kbd> removes the selected one. <kbd>Enter</kbd> confirms, <kbd>Esc</kbd> restores the shape from before editing. You change a freehand line the same way.</p><figure><img src=\"img/instrukcja/pioro.png\" alt=\"Point editing\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Point editing</figcaption></figure>"},{"id":"6.5","tytul":"Fill","html":"<p>The <b>Fill and stroke</b> section, the <b>Fill</b> list:</p><p><b>None</b>, <b>One color</b>, <b>Linear gradient</b> (in one direction, at the given <b>Angle</b>), <b>Radial gradient</b> (from the center outwards, its reach — <b>Radius</b>) or <b>Pattern</b>.</p><p>The gradient bar shows the colors. <span class=\"ui\">Add color</span> adds another one; clicking a color on the bar lets you change it and its position in %, <span class=\"ui\">Remove this color from the gradient</span> removes it.</p><p>Each gradient color has an <b>Opacity</b> field — e.g. a color fading into transparency over a photo.</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-krycie-przejscia.png\" alt=\"Color opacity in a gradient\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Color opacity in a gradient</figcaption></figure><p><b>Pattern</b> fills the shape with a repeating pattern from the Library — stripes, dots, check, herringbone, waves and more. You pick it from the tiles, and below them you set the <b>Pattern color</b>, <b>Background color</b>, <b>Pattern scale</b> and <b>Pattern rotation</b>. The pattern doesn't stretch when you resize the shape, and in the PDF file it stays sharp (vector). A closed pen shape and Library shapes have a <b>Fill with pattern</b> switch in the <b>Line</b> section. A pattern tile in the Library, with a shape selected, asks whether to <span class=\"ui\">Fill the selected shape</span> or <span class=\"ui\">Insert on page</span>.</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-wzor-ksztaltu.png\" alt=\"Shapes filled with a pattern\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Shapes filled with a pattern</figcaption></figure>"},{"id":"6.6","tytul":"Stroke","html":"<p>Stroke color and <b>Stroke weight</b> in mm. The weight doesn't change when you enlarge the shape. With the stroke turned on, there are also <b>Stroke opacity</b> (a semi-transparent frame) and <b>Line type</b> — solid, dashed, dotted or dash – dot; the dashes grow with the weight.</p><p>A shape (also one with an image inside) can have <b>Soft edges</b> — just like a photo (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"7.10\">Border and soft edges</a>).</p>"},{"id":"6.7","tytul":"Choosing a color","html":"<p>Clicking a color circle opens the color window: a picker, the <b>Color code</b> (e.g. <code>#2f5bd3</code>), preset and recently used colors, and <b>Used in project</b> — colors from all pages, masters and styles, most frequent first (a spot color shows its name, e.g. \"PANTONE 186 C\", <span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"22.1\">Spot colors</a>).</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-kolory-uzyte.png\" alt=\"Used in project in the color window\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Used in project in the color window</figcaption></figure><p>When you have a brand kit (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"27.9\">Brand kit</a>), there is a <b>Brand</b> section above \"Used in project\" — brand colors with names (hover to see the name and code).</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-marka-kolor.png\" alt=\"Brand colors in the color window\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Brand colors in the color window</figcaption></figure>"}]},
  {"nr":"7","tytul":"Images","czesc":"Editor","ikona":"olowek","wstep":"Photos and graphics: inserting, cropping, borders, filters and background removal.","sekcje":[{"id":"7.1","tytul":"Inserting an image","html":"<p><span class=\"ui\">Image</span> asks where to get the image from:</p><ul class=\"punkty\"><li><span class=\"ui\">Upload from device</span> — choose a PNG, JPEG, WebP, GIF or SVG file,</li><li><span class=\"ui\">Choose from project images</span> — an image that is already in the project, without a second copy.</li></ul><p>In a project with no images, the file picker opens right away. You can also insert an image by dragging a file onto the page or pasting from the clipboard (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>V</kbd>) — e.g. a screenshot or an image copied from a web page.</p>"},{"id":"7.2","tytul":"Cropping","html":"<p>Double-click the image or choose <span class=\"ui\">Drag to crop</span>: you move the photo inside the frame and change its edges. <kbd>Enter</kbd> confirms, <kbd>Esc</kbd> cancels. The same with numbers: <b>Crop (% of original)</b> — <b>From above</b>, <b>From below</b>, <b>Left</b>, <b>Right</b>.</p>"},{"id":"7.3","tytul":"Image properties","html":"<div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span class=\"ui\">Replace photo</span></td><td>A different photo in the same frame (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"7.4\">Replacing a photo</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ui\">Project images…</span></td><td>The window with all images (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"7.5\">The Project images window</a>).</td></tr><tr><td>Description under the heading</td><td>Original dimensions in pixels and the resolution at this size (dpi). For print — at least 300 dpi; below that the description changes color.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Border</b></td><td>A frame around the photo: color, <b>Weight</b>, <b>Stroke opacity</b>, <b>Line type</b> (solid, dashed, dotted, dash – dot) and <b>Corner rounding</b> in mm — the rounding also crops the photo itself (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"7.10\">Border and soft edges</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Flip horizontally</b></td><td>Mirrors the image.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Image from data</b></td><td>A different image on each duplicated page (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"17.5\">Image from data</a>).</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},{"id":"7.4","tytul":"Replacing a photo","html":"<p><span class=\"ui\">Replace photo</span> puts a different file into the same frame: the size, position, rotation, stroke and shadow stay, and a photo with different proportions is cropped to the frame.</p>"},{"id":"7.5","tytul":"The Project images window","html":"<p><span class=\"ui\">⋯</span> → <span class=\"ui\">Project images…</span> shows all the project's images: thumbnail, full file name, dimensions, size and where they are used (page numbers, masters, data tables).</p><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Button</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span class=\"ui\">Upload images…</span></td><td>Adds files from your device, several at once — also a ZIP archive with images.</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ui\">Copy name</span></td><td>On an image card: copies the file name, e.g. into a data table.</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ui\">⋯</span> on a card</td><td>Less frequent actions for this image: <span class=\"ui\">Rename</span>, <span class=\"ui\">Replace file…</span>, <span class=\"ui\">Copy name</span>, <span class=\"ui\">Remove image…</span>.</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ui\">Rename</span></td><td>A new name (also by clicking the name); data table cells with this name change along with it.</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ui\">Replace file…</span></td><td>A new file in place of the old one — everywhere the image was used.</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ui\">Delete</span></td><td>Deletes the selected images. The app won't delete images that are in use — it tells you where they are.</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ui\">Remove unused</span></td><td>Removes images that nothing uses.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-menu-obrazu.png\" alt=\"The ⋯ menu on an image card\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>The ⋯ menu on an image card</figcaption></figure><p>Clicking an image selects it; <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> adds more, <kbd>Shift</kbd> — a whole range. The same file uploaded a second time doesn't create a copy. A different file with the same name — the app shows both images and asks: <span class=\"ui\">Replace</span>, <span class=\"ui\">Keep both</span> or <span class=\"ui\">Skip</span>. When the project has duplicate images from before this window existed, a bar with <span class=\"ui\">Tidy up</span> appears at the top — it merges the copies into one file. With several images selected, <span class=\"ui\">Copy names</span> copies all the names at once, e.g. into a data table column.</p><p class=\"ramka-instrukcji uwaga\"><b>Note.</b> <span class=\"ui\">Undo</span> won't bring back a deleted image.</p><figure><img src=\"img/instrukcja/obrazy-projektu.png\" alt=\"Project images\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Project images</figcaption></figure>"},{"id":"7.6","tytul":"SVG drawings","html":"<p>An SVG file is inserted as an image: it looks faithful and stays sharp at any zoom, but you can't change its colors or text.</p><p><span class=\"ui\">Break drawing apart into elements</span> (in the image properties) turns it on the page into a <b>group of ordinary shapes and text</b> in the same place. You can change the shapes' color and points, and edit the text like any other text. The SVG file doesn't change and stays in the project images window.</p><p>Nothing gets lost along the way:</p><ul class=\"punkty\"><li><b>clipping</b> and <b>color gradients</b> remain shapes,</li><li>a <b>simple mask</b> (a white shape) becomes clipping, and a <b>pattern</b> (e.g. dots, check) — repeated shapes clipped to their figure,</li><li>parts with <b>effects the editor doesn't have</b> (blur, filter shadow, gradient mask, color blending, line arrowheads, text on a path) remain an <b>image</b> in the same place, at print resolution. After breaking apart, the app tells you how many there are; the rest of the drawing is editable.</li></ul><p><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd> restores the whole drawing.</p><figure><img src=\"img/instrukcja/rysunek-svg.png\" alt=\"SVG drawing before and after breaking apart\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>SVG drawing before and after breaking apart</figcaption></figure>"},{"id":"7.7","tytul":"Image in a shape","html":"<p>Any shape can be an image frame: rectangle, ellipse, polygon, star and a closed pen shape.</p><ol class=\"kroki\"><li>Select the shape and in the <b>Image in shape</b> section choose <span class=\"ui\">Insert image into shape</span> — or drop an image file straight onto the shape.</li><li>The image fills the shape and is clipped to its edges.</li><li>Double-click the shape to move the image and enlarge it with the corners; <kbd>Enter</kbd> confirms, <kbd>Esc</kbd> cancels. On a phone, use the <b>Zoom</b> and <b>Offset</b> fields.</li></ol><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-obraz-w-ksztalcie.png\" alt=\"A shape with an image and the &quot;Image in shape&quot; section\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>A shape with an image and the &quot;Image in shape&quot; section</figcaption></figure><figure><img src=\"img/instrukcja/obraz-w-ksztalcie-kadr.png\" alt=\"Cropping an image in a shape: the whole image semi-transparent, the shape as an outline\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Cropping an image in a shape: the whole image semi-transparent, the shape as an outline</figcaption></figure><p>You change the shape as usual — size, points, stroke, shadow — and the image follows it. <span class=\"ui\">Release image</span> leaves an ordinary image in place of the shape. On data pages, choose <b>Image from column</b>: each page gets a different image.</p><p>You can turn a photo into a shape with one click: in the <b>Cropping</b> section, <span class=\"ui\">Rounded corners</span> or <span class=\"ui\">Circle</span>. You can change the rounding later in the <b>Fill and stroke</b> section.</p>"},{"id":"7.8","tytul":"Removing the background","html":"<p>Select a photo (or a shape with an image) and choose <span class=\"ui\">Remove background…</span>.</p><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Magic wand</b></td><td>Click the background — colors similar to the clicked one disappear. <b>Tolerance</b> sets how similar.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Contiguous only</b></td><td>Leaves the same colors inside the photo untouched.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Delete</b> / <b>Restore</b></td><td>Brushes for touching up the edges, with a mouse or a finger.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Edge smoothing</b></td><td>Softens the edge.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><span class=\"ui\">Save as new image</span> adds a new file to the project images and puts it in place of the old one; the original stays.</p><figure><img src=\"img/instrukcja/usun-tlo.png\" alt=\"The &quot;Remove background&quot; window\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>The &quot;Remove background&quot; window</figcaption></figure>"},{"id":"7.9","tytul":"Filters","html":"<p>In the <b>Filters</b> section of a photo or an image in a shape, you set <b>brightness</b>, <b>contrast</b>, <b>saturation</b> and <b>blur</b>, and turn on <b>grayscale</b> or <b>sepia</b>. The file doesn't change; <span class=\"ui\">No filters</span> goes back to the original.</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-filtry.png\" alt=\"Image filters\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Image filters</figcaption></figure>"},{"id":"7.10","tytul":"Border and soft edges","html":"<p>The photo's <b>Border</b> section: color (the <b>none</b> field turns the frame off), <b>Weight</b> in mm, <b>Stroke opacity</b>, <b>Line type</b> and <b>Corner rounding</b> in mm. The rounding crops the photo itself, and the border follows the rounding. The weight and rounding stay the same when you resize the photo.</p><p>The <b>Soft edges</b> section (photos and shapes): <b>Transition width</b> in mm — how far from the edge the photo smoothly fades into transparency; 0 means a sharp edge. <b>Type</b>:</p><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Look</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>evenly along the edge</b></td><td>The transition runs along the edge — of the rectangle, the shape or the transparent areas of a PNG image.</td></tr><tr><td><b>oval vignette</b></td><td>An oval is visible in the middle, softly fading towards the edges and corners.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-obramowanie.png\" alt=\"Border with rounding and an oval vignette\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Border with rounding and an oval vignette</figcaption></figure><p>Border, rounding and soft edges are also carried over by <span class=\"ui\">Copy style</span> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"4.13\">Copy style (format painter)</a>).</p>"},{"id":"7.11","tytul":"AI image","html":"<p>AI draws an image from a description – in every project, not only comics. Google Gemini draws it with your key; you pay Google, and Makietka shows the cost before every generation.</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-obraz-ai.png\" alt=\"The AI image window\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>The AI image window</figcaption></figure><ol class=\"kroki\"><li>Select the frame, shape or comic panel the image should fill. With nothing selected, the image goes to the middle of the page.</li><li>Click <span class=\"ui\">AI ▾</span> on the insert bar → <b>Image from a description…</b>.</li><li>In the <b>What should be in the picture</b> field describe it in your own words: who, doing what, where, what mood and style, e.g. “A red-haired girl with a yellow backpack waving in a cobbled lane, morning, colourful children's illustration”.</li><li>Choose the <b>Quality</b>: <b>Draft – fast and cheapest</b> (for trying), <b>Detailed – for print</b> or <b>Best – characters and details</b>.</li><li>The <b>Image shape</b> (square, portrait, landscape, panorama, tall) is matched to the frame – change it only for an image placed on the page.</li><li>Check the <b>Cost</b> (and how much you have spent this month) and click <span class=\"ui\">Generate</span>. After 10–30 seconds the image is in the frame and in the <b>Project images</b> window.</li></ol><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Quality</th><th>What for</th><th>Approx. cost per image</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Draft</td><td>trying, preview, images for the screen</td><td>0.14 PLN (about $0.034)</td></tr><tr><td>Detailed</td><td>print</td><td>0.41 PLN (about $0.10)</td></tr><tr><td>Best</td><td>print, characters and small details</td><td>0.55 PLN (about $0.14)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ul class=\"punkty\"><li><b>No lettering in the image</b> – Makietka asks the AI not to draw any, and to fill the whole frame, with no white edges. You add text in Makietka (text frames, speech bubbles), so it is easy to fix and translate.</li><li><b>Again:</b> select the frame with the AI image and again <span class=\"ui\">AI ▾</span> → <b>Image from a description…</b>. The window has the description the current image was made from – change what you like in it, or generate unchanged. Earlier versions stay in <b>Project images</b> – go back to them with one choice (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"7.5\">The Project images window</a>).</li><li><b>Fix the current picture:</b> select the frame with a picture (from AI or your own) and choose <span class=\"ui\">AI ▾</span> → <b>Fix the picture in the frame…</b> – or in the <b>Image from a description…</b> window answer the question <b>What to do with the picture in the frame</b>. Choose <b>Fix the current picture – describe only the change</b> and write just the change, e.g. “give the girl a red hat” or “make it evening”. The AI gets the current picture and changes only what you ask for – the rest stays. <b>Draw it again from scratch</b> draws the whole picture from the description.</li><li><b>Without a Google Gemini key</b> the drawing and writing items in <span class=\"ui\">AI ▾</span> are greyed out, and at the top of the menu there is <b>You have no key for generating pictures and texts – add a key…</b> – it opens the AI settings, where you add the key (below).</li><li><b>How much you spent:</b> <span class=\"ui\">Account settings</span> → <b>Artificial intelligence</b> shows this month's spending on this device. There you can also set the <b>Monthly limit for AI images and texts</b> – once it is exceeded, Makietka will not generate an image or text until you change the limit.</li><li>The AI Assistant can also draw an image when you ask, e.g. “put a photo of a sunset over the sea on page 3” (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"25.6\">AI Assistant in Makietka (own key)</a>) – in the chat it writes what it is drawing and roughly how much it costs.</li></ul><p><b>How to get a Gemini key</b></p><ol class=\"kroki\"><li>Go to Google AI Studio (aistudio.google.com) and sign in with a Google account.</li><li>Click <b>Get API key</b> → <b>Create API key</b>. Copy the key.</li><li>To generate images, turn on <b>Billing</b> in Google AI Studio – without it only free texts work.</li><li>In Makietka: <span class=\"ui\">Account settings</span> → <b>Artificial intelligence</b> → <b>Chat in Makietka</b> → <span class=\"ui\">Add key</span> → <b>Google Gemini</b>, paste the key. The first time you set an <b>AI keys password</b> – the key is encrypted (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"25.6\">AI Assistant in Makietka (own key)</a>).</li></ol>"}]},
  {"nr":"8","tytul":"Table","czesc":"Editor","ikona":"olowek","wstep":"A table on the page — for price lists, schedules, lists. This is different from a data table for duplicating pages (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"14\">Data tables</a>).","sekcje":[{"id":"8.1","tytul":"Inserting a table","html":"<p><span class=\"ui\">Table</span> opens the <b>Insert table</b> window:</p><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Rows</b></td><td>Number of rows (1–60).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Columns</b></td><td>Number of columns (1–12).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Minimum row height (mm)</b></td><td>A row won't be lower than this; it grows when the text doesn't fit.</td></tr><tr><td><b>First row is a header</b></td><td>The first row is bold, with a thicker line below it.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>You move, resize and rotate the table as a whole, like any element. <b>A single click on a cell</b> of a selected table selects that cell (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"8.3\">Rows, columns and their size</a>), <b>a double-click</b> — starts typing in it (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"8.2\">Typing in cells</a>).</p><figure><img src=\"img/instrukcja/tabela.png\" alt=\"Table\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Table</figcaption></figure>"},{"id":"8.2","tytul":"Typing in cells","html":"<p>Double-click a cell to type in it. <kbd>Tab</kbd> moves to the next cell, <kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Tab</kbd> to the previous one; <kbd>Tab</kbd> in the last cell adds a row. A cell is ordinary text: you can make part of it bold, change the font, insert a data field (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"15\">Fields in text</a>). The row grows with the text — also on pages duplicated from data.</p>"},{"id":"8.3","tytul":"Rows, columns and their size","html":"<p><b>Whole table</b>: <b>Rows</b> and <b>Columns</b> <span class=\"ui\">−</span> <span class=\"ui\">+</span> (the last row and column), <b>First row is a header</b>, <b>Table width</b> in mm (the columns change proportionally), <b>Row height</b> (the minimum — a row grows with the text by itself) and <span class=\"ui\">Equalize column widths</span>.</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-tabela-cala.png\" alt=\"Whole table\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Whole table</figcaption></figure><p><b>Column and row</b> — click a cell of a selected table once (or type in it): the width of this column and the height of this row in mm, <span class=\"ui\">Column before</span>, <span class=\"ui\">Column after</span>, <span class=\"ui\">Row above</span>, <span class=\"ui\">Row below</span> and deleting.</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-tabela-komorka.png\" alt=\"Column and row of the selected cell\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Column and row of the selected cell</figcaption></figure><p><b>Widths of all columns</b> (a collapsed section) — the width of each column in mm, one below the other.</p><p>You can also move a column boundary with the mouse: with the table selected, there are blue markers above the column boundaries. The neighboring column narrows by the same amount, and the last marker changes the width of the whole table.</p>"},{"id":"8.4","tytul":"Merging cells","html":"<p>While typing in a cell: <span class=\"ui\">Merge with cell to the right</span> and <span class=\"ui\">Merge with cell below</span>. By clicking several times, you make a cell spanning several columns or rows. <span class=\"ui\">Split cells</span> goes back to single cells.</p>"},{"id":"8.5","tytul":"Lines and backgrounds","html":"<div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>What you set</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Table lines</b></td><td>Line color and weight (0 — no lines).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Row, column, table lines</b></td><td>Which lines — outer, horizontal, vertical — for this row, this column or the whole table.</td></tr><tr><td><b>This cell's lines</b></td><td>Each cell edge separately: as in the table, different or no line.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Row background</b></td><td>The color of the header row and of every other row (a striped table).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Text in cells</b></td><td>Font, size, color, alignment and the spacing from the cell edge.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}]},
  {"nr":"9","tytul":"Layers","czesc":"Editor","ikona":"olowek","wstep":"A list of all the elements on the page — for selecting, hiding and changing their order.","sekcje":[{"id":"9.1","tytul":"The Layers panel","html":"<p>The <b>Layers</b> panel shows all the elements of the open page, from the one on top to the one at the bottom. On a computer it's the <span class=\"ui\">Layers</span> tab at the top of the right column (<kbd>F7</kbd>), with the number of elements on the page; on a phone, the <span class=\"ui\">Layers</span> button at the bottom of the screen opens it.</p><figure><img src=\"img/instrukcja/warstwy.png\" alt=\"Layers\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Layers</figcaption></figure>"},{"id":"9.2","tytul":"A layer row","html":"<div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>In the row</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Name</b></td><td>A click selects the element, a double-click renames it.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Handle ⋮⋮</b></td><td>Drag the row to change the order — with a mouse or a finger.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Eye</b></td><td>Hides the element. A hidden element isn't printed.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Padlock</b></td><td>Locks the position and size.</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ui\">⋯</span></td><td><span class=\"ui\">Bring to front</span>, <span class=\"ui\">Higher</span>, <span class=\"ui\">Lower</span>, <span class=\"ui\">Send to back</span>, <span class=\"ui\">Rename</span>, <span class=\"ui\">Copy style</span> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"4.13\">Copy style (format painter)</a>), <span class=\"ui\">Save to library</span> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"4.8\">Library</a>).</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Selecting in the list comes in handy when an element lies under another one and is hard to hit on the page.</p>"},{"id":"9.3","tytul":"Groups, tables and masters in the list","html":"<ul class=\"punkty\"><li>The triangle next to a group or table expands its contents. Clicking an element of a group opens the group with that element selected (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"4.5\">Groups</a>); clicking a table cell starts typing in it.</li><li>At the top and bottom of the list are the elements of the page masters (above the content and below the content). You can't change them here — a click opens the master for editing (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"11.5\">Your own master and editing</a>).</li></ul>"}]},
  {"nr":"10","tytul":"Pages, sections and spreads","czesc":"Editor","ikona":"olowek","wstep":"Page order, sections with different formats, spreads, a cover with a spine, and page numbering.","sekcje":[{"id":"10.1","tytul":"Page panel","html":"<p>The panel on the left shows thumbnails of all pages in print order, grouped into sections (grey headers). Clicking a thumbnail opens the page. A page repeated from data has a <b>×N</b> badge — that is how many times it will appear in print (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"16\">Series</a>). Thumbnails are remembered on your device: the next time you open the project they appear right away, and only pages changed since then are redrawn.</p><p>On a phone, pages sit in a horizontal strip that you scroll with your finger.</p><figure class=\"kadr telefon\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-strony-telefon.png\" alt=\"Page strip on a phone\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Page strip on a phone</figcaption></figure><figure><img src=\"img/instrukcja/strony.png\" alt=\"Page panel with the page menu\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Page panel with the page menu</figcaption></figure>"},{"id":"10.2","tytul":"Adding and arranging pages","html":"<ul class=\"punkty\"><li>The <b>+</b> tile at the end of a section's pages adds a blank page after the current one.</li><li><span class=\"ui\">Section</span> at the bottom of the panel creates a new section.</li><li><b>Drag a thumbnail</b> to change the order; dropped in another section, it moves there.</li><li><span class=\"ui\">⋯</span> on a thumbnail: <span class=\"ui\">Rename</span>, <span class=\"ui\">Duplicate</span>, <span class=\"ui\">Move up</span>, <span class=\"ui\">Move down</span>, <span class=\"ui\">Delete page</span>.</li><li><span class=\"ui\">⋯</span> next to a section header: <span class=\"ui\">Section settings</span>, <span class=\"ui\">Add page to section</span>, <span class=\"ui\">Move section up</span>, <span class=\"ui\">Move section down</span>, <span class=\"ui\">Delete section</span>.</li></ul>"},{"id":"10.3","tytul":"Page properties","html":"<p>When nothing is selected, the properties panel shows the settings of the open page:</p><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Page name</b></td><td>The name in the page list.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Section</b></td><td>Moves the page to another section.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Background</b></td><td>Background color of this page.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Masters</b></td><td>Masters from the section or your own choice for this page (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"11.4\">Which pages a master is on</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Series</b></td><td>Repeats the page from a data table (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"16\">Series</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Guides</b></td><td>Guides entered as numbers (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"12.4\">Rulers and guides</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ui\">Paste copied elements</span></td><td>Pastes elements from the clipboard in the same place.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-seria.png\" alt=\"Page settings: series\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Page settings: series</figcaption></figure>"},{"id":"10.4","tytul":"Section settings","html":"<p>A section is a part of the publication with its own settings, e.g. “Cover” and “Interior”. You open the window with the format button on the top bar or with <span class=\"ui\">⋯</span> next to the section header → <span class=\"ui\">Section settings</span>.</p><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Section name</b></td><td>The name in the page list.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Page layout</b></td><td><b>Single</b> or <b>Spreads</b> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"10.5\">Spreads</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Section type</b></td><td>What the section is in the publication – a card with an icon and description: <b>Regular</b>, <b>Book block (interior)</b>, <b>Cover</b> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"10.6\">Cover with a spine</a>), <b>Front endpaper</b> and <b>back endpaper</b>, <b>Dust jacket</b>, <b>Insert</b>, <b>Backing board / back</b>, <b>Folded flyer</b>, <b>Yearly calendar</b>, <b>Three-month calendar</b>, <b>Die line</b>, <b>Imposition sheet</b> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"10.11\">Special sections: dust jacket, flyer, calendars, die lines, imposition</a>), <b>Paper model / cut-out</b> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"10.12\">Paper models and cut-outs</a>), <b>Comic</b> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"10.13\">Comic</a>). The type itself sets the format, zones, numbering and cover spine — the section name has no effect on this. You choose the type with <span class=\"ui\">Section</span> and change it here.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Format</b></td><td>Page size in mm. Changing it here does not touch the elements — to scale them together with the format, click <span class=\"ui\">Change format and fit elements…</span> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"10.9\">Changing the format with scaling</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Margins and bleed</b></td><td>You enter them on the page diagram, in mm.</td></tr><tr><td><b>First page of section</b></td><td>In spreads: <b>Right (as in a book)</b> or <b>Left (full spread right away)</b>. Quicker: in the page's <b>Properties</b> panel (nothing selected), the <b>Start section on a left page</b> switch – swaps left and right pages with one click, e.g. after adding a single page.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Page numbering</b></td><td><b>Continue from previous section</b>, <b>Start at number…</b> or <b>No numbers (e.g. cover)</b> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"10.8\">Page numbering</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Signatures (watch page count)</b></td><td><b>Don't check</b> or every 4, 8, 16, 32 pages (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"10.7\">Signatures</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Master pages on this section's pages</b></td><td>Default masters for the section's pages (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"11.4\">Which pages a master is on</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Substrate (what you print on)</b></td><td><b>White paper</b>, <b>Colored paper</b> (with a color) or <b>Clear film</b>. The paper color shows on the pages, in the preview and in the 3D mockup, and film – as a checkerboard; the substrate is not printed in the PDF (it's paper, not ink). White is not an ink: on colored paper and film, white elements come out in the substrate color – give them a <b>White underprint</b> (Print colors → Finishing). “Preflight check” reminds you of this.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Crop marks in the PDF</b></td><td>Crop marks on this section's pages in the file for the printer.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Baseline grid</b></td><td><b>Gap</b> and <b>First line</b> (mm from the top) — lines of texts with “Align to grid” sit on these lines (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"5.13\">Baseline grid</a>).</td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure><img src=\"img/instrukcja/sekcje.png\" alt=\"Section settings\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Section settings</figcaption></figure>"},{"id":"10.5","tytul":"Spreads","html":"<p>In a section with spreads, pages form pairs: left and right, like an open book.</p><ul class=\"punkty\"><li>You edit the left and right page <b>together</b>. New elements go onto the current page — you choose it by clicking in its area.</li><li>Margins are <b>mirrored</b>: inner by the spine, outer by the edge.</li><li>An element can lie <b>across the spine</b>, e.g. a photo over the whole spread — each page prints its own part. An element belongs to the page its center lies on.</li><li>The thick dark line is the spine. Don't put text right next to it — after binding it may not be visible; the app warns about elements that stick into the strip by the spine.</li><li>The button with two sheets next to the zoom switches between the whole spread and a single page (on a phone a single page by default).</li><li><b>A page moves from right to left on its own</b> (and the other way round): when you add, delete or move a page before it (or switch <b>Start section on a left page</b>), its elements go to mirrored positions — a number by the outer edge stays by the outer edge (aligned to it), a photo by the spine stays by the spine, and a text frame fitted to the margins lands in the new margins. Text and images are not flipped. Mark an element that should stay where it is in the panel (<b>Other</b> → <b>Fixed position when the page changes side</b>); an element across the spine belongs to the pair of pages and does not move. Pages repeated from data too: a copy that falls on the other side has a mirrored layout.</li></ul><figure><img src=\"img/instrukcja/rozkladowki.png\" alt=\"Spread\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Spread</figcaption></figure>"},{"id":"10.6","tytul":"Cover with a spine","html":"<p>A cover is a separate section with one wide page: back, spine and front. You create it in the section window (<b>Section type</b> → <b>Cover</b>), in the section menu (<span class=\"ui\">Convert to cover with spine</span>) or right away in the new project window (the <b>Cover</b> field).</p><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Binding</b></td><td><b>Softcover, perfect bound (with spine)</b>, <b>Saddle-stitched – stapled (no spine)</b> or <b>Hardcover (board with turn-in)</b> — for hardcover you set <b>Wrap</b>, <b>Crease</b>, <b>Board allowance</b>, <b>Board thickness</b> (mm) and <b>Spine</b>: <b>Rounded</b> (sewn block with a convex spine – the 3D mockup shows the curve) or <b>Straight</b> (flat).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Body paper</b></td><td><b>Paper weight</b> — <b>Paper weight (g/m²)</b> and <b>Paper type</b> (uncoated, matte coated, gloss coated, bulky) — or <b>Sheet thickness</b> in mm from the printer.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Body pages</b></td><td><b>Calculate automatically</b> — from all interior pages, including series repeats — or <b>Enter</b> the number. <b>Body is the sections</b> chooses which sections count when there are several.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The diagram in the window shows the back, spine and front, with the result below, e.g. “Spine 6.6 mm · Cover 352.6 × 246 mm”. The cover width and height are calculated automatically.</p><p><b>Dimensions</b> — when the printer gives you the spine or the whole cover size:</p><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Choice</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Calculate automatically</b></td><td>Spine from the paper and page count, size from the interior format.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Enter spine</b></td><td><b>Spine (mm)</b> from the printer instead of the calculated one. The rest is calculated as usual.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Enter size</b></td><td><b>Cover width</b> and <b>Cover height</b> (mm, without bleed) — the back and front split the rest of the width equally around the spine. You can also enter the spine; an empty field — the spine is calculated.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-okladka-wymiary.png\" alt=\"Cover dimensions entered manually\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Cover dimensions entered manually</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-okladka-okno.png\" alt=\"Section window: cover with spine, diagram and result\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Section window: cover with spine, diagram and result</figcaption></figure><p>On the canvas, dashed lines show the folds (spine, creases, turn-ins), and blue frames — the safe areas of the back, spine and front.</p><figure><img src=\"img/instrukcja/okladka.png\" alt=\"Cover on the canvas: back, spine, front\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Cover on the canvas: back, spine, front</figcaption></figure><p>When the interior gains pages or you change the paper, the cover recalculates itself: the back stays, the front moves together with the spine, text on the spine stays centered on it, and a background across the whole cover stretches. In the element panel, in the <b>Other</b> part, the <b>Attached to the cover by</b> list (automatically, back, spine or front) tells what the element sticks to; the <b>Fit width to spine</b> switch stretches a strip on the spine to its full width. The warning “Wider than the spine” means the element overlaps the back or front.</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-okladka-przypiecie.png\" alt=\"Element pinned to a part of the cover\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Element pinned to a part of the cover</figcaption></figure><p class=\"ramka-instrukcji uwaga\"><b>Note.</b> masters assigned to the cover section don't move on their own.</p><p><b>Cover material and corners.</b> In the same window:</p><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What you choose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Cover material</b></td><td><b>Printed cover</b> (printed board) or an unprinted covering: <b>Bookbinding cloth</b>, <b>Faux leather</b>, <b>Textured paper</b>, <b>Velvet (velour)</b>.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Laminate</b></td><td>For a printed cover: <b>matte</b>, <b>gloss</b>, <b>soft-touch</b> or <b>no laminate</b>.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Cover cloth color</b></td><td>Typical colors of the given covering (e.g. navy, burgundy, bottle green cloth) or <b>Other color…</b>.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Corners</b></td><td><b>square</b> or <b>rounded</b> 3, 5, 6 or 10 mm — on the canvas, red arcs in the corners show what will be cut off.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>On a covering, the cover artwork is not printed — only elements with gold foil or embossing remain (Print colors → Finishing). The 3D mockup shows the covering in the chosen color, with a cloth, leather or paper texture, and the foil and embossing on it. <b>Preflight check</b> reminds you about the covering and corners.</p><p><b>Print specification</b> (the <b>⋯</b> menu) downloads two files: text for an order or email, and JSON. They contain the formats and page counts of the sections, binding and spine, body paper, cover material, laminate and corners, ribbon, signatures, substrate, color mode, spot colors and finishes.</p>"},{"id":"10.7","tytul":"Signatures","html":"<p>An interior sewn or glued from signatures must have a page count divisible by the signature size. In the section settings choose <b>Signatures</b>: every 4, 8, 16 or 32 pages.</p><p>In the page list, under the section name, you'll see e.g. “46 of 48 – 2 missing for a full signature (×16)”. <span class=\"ui\">Fill with blanks</span> adds the missing blank pages at the end of the section — you undo it like any change. <b>Preflight check</b> also reminds you of an incomplete signature, and for saddle-stitched binding — of a page count not divisible by 4.</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-skladki.png\" alt=\"Signature counter and “Fill with blanks”\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Signature counter and “Fill with blanks”</figcaption></figure>"},{"id":"10.8","tytul":"Page numbering","html":"<p>You insert the page number with the <code>{page}</code> field — best in a master, so it's on every page (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"11.7\">Fields in a master</a>). Numbering depends on the section settings (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"10.4\">Section settings</a>) and counts pages after series repeats. Only book block sections are numbered (type <b>Regular</b>, <b>Block</b>, <b>Yearly calendar</b>): the cover, endpapers, dust jacket, insert, backing board and separate prints have no numbers and don't shift the numbering — the first page of the block is number 1 even when a cover and endpaper come before it. Other ways of writing the number (Roman, with leading zeros) are in the field table (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"15.2\">Document fields</a>).</p>"},{"id":"10.9","tytul":"Changing the format with scaling","html":"<p>A calendar from A4 to A3, a book from B5 to A5 — you change the format together with the elements:</p><ul class=\"punkty\"><li>one section: section window (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"10.4\">Section settings</a>) → <span class=\"ui\">Change format and fit elements…</span>,</li><li>whole project: <span class=\"ui\">⋯</span> → <span class=\"ui\">Change project format…</span>.</li></ul><ol class=\"kroki\"><li>Choose a <b>New format</b> from the list or enter <b>Width (mm)</b> and <b>Height (mm)</b>; <span class=\"ui\">Swap orientation</span> rotates the sheet.</li><li>Choose <b>What happens to elements</b>:</li></ol><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Scale everything</b></td><td>Elements, type, lines, margins, guides and masters change proportionally. <b>Scale</b>: <b>Fit the whole page (centered)</b>, <b>Fit to width</b> or <b>Fit to height</b>.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Keep sizes, move relative to edges</b></td><td>Sizes stay; an element sticks to the nearest edge or the center. Backgrounds and full-width or full-height strips stretch to the new page.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Change format only</b></td><td>Elements stay where they are.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ol class=\"kroki\"><li>The <b>Now → After change</b> preview shows the first page before and after. Below it are notes, e.g. how many elements will end up outside the page.</li><li><span class=\"ui\">Change format</span>. You undo the whole change with a single <span class=\"ui\">Undo</span> (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd>).</li></ol><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-format.png\" alt=\"Format change with before and after preview\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Format change with before and after preview</figcaption></figure><p>A cover with a spine (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"10.6\">Cover with a spine</a>) recalculates itself after the interior format changes. Elements that stick out beyond the page after the change are shown by <b>Preflight check</b> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"20\">Preflight check</a>).</p>"},{"id":"10.10","tytul":"Ready-made sections (document templates)","html":"<p>You insert a whole ready-made document with one click as a new section: a wall calendar, a 365-day planner, a notebook, an invitation, certificates for a list of people, business cards, a flyer, a menu, a book chapter and over 30 more. Where: at the bottom of the page panel the <span class=\"ui\">Ready-made</span> button (next to <span class=\"ui\">Section</span>) and in the Library the <b>Ready-made sections</b> category (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"4.8\">Library</a>). Whole-project templates (a book with a cover and endpapers, a brochure…) are separate — when you create a new project (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"2.2\">New project</a>).</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-gotowe-sekcje.png\" alt=\"Ready-made sections – template gallery\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Ready-made sections – template gallery</figcaption></figure><ol class=\"kroki\"><li>Choose a kind at the top (<b>Book</b>, <b>Calendars</b>, <b>Planners and notebooks</b>, <b>Invitations and certificates</b>, <b>Business and advertising</b>, <b>Other</b>) or type a word in <b>Search</b>. The thumbnail shows the first page in your project's format, and below it you can see how many pages will be created. The <b>with data</b> badge – the pages take their content from a table (e.g. 365 days, people for certificates).</li><li>Click a template. You'll see a preview of the first pages and the settings: - the template's questions, e.g. the calendar's <b>Year</b> or the notebook's <b>Number of pages</b>, - <b>Colors</b>: brand colors (if you have a brand kit, <span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"27.9\">Brand kit</a>), the template's colors or one of the ready-made palettes, - templates with their own format (business card 85 × 55 mm, landscape A4 certificate, 70 × 40 mm label) have a format switch – uncheck it so the template fits the section format.</li><li><span class=\"ui\">Insert as new section</span>. The section goes after the current one, and the editor moves to its first page.</li></ol><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-gotowe-szczegoly.png\" alt=\"Template details – preview, year and colors\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Template details – preview, year and colors</figcaption></figure><p>A template isn't a frozen file: it is built for your format, margins and bleed, in your brand's colors and fonts. Once inserted, it's ordinary pages, masters (e.g. page number), styles and a data table with a series (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"14\">Data tables</a>, <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"16\">Series</a>) – you can change everything in them as in your own. Grey boxes are placeholders for photos: insert an image into them (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"7.7\">Image in a shape</a>). A single <span class=\"ui\">Undo</span> (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd>) removes the whole inserted section.</p>"},{"id":"10.11","tytul":"Special sections: dust jacket, flyer, calendars, die lines, imposition","html":"<p>A special section works like a cover with a spine (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"10.6\">Cover with a spine</a>): it knows what the printed thing is. It calculates its own format, labels zones on the page (panels, flaps), draws fold lines (creases, dashed), perforation lines (dotted) and cut lines (solid pink line), warns about text on a fold and elements beyond the cut line, and in the PDF gives a sheet in full size. You choose it as the <b>Section type</b> with <span class=\"ui\">Section</span> or in the section settings (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"10.4\">Section settings</a>). The diagram in the window shows zones and folds live, with the sheet dimensions below.</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-sekcja-ulotka.png\" alt=\"Folded flyer in the section settings\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Folded flyer in the section settings</figcaption></figure><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>What it calculates</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Front / back endpaper</b></td><td>Hardcover: a sheet two book-block pages wide, with a fold in the middle. The <b>On the cover (glued)</b> half is glued to the inside of the cover, the other is the first (front) or last (back) loose leaf of the book. <b>Printing</b>: blank, printed visible side or both sides; <b>Paper</b> (g/m²) — the loose leaf adds to the spine thickness. An endpaper has no page numbers.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Dust jacket with flaps</b></td><td>Back, spine and front from the project's hard cover (boards and spine), two flaps (<b>Flap</b>, 0 – about half the front) and an allowance for the fold at the board. If pages are added, the jacket recalculates itself and the front elements move with it.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Folded flyer</b></td><td>A sheet for 2, 3 or 4 panels, <b>Fold</b>: <b>Roll fold</b> (C-fold – the panel folded inward is narrower by the <b>Reduction</b> so it doesn't buckle) or <b>Accordion</b> (Z – equal panels). The section has two pages: outside (with the <b>Cover</b> panel) and inside.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Calendar with dates (year)</b></td><td><b>Year</b>, <b>One page per</b> month, week or day, first day of the week and <b>Holidays</b> (Poland: fixed and movable – Easter, Pentecost, Corpus Christi). Creates a data table and a series: the first page of the section is repeated for each month, week or day. Changing the year recalculates everything.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Three-month calendar</b></td><td>A header, three calendar pads one below the other, a window on the middle one and an advertising strip – with a crease under the header and perforations.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Die line</b></td><td><b>Envelope</b> (C6, DL, C5, square), <b>Presentation folder</b> with a pocket and business card slits, or <b>Box</b> (length, width, height → a net with a lid, bottom, flaps and a glue tab). <b>Printing on both sides</b> adds a second page – the inside of the sheet: zones, folds and the cut line are mirrored horizontally on it, as they'll fall when the sheet is turned over, so you design the inside of a box or envelope in the right place. Die lines go to the PDF only from the first page; the 3D mockup shows the inside.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Imposition sheet</b></td><td>Stickers, business cards, labels: you design one item (or a series of them from data), and in the PDF they stand side by side on a sheet (SRA3, A3, A4, B2) with crop marks. <b>Cutting</b>: rectangle (guillotine), <b>sticker</b> with rounded corners, <b>round / oval sticker</b> (a circle or oval within the item's size) or <b>tag with a hole</b> – clipped top corners (the clip in the rounding field) and a Ø 5 mm hole for string; the preflight check warns about elements in the hole and outside the die cut. Ready-made template: <b>Gift tags</b> (to whom, wishes, from whom – from a table).</td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-sekcja-wykrojnik.png\" alt=\"Box die line on the page: zones, folds and cut line\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Box die line on the page: zones, folds and cut line</figcaption></figure><p><b>Calendar-with-dates fields</b> to insert into text (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"15\">Fields in text</a>): <code>{data}</code>, <code>{dzien}</code>, <code>{miesiac}</code>, <code>{dzien_tygodnia}</code>, <code>{tydzien}</code>, <code>{swieto}</code>; the <code>wolne</code> column has the value “tak” on Sundays and holidays – use it in a data-driven color (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"17\">Appearance from data</a>) to make holidays red. Your own columns added to this table stay after the year changes.</p><p><b>In the PDF</b> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"21\">Export to PDF and PNG</a>): the die cut line is a separate spot color <b>CutContour</b>, folds – <b>Crease</b>, perforations – <b>Perforation</b>: the printer doesn't print them, only cuts, creases and perforates. For the dust jacket, flyer and three-month calendar (cut straight), folds and perforations are marked with ticks outside the format, next to the crop marks. An imposition sheet is laid out automatically when the export has <b>Sheet layout: Regular</b>. Notes about text on a fold and elements beyond the cut line are also shown by <b>Preflight check</b> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"20\">Preflight check</a>).</p><p>The ready-made sections (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"10.10\">Ready-made sections (document templates)</a>) <b>Folded brochure</b>, <b>Business cards</b> and <b>Labels and stickers</b> are inserted right away as a folded flyer and an imposition sheet, and a hardcover <b>Cover</b> — together with two blank endpapers.</p><p><b>A hardcover book</b> consists of sections of these types: <b>Cover</b> (hardcover binding), <b>Front endpaper</b>, <b>Block</b>, optionally an <b>Insert</b> (e.g. photos on coated paper — with its own paper, without page numbers) and <b>Back endpaper</b>. The cover spine is calculated from the block and adds the loose leaves of the endpapers and the leaves of the inserts, each with its own paper. <b>Preflight check</b> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"20\">Preflight check</a>) warns when a hard cover has no endpapers (the <span class=\"ui\">Add endpapers</span> button adds both, blank), and when an endpaper is used with a softcover. A hard cover can also have a <b>Bookmark – ribbon in the spine</b> in a chosen color. In the PDF export (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"21\">Export to PDF and PNG</a>), <b>Files for the printer: cover, endpapers, book block…</b> gives a separate file for each part: the cover, dust jacket, endpapers, block, inserts and each separate print.</p><p>A section from an older project that has “wyklejka” (endpaper) in its name but a regular type gets a one-time hint “Section … looks like an endpaper – set the type…?” with a <span class=\"ui\">Set type</span> button. It's only a suggestion — without a click the section stays regular.</p>"},{"id":"10.12","tytul":"Paper models and cut-outs","html":"<p>A model made of paper or card from many parts – a little house, a box with dividers, a solid, a toy – you make in a section of the <b>Paper model / cut-out</b> type. Each page of this section is one <b>sheet</b> with parts. In the section settings:</p><ul class=\"punkty\"><li><b>Sheet</b> – A4, A3, A5, Letter (home printer) or <b>Custom</b> (print shop sheet, plotter), portrait or landscape;</li><li><b>Material</b> – paper or card with a <b>paper weight</b> (thickness is calculated automatically: 300 g/m² card ≈ 0.39 mm), board or a custom thickness in millimeters; the width of slots depends on the thickness;</li><li><b>Printing and cutting</b> – <b>home printer</b> (cut and fold lines are printed – you cut with scissors and a craft knife) or <b>print shop with plotter</b> (the lines go to the PDF as separate colors: cut, folds and glue – the print shop doesn't print them); <b>printer margin</b> – the strip along the edge that the printer can't print on;</li><li><b>Back of the sheet</b> – unused, also printed, or <b>glue areas only</b> (unprinted).</li></ul><p>The section margins equal the printer margin, and there is no bleed – the model is cut along the outline of the parts.</p><p>In this section the insert bar has extra tools:</p><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span class=\"ui\">Template</span></td><td>A ready-made model from dimensions: <b>Box with a fitted lid</b> (two parts – the lid is larger by two material thicknesses and 0.5 mm of play, so it fits snugly but without forcing), <b>Box with a tuck-in flap</b> (a hinged lid with a tongue tucked behind the front wall – opens and closes without glue), <b>Box with a snap lock</b> (the lid's tongue goes into a slot in the front wall, as wide as the material thickness + 0.2 mm), <b>Rotating wheel</b> for books (two discs on a rivet – the top one with a window onto one of the bottom one's 3–12 fields; the window and the cross for the rivet are cut lines), <b>Slider</b> for books (a sheet with a slit near the edge and a T-shaped strip – the narrow part goes through the slit, the wide end stays under the sheet and keeps the strip from being pulled out; parameters: strip width and travel), <b>Pillow box</b> (two walls with arcs at the ends: a convex cut arc and a concave <b>curved fold</b> – once the ends are pushed in, the box closes itself; a curved fold prints as a crease along the curve, and the 3D mockup shows it flat because card bends springily along a curve), <b>Flaps</b> for books (lift-the-flap: 1–6 windows cut on three sides and folded on the fourth – the fold works as a hinge; a second sheet underneath for hidden pictures, glue only outside the windows), <b>Tray</b>, <b>Cuboid</b>, <b>Prism</b> (3–12 sides), <b>Pyramid</b>, <b>Cross-slotted dividers</b> (shorter by two thicknesses, lower by one, with slots of thickness + 0.2 mm), solids with curved walls – <b>Cylinder</b>, <b>Cone</b>, <b>Truncated cone</b> (cup, lampshade) and <b>Sphere from gores</b> (globe, 4–24 gores – each glued with tabs to the next), <b>Vase</b> (a solid of revolution from a profile, 6–32 segments – wall strips and a flat bottom), ready-made models <b>House with a gable roof</b>, <b>Car</b> and <b>Turtle</b> (a shell from segments, head, legs and tail – glued under the shell) (solids from a silhouette unfolded automatically, as in <span class=\"ui\">3D model</span>): the lateral surface unrolled exactly (the sector angle calculated from height and diameters), bottom and lid from many short sides, tabs along the arc with one number (a seam – the check verifies that both sides have the same length) – <b>Pop-up card with a V-fold</b> (a triangle glued to two slanted fields at the same angle to the spine as its edges – it lies flat on the open card, stands up when closing and hides inside; angle 15–75°), <b>Pop-up card with a platform</b> (a card and a strip glued with tabs to fields on both sides of the spine: when opened it stands like a box, when closed it lies flat inside; <b>Layers: 2</b> glues a second, smaller platform on top of it) and <b>Pop-up card (cut-out)</b>: a single sheet with slits and folds from which boxes pop up when the card is opened at a right angle (1–4, depth and height to choose; the slits end before the edge, so nothing sticks out when closed); the <b>3D mockup</b> shows it open at a right angle with the boxes popped up). Parts have folds, tabs and pair numbers; the window states the section's material thickness.</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ui\">Part</span></td><td>A new part – a 60 × 40 mm rectangle with a letter (A, B, C…).</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ui\">From shape</span></td><td>Turns a selected rectangle, polygon, ellipse or pen shape (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"6\">Shapes, lines and colors</a>) into a part – any outline. Pen curves stay curves (densely, about every 2 mm), so round shapes cut smoothly.</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ui\">Solid from shape</span></td><td>The selected shape is the base of a solid: a <b>prism</b> (walls perpendicular at a chosen height, lid at the first wall), a <b>pyramid</b> (walls up to an apex above the center of the base), or <b>walls with a roof</b> – two joined solids: walls with a bottom and a separate roof (a pyramid without a base, fanned out around the peak, with a <b>Roof height</b>); the tabs at the top of the walls have the same pair numbers as the bottom edges of the roof, so the roof is glued onto the walls like on a house. One part with a net is created – a wall on each side, tabs with pair numbers and folds. It works with any polygon, including a concave one (star, letter L) – with a concave one, check that the walls on the net don't overlap.</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ui\">3D model</span></td><td>A solid from a 3D program or the web (<b>OBJ</b> or <b>STL</b>) unfolded flat – as in paper-model programs: Makietka splits the faces into triangles, unfolds them along the flattest edges (flat faces stay whole, without lines), adds mountain and valley folds with an angle, and tabs with pair numbers. When a part would overlap itself, further faces go into the next part. You give the model's largest dimension in mm; then <span class=\"ui\">Lay out</span> arranges the parts on sheets. Models of up to a few hundred faces work best (in Blender: Decimate). You can add an <b>MTL</b> file to an OBJ (select both at once) – each part gets the color of the material that takes up most of it; without MTL, materials get colors from the palette. When the model has a <b>texture</b> (UV coordinates in the OBJ and a <code>map_Kd</code> image in the MTL), select the image file (PNG, JPG or WebP) together with them – each part gets a print: the image transferred onto its faces exactly as it lies on the model. The print goes to <b>Project images</b> (“… – nadruk.png”) and moves together with the part on <span class=\"ui\">Lay out</span>; after <span class=\"ui\">Split</span> and <span class=\"ui\">Merge</span> it disappears (it covered the whole part). When the image is missing, Makietka tells you which file it is looking for.</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ui\">Fold</span></td><td>Two clicks on the part's edges: a fold line divides it into faces. A line along the edge itself or outside the part isn't added.</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ui\">Join</span></td><td>Click near the edge that will get a <b>glue tab</b>, then near the edge of another part (or the same one) it is glued to – both get <b>the same number</b>. A second click in the middle of a face instead of near an edge adds a <b>glue area</b> the length of the edge there. When the edges have different lengths, the app tells you.</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ui\">Glue</span></td><td>A click in a part adds a glue area (with <kbd>Shift</kbd> – on the underside, unprinted, when the back of the sheet is for glue).</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ui\">Glue line</span></td><td>Gluing isn't always at an edge: a helper line <b>anywhere on a face</b> – it doesn't have to reach an edge or be closed. With nothing selected: two clicks in the part (start and end). Draw a curve, wave or polyline on the part with a line, arc or the pen, select it and use <span class=\"ui\">Glue line</span> – it turns into a glue line (a closed shape, e.g. a circle, too). Then <span class=\"ui\">Join</span>: an edge or tab of another part, then the line – they get the same number. A <b>straight</b> line: the attached part stands at an angle (it folds on the line); a <b>curved</b> one: it bends and wraps along it. The line prints as a green stroke with a number, and in the file for the printer it goes to the glue plate; in the part panel (<b>Glue lines</b>) you see its length, change its number or move it to the underside. <b>Preflight check</b> compares the length of the edge and the line, and the assembly instructions say whether the attached part stands at an angle or wraps along a curve.</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ui\">Slit</span></td><td>A cut line inside a part or from its edge, <b>at any angle</b> – two clicks (the ends snap to vertices, fold ends and other slits, up to 1.5 mm). Curves and polylines: draw a line, arc or pen shape on the part, select it and use <span class=\"ui\">Slit</span> – one slit made of many segments is created. Slits together with folds divide a face: e.g. two slanted slits from the ends of a fold give a triangular flap that tilts on its fold in the 3D mockup (pop-up cut-out). A slit is printed on the cut plate together with the outline; in the part panel (<b>Slits</b>) you see its length and can delete it. <b>Preflight check</b> warns when a slit goes outside the part. A slot (<span class=\"ui\">Slot</span>) is something else – a narrow opening with a width for a tab.</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ui\">Cutout</span></td><td>A hole in a part: draw a closed shape on it (rectangle, ellipse, polygon, pen shape), select it and use <span class=\"ui\">Cutout</span>. The piece inside falls out – in the editor it has grey hatching (it's waste), in the 3D mockup there's a hole in the face. <b>A window with a door</b>: instead of a cutout, make a <span class=\"ui\">Slit</span> on three sides and fold the fourth side (<span class=\"ui\">Fold</span> or slits from the ends of a fold) – the door swings on its hinge and reveals the opening.</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ui\">Onto part</span></td><td>A face isn't just a color: select a text, image, shape or ornament lying on a part and use <span class=\"ui\">Onto part</span> – the element goes onto the face and from then on is part of the part: it moves and rotates with it, stays in place when you change folds and tabs, travels with it on <span class=\"ui\">Lay out</span> (elements lying wholly on a part are placed on it by <span class=\"ui\">Lay out</span> itself), on <span class=\"ui\">Split</span> goes to the piece it lies on, and in the 3D mockup sits on its face and folds with it. Face artwork is <b>clipped to the part's outline</b> – whatever sticks out doesn't print beyond the cut line or on the tabs. The part panel → <b>On faces</b> shows how many elements are on the faces; <b>Take out of part (to edit)</b> returns them to the page in the same place – e.g. to correct a text – then <span class=\"ui\">Onto part</span> again.</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ui\">Lay out</span></td><td>All the section's parts are arranged anew on the sheets – in rows within the print area (without the printer margin), tallest first; a part that doesn't fit horizontally is rotated 90°; missing sheets are added at the end of the section (with double-sided printing, parts go on the first page of the sheet). Other page elements stay. A part too big even after rotating stays in place – the app tells you.</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ui\">Instructions</span></td><td>Assembly instructions from the model: what to print (material, paper weight), which parts to cut out (with sheet numbers), how many mountain and valley folds each part has, then gluing the pairs in order – “Put glue on tab 3 (part A) and stick it under edge 4 of part B with number 3”, “Slide tab 7 into slot 7”. <span class=\"ui\">Download (.txt)</span> or <span class=\"ui\">Insert as page</span> – a new A4 section after the sheets with numbered steps (editable like ordinary text).</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ui\">Slot</span></td><td>Two clicks in a part: a slit for a tab or divider – the width is the material thickness + 0.2 mm of play.</td></tr><tr><td>Split (in the part panel)</td><td>Two clicks on the edges divide the part in two – e.g. when after <span class=\"ui\">3D model</span> a part doesn't fit on the sheet. Edges keep their tabs and numbers, folds go to their part (those crossing the line are trimmed to it), and a new pair is created on the cut line: a tab on one part, an edge to glue on the other.</td></tr><tr><td>Merge (in the part panel)</td><td>A click near an edge with a pair number joins both parts into one: the second rotates and moves so the pair's edges coincide, and the glue edge becomes a fold (mountain, 90°). When the parts would overlap, Makietka refuses – they stay separate.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><kbd>Esc</kbd> ends the tool. You move and rotate a part like any element; you can't stretch it (its dimensions are the model's dimensions). A selected part has in the <b>Properties</b> panel:</p><ul class=\"punkty\"><li><b>Part</b> – its name (letter), face color, the <b>Pair numbers and part name</b> switch and tool buttons;</li><li><b>Edges</b> – each edge with its length and kind: <b>Cutting</b>, <b>Glue tab</b> (with width), <b>To be glued (no tab)</b> or <b>Tab into slot</b> (with length) – and a <b>pair number</b>;</li><li><b>Folds</b> – <b>mountain</b> (away from you, dash-dot – the print stays on the outside; template folds and new folds are like this) or <b>valley</b> (towards you, dashes) and <b>angle when folded</b> – the angle between faces in the finished model (box 90°, walls of a hexagonal prism 120°), and in a pop-up card the <b>Card spine (pop-up)</b> switch;</li><li><b>Glue areas</b> and <b>Slots</b> – pair number, dimensions, on the underside, deleting.</li></ul><p>On the sheet, tabs are light grey with a pair number, and the same number stands by the edge or at the spot they are glued to – after cutting out you know what to glue to what. The plotter file (menu <b>⋯</b>) and the PDF for the printer get the cut outline of the parts with tabs and slots, folds (also at the base of each tab) and glue areas.</p><p><b>What joins what.</b> When nothing is selected, the page panel in a paper-model section has a <b>Connections</b> list: each number with what carries it, e.g. “1 – A: tab 2 ↔ B: edge 4”, and with several sheets also the sheet number. Hovering over an item highlights both sides of the pair on the sheet in orange, clicking selects the part. A number without a pair or used more than twice has a warning bar. Hovering the mouse over a part highlights all its pairs.</p><p><b>Preflight check</b> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"20\">Preflight check</a>) watches over the model: a part sticking out of the sheet or into the printer margin, parts overlapping each other including tabs (they can't be cut out), a number without a pair (you can't tell what to glue it to), a number used more than twice, a pair of edges of different lengths, a glue area smaller than a tab, a slot narrower than the material or shorter than the tab, and in a pop-up card – a part that sticks out of the card when it's closed (by how many millimeters), and a card that doesn't close flat because a platform strip or V arm doesn't match the distance between glue areas. For each note, <span class=\"ui\">Open template</span> selects the part.</p><p>The <b>3D mockup</b> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"19.4\">3D mockup</a>) has a <b>Paper model (from parts)</b> model for paper-model sections: first the sheets lie flat, print side up, then each part folds along its folds – with the print from the sheet on its faces and card on the inside (<b>Folded parts</b> – they stand side by side as they lie on the sheets). The last position, <b>Glued model</b>, glues the parts in pairs into one solid – starting from the largest part, the next ones come to where they should be glued: edge to edge (rotated so that all pairs of that part match; with no other pairs – at the <b>Angle after gluing</b> from the edge panel: 180° flat, like an extension, 90° perpendicular), edge to a straight glue line, glue area or slot – the part stands perpendicular on the print side, edge to a curved glue line – the part bends and wraps along the curve, keeping its folds (e.g. a strip's visor stays folded). Ornaments, texts and images on the faces travel with them. Parts with no pairs to the rest stay on the sheet.</p><p><b>Pop-up in motion.</b> When a card's fold has <b>Card spine (pop-up)</b> turned on in the panel (pop-up templates have it right away), an <b>Opening the pop-up card</b> slider (0–180°) appears in the mockup window. The card opens around the spine, and the attached platforms, V-folds, second layers and cut-outs move as in a real book: faces are rigid, folds act as hinges, and glue areas hold the attached parts. At 180° everything lies flat, at 90° the elements stand up, and with the card almost closed they fold inward. The setting is saved in the shot.</p><p><b>Angled elements from any part.</b> You don't need the V template: on a card with a spine, draw two straight <b>glue lines</b> on both sides of the spine (slanted to it) and join the edges of any part to them – in the mockup the part stands at the angle that follows from the geometry. The card panel shows the <b>angle to spine</b> and the distance from it for each straight line, and the panel of the attached part has a <b>Pop-up</b> section: at what angle it stands with the card open at 90°, how far it reaches from the card, and whether it hides inside the card when closed or sticks out (by how many millimeters).</p>"},{"id":"10.13","tytul":"Comic","html":"<p>A comic is an add-on to an ordinary publication: a booklet, a book, loose sheets or a file for viewing on screen. Everything works without AI; AI (marked with a star ✦) can help write the script and draw the panels.</p><p><b>How to start</b></p><ul class=\"punkty\"><li><b>From a template:</b> <b>New project</b> → <b>Start from: Template</b> → <b>Comic</b>. You get pages with ready panels, a sample script, cards for two characters (Ala and Ola) and a place (Small town).</li><li><b>From a blank page:</b> <b>New project</b> → <b>Start from: Blank page</b> → tick <b>It is a comic – panels, bubbles, script and characters</b>.</li><li><b>In an existing project:</b> <span class=\"ui\">Section</span> → type <b>Comic</b> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"10.4\">Section settings</a>).</li></ul><p>In the template you choose the comic's <b>Form</b>:</p><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>What you get</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Booklet with a cover – for print</b></td><td>A booklet cover (with room for a title and a drawing) and the comic pages, saddle-stitched.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Paperback book – for print</b></td><td>A cover with a spine and the comic pages.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Loose sheets – no cover</b></td><td>Just the comic pages, e.g. for printing at home.</td></tr><tr><td><b>On screen – PDF or presentation (landscape)</b></td><td>Landscape pages like slides, without bleed – for viewing in the preview or as an on-screen PDF.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-komiks.png\" alt=\"A comic page with panels and bubbles; Panels, Speech bubble and Comic on the bar\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>A comic page with panels and bubbles; Panels, Speech bubble and Comic on the bar</figcaption></figure><p><b>The comic bar.</b> On a comic page the insert bar has extra buttons:</p><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Button</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span class=\"ui\">Panels</span></td><td>A ready panel layout for this page, the gap between panels, bleed; in the same window <span class=\"ui\">Split panel</span>.</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ui\">Speech bubble ▾</span></td><td>A menu: a <b>Speech</b>, <b>Thought</b>, <b>Shout</b>, <b>Whisper</b> or <b>Caption</b> bubble (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"4.14\">Speech bubble</a>), <b>Sound effect</b> (a big outlined “BOOM!” lettering) and <b>Bridge</b> (joins two selected bubbles of one character).</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ui\">Comic</span></td><td>The <b>Comic step by step</b> window – the whole comic in five steps (described below).</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The comic AI tools – <b>Script with AI</b>, <b>Reference pictures of characters and places</b>, <b>Draw panels</b> – are in the <span class=\"ui\">AI ▾</span> menu (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"7.11\">AI image</a>), and the chat with the AI – under <span class=\"ui\">AI assistant</span> on the top bar (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"25.6\">AI Assistant in Makietka (own key)</a>). When you have no Google Gemini key, the drawing and writing items are greyed out, and at the top of the menu there is <b>You have no key for generating pictures and texts – add a key…</b> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"7.11\">AI image</a>).</p><p><b>Comic in the Library.</b> <span class=\"ui\">Library</span> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"4.8\">Library</a>) has a <b>Comic</b> category: bubbles of every kind and the sound effect, ready panel layouts (a click replaces the panels on this page; pictures and bubbles stay), halftone and motion lines, and on a comic page also <b>Comic windows</b>: <b>Comic step by step</b>, <b>Script</b>, <b>Characters, places and props</b>, <b>Upload ready pages</b> and <b>Webtoon – for reading on a phone</b>. It is a good way on a phone, where the bar is shorter.</p><h4>Comic step by step</h4><p><span class=\"ui\">Comic</span> opens a window with five steps. Each step shows what is already there (e.g. “Done: 5 lines on 2 pages.”). Do them in order or go back to any of them – each button opens its own window.</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-komiks-okno.png\" alt=\"The Comic step by step window\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>The Comic step by step window</figcaption></figure><ol class=\"kroki\"><li><b>Story</b> – <span class=\"ui\">Open the script</span> (the <b>Script</b> window, below) or <span class=\"ui\">Write with AI…</span> (Script with AI, below).</li><li><b>Characters, places and props</b> – <span class=\"ui\">Characters, places and props…</span>: character, place and prop cards and the <b>Drawing style</b>. The thumbnails show the reference images.</li><li><b>Panels and images</b> – <span class=\"ui\">Panel layout…</span>, <span class=\"ui\">Draw this page's panels…</span> (AI) or <span class=\"ui\">Upload finished pages…</span>.</li><li><b>Speech bubbles</b> – <span class=\"ui\">Place bubbles</span>: texts from the script go into bubbles in the panels.</li><li><b>Finished comic</b> – <span class=\"ui\">Preview</span>, <span class=\"ui\">PDF…</span> (for print or for the screen) and <span class=\"ui\">Webtoon</span> (a strip for reading on a phone).</li></ol><h4>Step 1. Script</h4><p>The <b>Script</b> window shows the comic page by page: panels in each page, lines in each panel. You don't have to type numbers or remember column names.</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-komiks-scenariusz.png\" alt=\"The Script window: page, panels, shot, place, who is in the panel and the lines\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>The Script window: page, panels, shot, place, who is in the panel and the lines</figcaption></figure><p>In each panel:</p><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What to choose or type</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Shot</b></td><td>How the scene is seen: <b>wide shot</b> (the whole place), <b>full shot</b> (characters from head to toe), <b>medium shot</b> (from the waist up), <b>close-up</b> (the face), <b>detail</b> (an important object), <b>from above</b>, <b>from below</b>, <b>over the shoulder</b>. <b>Shot: any</b> – no requirements.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Place</b></td><td>A place from the cards (step 2) – its description and image become the panel's background.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Characters in the panel</b></td><td>Tick the characters visible in the panel, including those who say nothing (those who speak are ticked automatically). For each character in the panel choose an <b>emotion</b> (joy, sadness, anger, fear, surprise, curiosity, calm, tiredness) and type <b>what they are doing</b>, e.g. “running, waving”. The AI then draws the character with that expression and in that motion.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Props in the panel</b></td><td>Objects from the prop cards visible in the panel (e.g. <b>Parcel</b>). Their description and reference image go to the AI.</td></tr><tr><td><b>What the panel shows</b></td><td>Briefly, e.g. “Ala runs down the lane, houses behind her”. It does not go on the page – it is a description for the artist or the AI.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Lines</b></td><td><span class=\"ui\">+ Line</span>: <b>Who speaks</b> (a character from the cards or <b>Caption</b>), <b>Bubble kind</b> (speech, thought, shout, whisper) and <b>Bubble text</b>. The bin icon deletes the line.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><span class=\"ui\">+ Panel</span> adds a panel on the page, <span class=\"ui\">+ Page</span> – a new script page; the bin icon next to a panel deletes the panel. <span class=\"ui\">Save</span> saves the script. You add characters and places in step 2 – then you pick them here from a list.</p><p><b>For advanced users.</b> The script is an ordinary <b>Scenariusz</b> (script) data table (one row per line, columns <b>strona</b> (page), <b>kadr</b> (panel), <b>ujęcie</b> (shot), <b>miejsce</b> (place), <b>w kadrze</b> (in the panel), <b>opis sceny</b> (scene), <b>postać</b> (character), <b>tekst</b> (text), <b>rodzaj</b> (kind)). <span class=\"ui\">Table</span> in the Script window opens it in the <b>Data</b> window (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"14\">Data tables</a>) – there you can paste a script from a spreadsheet (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"14.3\">Editing data in the app</a>) or add columns with other languages. Page 1 is the first page of the comic (the cover does not count).</p><p><b>Script with AI.</b> <span class=\"ui\">Write with AI…</span> in step 1 (or <span class=\"ui\">AI ▾</span> → <b>Script with AI</b>) writes the comic out for you – including the characters' emotions and actions:</p><ol class=\"kroki\"><li>Choose <b>From an idea</b> and describe the story in 3–5 sentences, e.g. “Two friends, Ala and Ola, find a parcel at their door. Inside there is a kitten…”, and set <b>How many pages</b>. Or choose <b>From a document</b> and a file with a script or story: Word (.docx), LibreOffice (.odt), Markdown (.md) or plain text (.txt). From Google Docs: <b>File → Download → Microsoft Word</b>. Dialogue from the document is kept word for word.</li><li>Click <span class=\"ui\">Write it out</span>. After a few seconds you see a preview: pages, panels, who speaks, text, what the panel shows.</li><li><span class=\"ui\">Save script</span> – the script goes into the <b>Script</b> window (with shots, places and characters in the panels), character and place cards are created (existing cards are only completed), and pages without panels get panels. <span class=\"ui\">Back</span> returns to the description without saving.</li></ol><p>Cost – about 0.01 PLN; you see it before writing it out.</p><h4>Step 2. Characters, places and props</h4><p><span class=\"ui\">Characters, places and props…</span> opens the cards. Describe a character once – the description and reference image make sure it looks the same in every panel (for you, an artist or the AI).</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-komiks-postacie.png\" alt=\"Character and place cards\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Character and place cards</figcaption></figure><ul class=\"punkty\"><li><b>Character:</b> <b>Name</b>, <b>Appearance</b> (e.g. “8 years old, red braids, green dress”), <b>Distinctive feature</b> (e.g. “yellow backpack”), <b>Personality</b>, <b>Reference image</b> and <b>Bubble colour</b> (this character's bubbles get this background colour; white – a normal bubble).</li><li><b>Place:</b> <b>Place</b> (name), <b>Description</b>, <b>Time of day</b>, <b>Light</b> and <b>Reference image</b> – a view of the place itself, without characters. In panels with this place (chosen in the script) the place's image is the background, and the characters stand on it.</li><li><b>Reference image:</b> <span class=\"ui\">Choose image…</span> – a drawing or photo from your device; or <span class=\"ui\">Draw from description (AI)</span> – AI draws it from the card fields in the comic's style (you see the cost before drawing, about 0.14 PLN). A character – the whole figure on a white background; a place – a wide view without people.</li><li><b>Prop:</b> an object that comes back in many panels (a backpack, a kite, a map) – <b>Prop</b> (name), <b>Description</b>, <b>Whose (character)</b> and <b>Reference image</b> (the object alone on a white background). In the script you tick it in the panel (<b>Props in the panel</b>); a prop mentioned in the scene description is used by the AI even without ticking. The <b>Comic</b> template has a sample <b>Parcel</b>.</li><li><b>Drawing style</b> (at the top of the window; each tile shows the same sample scene in that style): <b>Colourful storybook</b>, <b>Watercolour</b>, <b>Simple cartoon</b>, <b>Manga (black and white)</b>, <b>Retro comic</b>, <b>Pencil sketch</b>, <b>European comic (clear line)</b>, <b>Superhero comic</b>, <b>Anime (colour)</b> or <b>Paper cut-out</b> – it applies to all AI images in this comic.</li></ul><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-komiks-postac.png\" alt=\"Character card\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Character card</figcaption></figure><p>Clicking a card opens it for editing, <span class=\"ui\">Save</span> saves it, <span class=\"ui\">Delete</span> removes the card (bubbles and images on the pages stay). The cards are ordinary <b>Postacie</b> (characters) and <b>Miejsca</b> (places) tables – you also see them in the <b>Data</b> window.</p><h4>Step 3. Panels and images</h4><p><b>Panel layout.</b> <span class=\"ui\">Panels</span> on the bar (or <span class=\"ui\">Panel layout…</span> in step 3) shows 9 ready layouts (1–9 panels). Click a preview, set <b>Gap between panels (mm)</b> and, if you like, <b>Bleed</b> – panels at the edge then run off the page, up to the trim line. <span class=\"ui\">Lay out panels</span> replaces the panels on this page; images and bubbles stay. <span class=\"ui\">Split panel</span> in this window: click two points on the page – the line between them splits the panel in two (it can be diagonal). <kbd>Esc</kbd> ends.</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-komiks-kadry.png\" alt=\"The Panels window – 9 ready layouts\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>The Panels window – 9 ready layouts</figcaption></figure><p>A panel is a shape with a white fill and a black outline, numbered in reading order (rows from the top, left to right within a row – in the layers “Panel 1”, “Panel 2”…). Move and change it like any shape; put an image into a panel like into any shape (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"7.7\">Image in a shape</a>), e.g. by dragging a file onto the panel. A character can break out of the panel – then place the image above the panel.</p><p><b>Draw panels (AI).</b> Open a comic page and click <span class=\"ui\">Draw this page's panels…</span> in step 3 (or <span class=\"ui\">AI ▾</span> → <b>Draw panels</b>). You don't write instructions for the AI – Makietka builds them from the script and the cards:</p><ol class=\"kroki\"><li>The window shows each panel of the page: a thumbnail, a field with the description and a tick box. The description is in plain words, e.g. “Panel 2. Shot: full shot. Scene: Ala runs down the street. In the panel: Ala – 8 years old, red braids…, always with: yellow backpack, emotion: joy, doing: running. Place (background): Small town…”.</li><li>Edit the description if you like. Panels with an image are unticked – tick them to draw them again.</li><li>Choose the <b>Quality</b> and check the cost below the list.</li><li><span class=\"ui\">Draw</span> – the panels fill one by one (“Drawing 2 of 3…”). The reference images of the characters and the place go to the AI together with the description, so the characters and the background look the same in every panel. There is no lettering in the images – the text is in the bubbles.</li></ol><p><b>The whole page as one picture.</b> In the drawing window tick <b>The whole page as one picture – cheaper and more consistent; AI also draws the panel borders (the picture replaces the page's panels, bubbles stay)</b>. The AI gets the descriptions of all panels at once, the page's panel layout and the characters' reference pictures, and draws the whole page – with panel borders, one light and one line style. It costs as much as one picture (e.g. about 0.14 PLN in Draft instead of about 0.42 PLN for three panels). The picture covers the whole page with bleed, and the page's panels disappear (they are already in the picture). If you want to fix panels separately, draw panel by panel.</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-komiks-cala-strona.jpg\" alt=\"A whole comic page drawn as one picture (Draft) – before adding bubbles\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>A whole comic page drawn as one picture (Draft) – before adding bubbles</figcaption></figure><p><b>Draw again.</b> Don't like an image? Open <span class=\"ui\">Draw this page's panels…</span> again and tick that panel. The description field shows the description <b>the current image was made from</b> – change one word or draw again unchanged. <span class=\"ui\">Description from the script</span> goes back to a freshly built description (e.g. after changing the script or a card). Earlier images stay in <b>Project images</b> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"7.5\">The Project images window</a>). A small change in one panel (e.g. “Ala should have her eyes closed”) needs no redrawing: select the panel → <span class=\"ui\">AI ▾</span> → <b>Fix the picture in the frame…</b> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"7.11\">AI image</a>).</p><p><b>Pictures made elsewhere.</b> You don't have to draw in Makietka. Pictures from the free Gemini, from Flow, from another generator, or your own drawings and scans go in like this:</p><ol class=\"kroki\"><li>Open the comic page where the pictures should start.</li><li><b>Drag several files at once onto the page</b> (or a ZIP file) – or click <span class=\"ui\">Upload ready pictures…</span> in step 3 and choose the files.</li><li>The pictures go one by one into empty panels: from this page on, in reading order, files in name order. Panels that already have a picture are skipped. If there are not enough panels, Makietka tells you which files it did not insert.</li></ol><p>A single picture dropped onto a panel goes into that panel (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"7.7\">Image in a shape</a>). A number in the file name puts the picture in a specific place:</p><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>File name</th><th>Where it goes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>01.png</code>, <code>page 7.jpg</code>, <code>page_3.png</code></td><td>The whole page 1 (7, 3) with bleed. That page's panels disappear (they are already in the drawing); bubbles stay above the image.</td></tr><tr><td><code>s03-k2.png</code>, <code>p3_panel2.jpg</code></td><td>Panel 2 on page 3 – the image fills the panel (move it by double-clicking, <span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"7\">Images</a>).</td></tr><tr><td>any other, e.g. <code>Gemini_Generated_Image_….png</code></td><td>The next empty panel.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Pages count from the first comic page; missing pages are added at the end of the section.</p><p><b>Panel descriptions for free AI.</b> In the <b>Draw panels</b> window click <span class=\"ui\">Copy panel descriptions</span>. Makietka copies to the clipboard the descriptions of all panels on this page – with the drawing style and the request “no text and no speech bubbles”. Paste them into the free Gemini or Flow, download the pictures and drag them onto the page (above). Without a Google Gemini key the window is available for exactly this: <span class=\"ui\">Draw</span> is greyed out, but copying the descriptions works.</p><h4>Step 4. Speech bubbles</h4><p><span class=\"ui\">Place bubbles</span> puts bubbles with the script texts into the right panels, with the tail pointing down. Then:</p><ol class=\"kroki\"><li>Move the bubbles and tail ends to where they fit the drawing.</li><li>Fixing the script? Click <span class=\"ui\">Place bubbles</span> again – only the text changes; bubbles you moved stay where they are. A line deleted from the script removes its bubble.</li></ol><ul class=\"punkty\"><li>When a character speaks twice in a row in the same panel, the second bubble goes below the first and is joined to it with a <b>bridge</b>. By hand: select two bubbles (with <kbd>Shift</kbd>) → <span class=\"ui\">Speech bubble ▾</span> → <b>Bridge</b>; select a joined bubble and choose <b>Bridge</b> again – you unjoin them.</li><li>You can also insert a bubble by hand: <span class=\"ui\">Speech bubble ▾</span> → the bubble kind (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"4.14\">Speech bubble</a>). <b>Sound effect</b> is a big outlined “BOOM!” – change the text, enlarge, rotate.</li><li><b>Comic styles.</b> Bubbles use the <b>Dialogue</b> style (Comic Neue, capitals), effects the <b>Sound effect</b> style (Bangers, yellow with a black outline). Changing a style (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"5.4\">Styles</a>) changes all bubbles or effects at once. In a Ukrainian project both styles use the Pangolin font (with Cyrillic).</li><li><b>Language versions.</b> Add columns <code>tekst_en</code>, <code>tekst_uk</code>… to the script table (the <b>Data</b> window). <span class=\"ui\">Place bubbles</span> then asks which column to take the texts from – the same comic gets the texts in another language. AI can also translate a column: the <b>✦</b> button next to the column name (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"14.6\">Fill a column with AI</a>), e.g. “translate the tekst column into English”.</li></ul><h4>Step 5. Finished comic</h4><ul class=\"punkty\"><li><span class=\"ui\">Preview</span> – the whole comic page by page or in spreads (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"19\">Preview</a>).</li><li><span class=\"ui\">PDF…</span> – a file for the print shop or for the screen (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"21\">Export to PDF and PNG</a>). Save a comic in the <b>On screen</b> form as an on-screen PDF – you open it on a computer or phone like a presentation.</li><li><span class=\"ui\">Webtoon</span> – the comic pages one below another as images in a ZIP file, ready to upload to a platform for reading comics on a phone.</li></ul><p><b>Preflight check</b> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"20\">Preflight check</a>) watches the comic: bubble text smaller than 7 pt, a bubble going off the page or lying across two panels, a tail pointing outside the panels, and empty panels (no image). For a bubble warning, <span class=\"ui\">Open template</span> selects it on the page.</p><p><b>Halftone and speed lines.</b> In <span class=\"ui\">Library</span> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"4.8\">Library</a>), <b>Backgrounds and patterns</b> has comic patterns: <b>Comic halftone</b>, <b>Speed lines (radial)</b> – for a blast or surprise – and <b>Speed lines (horizontal)</b> – for speed. You can put the same pattern on a selected shape, e.g. a panel (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"6.5\">Fill</a>).</p><h4>Example: an AI comic from idea to PDF</h4><ol class=\"kroki\"><li><b>New project</b> → <b>Start from: Template</b> → <b>Comic</b>, form <b>Booklet with a cover – for print</b>, 8 pages.</li><li><span class=\"ui\">Comic</span> → step 1 <span class=\"ui\">Write with AI…</span> → <b>From an idea</b>: “Ala and Ola find a parcel with a kitten at their door and look for its owner”, <b>How many pages</b>: 3 → <span class=\"ui\">Write it out</span> → <span class=\"ui\">Save script</span>.</li><li>Step 1 <span class=\"ui\">Open the script</span> – look through the panels; e.g. in panel 1 of page 1 set the shot <b>wide shot</b> and the place <b>Small town</b>. <span class=\"ui\">Save</span>.</li><li>Step 2 <span class=\"ui\">Characters, places and props…</span> – choose the <b>Drawing style</b>, in each card <span class=\"ui\">Draw from description (AI)</span> → <span class=\"ui\">Save</span>.</li><li>Go to the first comic page. Step 3 <span class=\"ui\">Draw this page's panels…</span> → <span class=\"ui\">Draw</span>. Repeat on the following pages.</li><li>Step 4 <span class=\"ui\">Place bubbles</span> and move the bubbles to where they fit.</li><li>Step 5 <span class=\"ui\">PDF…</span>.</li></ol><p><b>What it costs.</b> 3 pages with 3 panels each and two characters in <b>Draft</b> quality: 2 reference images + 9 panels = 11 images, <b>about 1.50 PLN</b> (about $0.38) and a few minutes. The script – about 0.01 PLN. For 24 pages (about 85 images) expect about 12 PLN in draft; in <b>Detailed</b> quality roughly three times as much. Makietka shows the cost before every drawing.</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-komiks-ai-przyklad.png\" alt=\"Page 1 of the example comic drawn by AI (Draft), with bubbles from the script\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Page 1 of the example comic drawn by AI (Draft), with bubbles from the script</figcaption></figure><p class=\"ramka-instrukcji wskazowka\"><b>Tip.</b> You can make the panel images in any program – the text always stays in Makietka's bubbles, not in the image, so it is easy to fix and translate.</p>"}]},
  {"nr":"11","tytul":"Master pages","czesc":"Editor","ikona":"olowek","sekcje":[{"id":"11.1","tytul":"What a master is and when to use it","html":"<p>Some things repeat on many pages: <b>page number, header, footer, background, frame, writing lines</b>. Instead of inserting them on each page separately, you insert them once — as a <b>master</b>.</p><ul class=\"punkty\"><li>A master lies <b>under</b> the content of every page that has it. You can't select it on the page itself or move it — so it won't get shifted by accident.</li><li>You change it once, and <b>all</b> those pages change.</li><li>The <code>{page}</code> field in a master shows each page's own number: one master, and on the pages 1, 2, 3…</li><li>A page can have several masters at once, e.g. “Grid” under the content and “Page number” at the bottom.</li></ul><p>When a master, and when an ordinary element? If something should be <b>on many pages in the same place</b> — a master. If only on one page — an ordinary element.</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-wzorce-galeria.png\" alt=\"Master pages window: what it is and ready-made masters\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Master pages window: what it is and ready-made masters</figcaption></figure>"},{"id":"11.2","tytul":"Where the masters are","html":"<ul class=\"punkty\"><li><span class=\"ui\">Masters</span> under the page list (next to <span class=\"ui\">Section</span>) and on the editor's top bar — opens the <b>Master pages</b> window.</li><li>Page ⋯ menu → <span class=\"ui\">Masters of this page…</span> — goes to that page and opens the window.</li><li>Section ⋯ menu → <span class=\"ui\">Section masters…</span>.</li><li>Page properties (nothing selected) → the <b>Masters</b> part: which masters are on this page; as long as the project has no masters, there's a <span class=\"ui\">Choose a ready-made master…</span> button there.</li><li>Library → the <b>Master pages</b> category — the same ready-made masters as in the window.</li></ul><p>A page with a master on it has a small purple mark on its thumbnail; the tooltip on it says which masters they are.</p>"},{"id":"11.3","tytul":"Ready-made masters","html":"<p>Lower down in the <b>Master pages</b> window there is a gallery of ready-made masters with previews — in the diagram the master's elements are distinct, and the faint blocks are page content. Groups:</p><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Numbering</td><td>number at the bottom, by the outer edge, “Page 5 of 120”, in a circle, on a side bar, Roman</td></tr><tr><td>Headers and footers</td><td>section name, project and section, colored band, footer with contact details</td></tr><tr><td>Backgrounds and paper</td><td>lines, grid, dots, graph paper, exercise book with margin, colored background, watermark</td></tr><tr><td>Frames</td><td>in the margins, double, rounded, page border, corners</td></tr><tr><td>Calendar and planner</td><td>tab on the edge, day header, “Notes” column, week</td></tr><tr><td>Book</td><td>running head with chapter title, number and chapter at the top</td></tr><tr><td>Catalog and offer, Newsletter, Invitation</td><td>logo and contact details, masthead, columns, decorative frame, bands</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Clicking a card adds the master to the project and <b>right away to all pages of the section</b> of the open page — the window closes, and the master is visible on the page. <span class=\"ui\">Undo</span> in the message or <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd> reverses it in one step. Ready-made masters adapt to the section's format and margins, and take colors and fonts from the brand kit (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"27.9\">Brand kit</a>) if you have one. Once added, it's an ordinary master — you can change it.</p>"},{"id":"11.4","tytul":"Which pages a master is on","html":"<p>In the window, in the <b>In this project</b> part, next to each master:</p><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Button</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span class=\"ui\">+ Add to this page</span></td><td>Turns the master on only on the open page. Clicking again (✓) removes it.</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ui\">+ To whole section</span></td><td>Turns the master on for the section's pages that don't have their own choice.</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ui\">Edit</span></td><td>Opens the master for editing (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"11.5\">Your own master and editing</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ui\">⋯</span></td><td><span class=\"ui\">Rename</span>, <span class=\"ui\">Duplicate</span>, <span class=\"ui\">Move up</span>, <span class=\"ui\">Move down</span>, <span class=\"ui\">Delete master</span>.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>You can also choose masters for a single page in the page properties: <b>Masters on this page</b> → <b>As in section</b> or <b>Custom selection</b> and the switches next to the names.</p><figure><img src=\"img/instrukcja/okno-wzorcow.png\" alt=\"Masters window\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Masters window</figcaption></figure>"},{"id":"11.5","tytul":"Your own master and editing","html":"<p><span class=\"ui\">Draw your own master</span> creates an empty master and opens it right away. <span class=\"ui\">Edit</span> opens an existing one. The purple bar above the page reminds you that you're changing a master — everything you draw here will appear on the pages with this master; <span class=\"ui\">Done</span> returns to the pages. On ordinary pages, master elements are visible in the layer list, and clicking there opens the master.</p><figure><img src=\"img/instrukcja/wzorce.png\" alt=\"Editing a master\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Editing a master</figcaption></figure>"},{"id":"11.6","tytul":"Above content and mirroring","html":"<p>Master elements lie <b>under</b> the page content. Select an element in the master and turn on:</p><ul class=\"punkty\"><li><b>Above page content</b> — the element will cover the content, e.g. a frame or a watermark,</li><li><b>Mirror on left-hand pages</b> — an element by the right edge (e.g. a page number) moves to the left edge on the left pages of spreads. Ready-made masters “by the outer edge” have this turned on.</li></ul>"},{"id":"11.7","tytul":"Fields in a master","html":"<p>Type a text with a field in the master, e.g. <code>Page {page} of {page_count}</code>, <code>{section}</code> or <code>{chapter}</code> (the title of the current chapter). Each page will show its own value. The easiest way is to start from a ready-made master with a page number (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"11.3\">Ready-made masters</a>).</p>"}]},
  {"nr":"12","tytul":"Guide lines, grid and zoom","czesc":"Editor","ikona":"olowek","wstep":"Things that help you position elements and don't print: margins, bleed, grid, guides, zoom.","sekcje":[{"id":"12.1","tytul":"Lines on the page","html":"<p>Guide lines are not printed:</p><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Red</b></td><td>The page edge after trimming.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Pink band</b></td><td>Bleed. Stretch backgrounds and photos that should reach the edge all the way to its end.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Blue dashed</b></td><td>Margin. Keep texts inside.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Thick dark</b></td><td>The spine of a spread.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Dashed with labels</b></td><td>On a cover: folds — spine, creases, turn-ins (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"10.6\">Cover with a spine</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Turquoise</b></td><td>Guide (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"12.4\">Rulers and guides</a>).</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The guide lines button (<kbd>W</kbd>) next to the zoom hides them all.</p>"},{"id":"12.2","tytul":"Warnings","html":"<p>An element in the margin or beyond the bleed, text that doesn't fit, or a field with a typo get an orange frame and go to the <b>Warnings</b> list. On a computer it's opened by the triangle button next to the zoom (<kbd>F8</kbd>) — the number on it tells you how many notes there are; the window doesn't block your work, you close it with the cross or <kbd>Esc</kbd>. On a phone — the <span class=\"ui\">Warnings</span> button at the bottom of the screen. Clicking an item selects the element. When it's intentional (e.g. a background into the bleed), click <span class=\"ui\">Mute</span>.</p><figure><img src=\"img/instrukcja/ostrzezenia.png\" alt=\"Warnings\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Warnings</figcaption></figure>"},{"id":"12.3","tytul":"Grid","html":"<p>In the view menu (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"3.6\">Bottom-right corner and the view menu</a>): <b>Grid</b> shows lines every <b>Grid step</b> (in mm, from the page corner), and <b>Snap to grid</b> puts the top left corner of the element you're moving on a grid line.</p><figure><img src=\"img/instrukcja/siatka.png\" alt=\"Grid and guides\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Grid and guides</figcaption></figure>"},{"id":"12.4","tytul":"Rulers and guides","html":"<ul class=\"punkty\"><li><b>Rulers</b> show millimeters; zero is in the corner of the open page.</li><li>You pull a <b>guide</b> out of a ruler: from the top one downward — horizontal, from the left one to the right — vertical. A guide can be moved; dragged off the page, it disappears.</li><li>In the page properties, the <b>Guides</b> part lets you enter a position as a number and add a vertical or horizontal guide.</li><li><b>Lock guides</b> in the view menu protects them from being moved by accident.</li></ul>"},{"id":"12.5","tytul":"Snapping and distances","html":"<p>When you move or resize an element, it snaps to the edges, margins and center of the page, to guides and to other elements:</p><ul class=\"punkty\"><li><b>pink line</b> — an edge or center lined up with another element,</li><li><b>arrows with a number</b> — the distance in mm to the nearest elements,</li><li><b>purple numbers</b> — a gap equals another gap in the row.</li></ul><p>Holding <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> temporarily turns snapping off. Holding <kbd>Alt</kbd> with an element selected also shows the distances to the page edges.</p><figure><img src=\"img/instrukcja/odleglosci.png\" alt=\"Equal spacing\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Equal spacing</figcaption></figure>"},{"id":"12.6","tytul":"Zoom","html":"<ul class=\"punkty\"><li><span class=\"ui\">−</span> and <span class=\"ui\">+</span> in the bottom right corner; clicking the percentage fits the page to the window (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>0</kbd>).</li><li><kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + mouse wheel zooms in at the cursor.</li><li>Space + dragging moves the view.</li><li>On a touch screen — two fingers (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"28.1\">Gestures</a>).</li></ul>"}]},
  {"nr":"13","tytul":"Book: table of contents, running head, footnotes, cross-references, index","czesc":"Editor","ikona":"olowek","wstep":"Table of contents, chapter title in the footer, footnotes, cross-references and index — they update themselves.","sekcje":[{"id":"13.1","tytul":"In brief","html":"<p>Page numbers come from the section numbering (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"10.8\">Page numbering</a>), including pages repeated by a series (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"16\">Series</a>).</p><figure><img src=\"img/instrukcja/ksiazka.png\" alt=\"Table of contents, footnote and running head on book pages\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Table of contents, footnote and running head on book pages</figcaption></figure>"},{"id":"13.2","tytul":"Headings","html":"<p>Headings are texts in paragraph styles (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"5.4\">Styles</a>) that the project treats as chapter titles. The table of contents, running head and cross-reference list are built from them.</p><ul class=\"punkty\"><li>Without any settings, the <b>Title</b> style is heading 1 (chapter), and <b>Heading</b> – heading 2.</li><li>You set other styles in the table of contents panel: for each style choose <b>heading 1</b>, <b>heading 2</b>, <b>heading 3</b> or <b>not a heading</b>.</li></ul><p class=\"ramka-instrukcji wskazowka\"><b>Tip.</b> give all chapter titles the same style – then the table of contents and running head arrange themselves.</p>"},{"id":"13.3","tytul":"Table of contents","html":"<ol class=\"kroki\"><li>Open <span class=\"ui\">Library</span> → <b>Book</b> → <b>Contents</b> (or <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>K</kbd> and “table of contents”).</li><li>The table of contents lands on the page as text and immediately fills with headings and page numbers.</li><li>You change its look (font, size, line spacing) as in any text.</li></ol><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-spis-tresci.png\" alt=\"Table of contents and its panel\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Table of contents and its panel</figcaption></figure><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Levels</b></td><td><b>chapters only</b>, <b>chapters and subchapters</b> or <b>three levels</b>; lower levels are indented.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Dot leaders to page number</b></td><td>Fills the space between the title and the number with dots.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Style “…”</b></td><td>Which style is a heading and at what level (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"13.2\">Headings</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ui\">Refresh now</span></td><td>Builds the table of contents right away. Usually not needed – it refreshes itself after every change.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Flow a long table of contents into another frame, also on the next page (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"5.7\">Columns and text across several frames</a>). Don't type the table of contents by hand – at the next refresh it would be rebuilt from scratch.</p>"},{"id":"13.4","tytul":"Running head","html":"<p>A running head is the title of the current chapter in the page footer or header.</p><ol class=\"kroki\"><li>Open the footer master (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"11\">Master pages</a>).</li><li>In the footer text choose <b>Insert field</b> → <b>{chapter}</b>. You can put the page number next to it: <code>{chapter} – {page}</code>.</li></ol><p>On each page the field shows the last heading 1 up to that page. <code>{chapter:2}</code> shows heading 2 – a new chapter resets it.</p>"},{"id":"13.5","tytul":"Footnotes","html":"<ol class=\"kroki\"><li>Select a text (or type in it – the footnote will go at the cursor position).</li><li>In the panel, in the <b>Footnotes, cross-references, index</b> part, choose <span class=\"ui\">Insert footnote</span> (or <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>K</kbd> and “footnote”).</li><li>Type the content in the <b>Footnote 1</b> field.</li></ol><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-przypisy.png\" alt=\"Footnote in the panel and at the bottom of the page\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Footnote in the panel and at the bottom of the page</figcaption></figure><ul class=\"punkty\"><li>A superscript number appears in the text, and the content – at the bottom of the same page, above the bottom margin, under a short rule. In the editor you see it right away, but you can't select it: you change it in the panel.</li><li>Numbers are counted automatically. <b>Footnote numbering</b>: <b>Restart in each section</b>, <b>Restart on each page</b> or <b>Across the whole project</b>.</li><li>Text flowing through several frames takes the footnote with it: the footnote is on the page where its number stands.</li><li>Footnote look: the paragraph style named <b>Footnote</b> (if there isn't one – 8 pt type).</li><li><span class=\"ui\">Delete footnote</span> removes both the number from the text and the content.</li></ul>"},{"id":"13.6","tytul":"Cross-references","html":"<p>A cross-reference is the page number of another place in the book, e.g. “see p. 12”.</p><ol class=\"kroki\"><li>Select the text where the cross-reference should go.</li><li>In the <b>Footnotes, cross-references, index</b> part, choose a heading from the <b>Insert cross-reference to</b> list.</li><li>“p. 12” appears in the text – the number updates itself when pages move.</li></ol><p>In the editor a cross-reference shows as a <code>{s:…}</code> field; you'll see the number in the preview and in the PDF. If you delete the heading, a “?” appears instead of the number, and <b>Preflight check</b> reminds you of it (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"20\">Preflight check</a>).</p>"},{"id":"13.7","tytul":"Index","html":"<ol class=\"kroki\"><li>Select a word in a text you are typing.</li><li>Choose <span class=\"ui\">Add to index</span> (or <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>K</kbd> and “index”). With nothing selected, the app asks for the entry.</li><li>Insert the index: <span class=\"ui\">Library</span> → <b>Book</b> → <b>Index</b>.</li></ol><p>The index lists entries alphabetically (Polish order: ą after a, ł after l) with the numbers of all pages where the word appears in any text – letter case doesn't matter, and consecutive pages merge into a range (3–5). A text's entries are shown in the panel as chips; clicking a chip removes the entry.</p>"}]},
  {"nr":"14","tytul":"Data tables","czesc":"Data and series","ikona":"dane","wstep":"You keep data in the project's tables; field values and repeated pages come from them.","sekcje":[{"id":"14.1","tytul":"The Data window","html":"<p>A table is a sheet: columns have names (e.g. <code>dzien</code>, <code>imie</code>), and rows have values. Tables provide the values of fields in text (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"15\">Fields in text</a>) and repeated pages (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"16\">Series</a>).</p><p>The window opens with <span class=\"ui\">Data</span> on the editor's top bar. On the left is the list of the project's tables, on the right the selected table. <span class=\"ui\">+</span> adds an empty table, <span class=\"ui\">Import CSV or Excel</span> — a table from a file (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"14.2\">Importing from Excel and CSV</a>), <span class=\"ui\">Skipped row</span> — a <code>[skip]</code> row (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"16.4\">Skipped rows</a>), and <span class=\"ui\">Download table as CSV</span> gives you the table to finish in a spreadsheet. Changes made during one opening of the window are undone with a single <span class=\"ui\">Undo</span>.</p><figure><img src=\"img/instrukcja/dane.png\" alt=\"The Data window\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>The Data window</figcaption></figure>"},{"id":"14.2","tytul":"Importing from Excel and CSV","html":"<p><span class=\"ui\">Import CSV or Excel</span> loads an <code>.xlsx</code>, <code>.xls</code>, <code>.ods</code> or <code>.csv</code> file:</p><ul class=\"punkty\"><li>the first row of the file holds the column names,</li><li>an Excel file gives one table for each sheet,</li><li>the app detects the separator (semicolon, comma, tab) and special characters by itself,</li><li>numbers and dates go into the table the way they look in the spreadsheet. It's best to split a date into separate columns, e.g. <code>dzien</code>, <code>miesiac</code>, <code>rok</code>.</li></ul><p>Fixed the data in the spreadsheet? Import the file again. If the project has a table with the same name, the app will ask whether to replace its data.</p><h4>Data source linked to the table</h4><p>A table remembers where it came from. Below its name you see a bar, e.g. “Data from: goscie.xlsx, sheet »Lista« · loaded 18.11.2026, 14:20”. The original file (up to 5 MB) is stored in the project – it isn't among the images and isn't removed during cleanup.</p><ul class=\"punkty\"><li><span class=\"ui\">Load new version…</span> – you choose the corrected file; fields and series stay. Before the data is replaced, you'll see what will change: the number of rows, changed cells, new and removed columns, and separately a warning when a column used in page fields disappears (those fields would be empty).</li><li><span class=\"ui\">Refresh from file</span> – in Chrome and Edge on a computer, the browser remembers access to the file chosen with “Load new version…”. After a fix in Excel, one click is enough (the browser may ask for permission once).</li><li><b>changed in Makietka</b> – a marker on a table that was edited in the Data window after the file was loaded. A new version of the file will replace those edits – the replace prompt will remind you of this too.</li><li>The down-arrow icon downloads the stored source file, the table icon – the current data as an Excel file (<code>.xlsx</code>), the trash can unlinks the source (the data stays).</li></ul><p><b>Data from a link</b> (chain icon next to <span class=\"ui\">Import</span>) – paste the address of a Google Sheet or a CSV/XLSX file on the web. The Google Sheet must be public: File → Share → Publish to the web → CSV (a regular link to a sheet accessible to anyone with the link also works). The table remembers the link, and <span class=\"ui\">Refresh from link</span> downloads the data again, with the same comparison of changes. Some services don't allow downloading from a browser – the app will tell you; then download the file and load it with a regular import.</p>"},{"id":"14.3","tytul":"Editing data in the app","html":"<ul class=\"punkty\"><li>Click a cell and type. <kbd>Enter</kbd> moves one row down, <kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>Enter</kbd> makes a new line in the cell.</li><li>Data copied from Excel or Google Sheets is pasted starting at the selected cell (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>V</kbd>); the table grows down and to the right by itself.</li><li><b>×</b> next to a row or column deletes it.</li><li>You rename a column by clicking its header. The app will update that name in all fields in the project.</li></ul>"},{"id":"14.4","tytul":"Cell formatting","html":"<p>Bold, italic and text color from an Excel file carry over to the pages: a value inserted into a field gets the look of its cell — e.g. holidays in bold red. The look of parts of the text in a cell carries over too.</p><p>In the Data window select a cell (<kbd>Shift</kbd>+click selects a rectangle of several cells) and use: <span class=\"ui\">B</span> — bold, <span class=\"ui\">I</span> — italic, text color, <span class=\"ui\">Clear formatting</span> (eraser).</p><p>The <b>Carry cell formatting to pages</b> switch turns the carrying over off — the formatting stays saved in the table.</p>"},{"id":"14.5","tytul":"Checking values","html":"<p>Next to each column name there are two signs:</p><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Sign</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>≠</b></td><td>Values in the column shouldn't repeat (e.g. quotes of the day). Repeated cells are red, and before printing the app will list the pages where the same value appears twice. Letter case and spaces don't matter.</td></tr><tr><td><b>☰</b></td><td>A list of allowed values, e.g. <code>praca; dom; wolne</code>. Other values are highlighted and go on the list before printing; in the data template the cells get a drop-down list. <span class=\"ui\">Remove list</span> removes it.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},{"id":"14.6","tytul":"Fill a column with AI","html":"<p>The <b>✦</b> button next to a column name in the <b>Data</b> window writes values into the column – e.g. “a short thought for this day” in a calendar or “a one-sentence description” for each product. Write what to fill in; AI sees the other columns of the row (e.g. the date or name). By default it fills only <b>empty cells</b>; the window shows the number of rows and the cost (usually a few grosz for hundreds of rows). Check the result – content that must be exact (quotes, verses) should come from the source.</p>"}]},
  {"nr":"15","tytul":"Fields in text","czesc":"Data and series","ikona":"dane","wstep":"A field is a place in text where a value from a table or from the project goes, e.g. the page number.","sekcje":[{"id":"15.1","tytul":"Inserting a field","html":"<p>A field is a column name in curly braces, typed into ordinary text, e.g. <code>Name day: {imieniny}</code>. On the finished page the value from the table takes the place of the field.</p><p>Select the text and choose a column from the <span class=\"ui\">Insert field</span> list in the properties. The list starts with the columns of this page's table, followed by document fields (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"15.2\">Document fields</a>). You can also type a field by hand.</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-wstaw-pole.png\" alt=\"Insert field\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Insert field</figcaption></figure><p>In the editor fields have a purple background, and a field the app doesn't recognise (e.g. a typo) — a red one. The real values are visible in thumbnails, in the preview and in the PDF.</p><ul class=\"punkty\"><li>Table fields work on the pages of a series that uses that table (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"16\">Series</a>).</li><li>A field from an additional table is typed with the table name: <code>{Tygodnie.haslo}</code> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"16.5\">Additional tables</a>).</li><li>A bold field gives a bold value.</li></ul><figure><img src=\"img/instrukcja/pola.png\" alt=\"Fields in text\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Fields in text</figcaption></figure>"},{"id":"15.2","tytul":"Document fields","html":"<p>Document fields come from the project itself and work on every page, masters included:</p><div data-wstaw=\"pola\"></div><p>Document field names work in every app language: <code>{page}</code>, <code>{nr}</code> (Polish) and <code>{номер}</code> (Ukrainian) are the same field. <b>Insert field</b> lists the names in the language you have chosen; a project opened in another language is calculated the same way.</p><p>Fields <b>from project settings</b> — <code>{format}</code>, <code>{binding}</code>, <code>{spine}</code>, <code>{block_pages}</code>, <code>{paper}</code>, <code>{calendar_year}</code> (and <code>{project}</code>) — need no typing in: they take their value from the section, cover and calendar settings and change along with them, e.g. an imprint line “Format {format}, binding {binding}, {block_pages} pages”. In the <b>Insert field</b> list they're in a separate group, <b>From project settings</b>. When the project has no cover or calendar, the field is empty.</p><p>When a table column has the same name as a document field, the column wins on that table's pages.</p>"},{"id":"15.3","tytul":"Plain braces","html":"<ul class=\"punkty\"><li>Whole text without fields: select the text and turn on <b>Braces { } are plain text, not fields</b> (the switch appears when the text contains braces).</li><li>A field and braces in one text: type the braces twice — <code>{{</code> and <code>}}</code> become single braces, e.g. <code>Day {dzien}, formula {{x}}</code> gives “Day 5, formula {x}”.</li></ul>"}]},
  {"nr":"16","tytul":"Series","czesc":"Data and series","ikona":"dane","wstep":"A series repeats a page for every row of a table — that's how a 365-day calendar comes from one page.","sekcje":[{"id":"16.1","tytul":"What a series is","html":"<p>You design one day page — you get 365 pages, each with the data from its own row. A series can cover several neighbouring pages (a <b>block</b>), e.g. a week on a spread: then the whole block repeats together.</p><p>Repeated pages aren't saved separately — they're generated on the fly, so a change to the table immediately changes the publication.</p>"},{"id":"16.2","tytul":"New series","html":"<ol class=\"kroki\"><li>Click an empty spot on the page — the properties will show the page settings.</li><li>In the <b>Series</b> field choose <span class=\"ui\">+ New series…</span>.</li><li>Set up the series in the window (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"16.3\">Series settings</a>) and click <span class=\"ui\">Save</span>.</li><li>For the following pages of the block, choose the same series.</li></ol><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-seria.png\" alt=\"Series in the page properties\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Series in the page properties</figcaption></figure><p>Series pages have a purple badge in the page list with the number of repeats, e.g. <b>×31</b>. <b>×0</b> means that all rows for this page are skipped.</p><figure><img src=\"img/instrukcja/serie.png\" alt=\"Repeated pages in the preview\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Repeated pages in the preview</figcaption></figure>"},{"id":"16.3","tytul":"Series settings","html":"<p>The series window opens when you choose <span class=\"ui\">+ New series…</span>, and for an existing series — with the <span class=\"ui\">Series settings</span> button in the page properties. Below the <b>Series</b> field you can see how many pages will be printed.</p><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Series name</b></td><td>The name in the list of series.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Data from table</b></td><td>The table the rows come from.</td></tr><tr><td><b>How many times to repeat</b></td><td>Switch: <b>As many as table rows</b> or <b>A fixed number of times</b> (then the <b>Number of repeats</b> field appears) — e.g. 12 months; the data then loops around.</td></tr><tr><td><b>How to read the table</b></td><td>Switch: <b>Row per block</b> (columns e.g. <code>s1_tytul</code>, <code>s2_tytul</code> for consecutive pages) or <b>Row per page</b>.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Complete the last block with pages without data</b></td><td>The series ends at the last row; with this setting the last block is full.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Keep left and right pages</b></td><td>In spreads (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"16.6\">Series in spreads</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Additional tables</b></td><td>Data that changes less often than the pages (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"16.5\">Additional tables</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Data the pages need</b></td><td>Fields used on the block's pages. <span class=\"ui\">Add missing columns to table</span> adds the missing ones.</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ui\">Download data template (Excel)</span></td><td>A file with the columns the pages need (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"16.7\">Data template</a>).</td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-seria-segmenty.png\" alt=\"Segments in the Series window\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Segments in the Series window</figcaption></figure>"},{"id":"16.4","tytul":"Skipped rows","html":"<p>Example: a calendar with a block per week (Monday–Sunday), and the year starts on a Thursday. Without doing anything, 1 January would land on the Monday page. So add three <b>skipped rows</b> at the start of the table — for Monday, Tuesday and Wednesday.</p><p>A skipped row has the <code>[skip]</code> marker in its first cell. It takes its place in the block, but its page isn't created: it isn't in the preview, the PDF or the numbering. You insert it with the <span class=\"ui\">Skipped row</span> button in the Data window — above the row where the cursor is — or type <code>[skip]</code> in Excel. The marker works in every language: <code>[skip]</code>, <code>[pomiń]</code> (Polish) and <code>[пропустити]</code> (Ukrainian) mean the same.</p><p>The end of the year needs nothing: the series ends at the last data row.</p>"},{"id":"16.5","tytul":"Additional tables","html":"<p>An additional table provides data that changes less often than the pages — e.g. a quote of the week on the day pages or a picture of the month. In the series window: <span class=\"ui\">Add table</span>, and then <b>New row</b>:</p><ul class=\"punkty\"><li><b>every fixed number of pages</b> — e.g. every 7 pages for weeks,</li><li><b>when column “…” changes</b> — e.g. <code>miesiac</code>: a new row where the month changes in the main table. You can choose several columns (e.g. month and year) in the following <b>or column</b> lists.</li></ul><p>A field from an additional table: <code>{Tygodnie.haslo}</code>. When an additional table runs out of rows, the series window shows it in red, and before printing — in the list of warnings.</p>"},{"id":"16.6","tytul":"Series in spreads","html":"<p><b>Keep left and right pages – insert a blank page when needed</b> makes a page designed as a left page always come out on the left, and a right page on the right. When needed, the app inserts a blank white page — e.g. after the series, when the data ends on a left page. The blank page counts in the numbering, as in printed books. <span class=\"ui\">Preflight check</span> lists where the blank pages are.</p>"},{"id":"16.7","tytul":"Data template","html":"<p><span class=\"ui\">Download data template (Excel)</span> gives you a file with exactly the columns the pages require, together with the data already in the table. The “Instrukcja” sheet describes each column. Columns with a list of values, image names or a crop have drop-down lists. Import the filled-in file in the Data window.</p>"}]},
  {"nr":"17","tytul":"Appearance from data","czesc":"Data and series","ikona":"dane","wstep":"An element's color, position, image or visibility can change on each repeated page.","sekcje":[{"id":"17.1","tytul":"The From data part","html":"<p>An element can change along with the data on consecutive repeated pages: color, position, size, rotation, image or visibility. Select the element and expand the <b>From data</b> part in the properties.</p><p>In the editor the element looks like the template (position 1, the template's color, always visible). The actual look of each page is visible in the preview, in thumbnails and in the PDF.</p><figure><img src=\"img/instrukcja/zakladki.png\" alt=\"A tab on the edge: a different position and color on each page\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>A tab on the edge: a different position and color on each page</figcaption></figure>"},{"id":"17.2","tytul":"Color from field","html":"<p><b>Color from field</b> — a column with colors, e.g. <code>#d1495b</code>, <code>2e86ab</code> or a name in English (<code>navy</code>). An empty or invalid value keeps the template's color. A line and a shape without fill get the stroke color. With a gradient fill you choose what the color from the field changes: the first color, the last color or the whole gradient in shades.</p>"},{"id":"17.3","tytul":"Position, size and rotation from field","html":"<div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Horizontal offset from field</b>, <b>Vertical offset from field</b></td><td>A field with a number — a table column or e.g. <code>{copy}</code> (the repeat number).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Field value is</b></td><td><b>position number</b> — the element moves by the <b>Step</b> (mm) for each position; <b>Restart every</b> N goes back to the start. Or <b>offset in mm</b> — the number from the table taken directly.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Width from field</b>, <b>Height from field</b></td><td>The value is a <b>percentage of the template size (100 = unchanged)</b> or a <b>size in mm</b>. <b>When resizing, keep in place</b> — which side or corner stays put.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Rotation from field (degrees)</b></td><td>An angle added to the template's rotation, e.g. a clock hand that's different on each page.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>That's how you make tabs on the page edge — lower on each page — or bars with heights from a table.</p>"},{"id":"17.4","tytul":"Show element","html":"<p><b>Show element</b>: <b>always</b> or only when the chosen field <b>is not empty</b>, <b>is empty</b> or <b>equals</b> the value you enter. Example: a star next to a holiday — the <code>swieto</code> column and “is not empty”.</p>"},{"id":"17.5","tytul":"Image from data","html":"<p>Insert any image as a template — it sets the position and size. In the image properties, in the <b>Image from data</b> part:</p><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>File name column</b></td><td>A file name in each row, e.g. <code>styczen.jpg</code>. Upload all the files at once in the Project images window (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"7.5\">The Project images window</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Replaced image</b></td><td><b>fits in the frame (fully visible)</b> — with free space at the sides or at the top; <b>fills the frame (edges cropped)</b> — without empty bands.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Part of the photo remains</b></td><td>When filling: center, top, bottom, left or right side — e.g. “top”, so heads aren't cut off in portraits.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Crop from column</b></td><td><b>the same on every page</b> or a column where for each photo you type <code>góra</code>, <code>dół</code>, <code>lewa</code>, <code>prawa</code> or <code>środek</code>.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},{"id":"17.6","tytul":"Text of varying length","html":"<p>Texts from a table have different lengths, but the page layout should stay the same. Give the frame a <b>Fixed height</b> and turn on <b>Shrink text when it doesn't fit</b> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"5.6\">Text frame</a>). <span class=\"ui\">Preflight check</span> will list the pages where the text still doesn't fit.</p><figure><img src=\"img/instrukcja/ramka-tekstu.png\" alt=\"A frame with a fixed height\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>A frame with a fixed height</figcaption></figure>"}]},
  {"nr":"18","tytul":"Examples","czesc":"Data and series","ikona":"dane","wstep":"Ready-made step-by-step recipes.","sekcje":[{"id":"18.1","tytul":"Day-per-page calendar","html":"<p>One page per day, with the day of the week, name days and holidays in color.</p><ol class=\"kroki\"><li>New A5 project, <b>Spreads</b> layout (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"2.2\">New project</a>).</li><li>In the Data window import a <b>Dni</b> table with the columns <code>dzien</code>, <code>miesiac</code>, <code>dzien_tygodnia</code>, <code>imieniny</code>, <code>swieto</code> — or ask AI for it (prompt below).</li><li>On the page insert the texts <code>{dzien}</code>, <code>{dzien_tygodnia}</code> and <code>Name day: {imieniny}</code> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"15.1\">Inserting a field</a>).</li><li>Click an empty spot on the page and in <b>Series</b> create a series with the Dni table (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"16.2\">New series</a>) — 365 pages will be created.</li><li>Insert a small <code>{swieto}</code> text in red and set <b>Show element → is not empty</b> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"17.4\">Show element</a>).</li><li>Add the page number and footer as a master (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"11.3\">Ready-made masters</a>).</li><li>Check the preview and export the PDF (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"19\">Preview</a>, <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"21\">Export to PDF and PNG</a>).</li></ol><div class=\"prompt-instrukcji\"><div class=\"prompt-naglowek\"><b>Prompt for AI: a table of days for a calendar</b><button type=\"button\" class=\"przycisk\" data-kopiuj-prompt data-ikona=\"kopiuj\">Copy</button></div><p class=\"ciche\">Copy it, replace the project name and paste it into a chat with an AI that has Makietka connected (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"25\">Working with AI</a>).</p><pre>Korzystając z łącznika Makietka, w projekcie „&lt;nazwa projektu&gt;”:\n1. Przeczytaj opis formatu (narzędzie opis_formatu) i przegląd projektu.\n2. Zrób tabelę danych „Dni 2027” z kolumnami: data, dzien, miesiac, dzien_tygodnia, swieto, kolor (kolor liczby: czarny w dni zwykłe, czerwony #c0392b w niedziele i święta).\n3. Wypełnij ją dla całego roku 2027 (polskie święta państwowe i kościelne w kolumnie swieto).\n4. Zrób stronę-wzór dnia: duża liczba {dzien} z kolorem z pola „kolor”, pod nią {dzien_tygodnia} i {miesiac}, a niżej {swieto}. Ustaw serię „Dni” z tą tabelą.\n5. Pokaż mi, ile stron powstanie, i uruchom kontrolę przed drukiem.</pre></div>"},{"id":"18.2","tytul":"Planner with a picture of the month","html":"<ol class=\"kroki\"><li>Make the day pages as in the calendar (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"18.1\">Day-per-page calendar</a>).</li><li>Add a <b>Miesiące</b> table with the columns <code>miesiac</code> and <code>obraz</code> (file names), and upload the photos in the Project images window (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"7.5\">The Project images window</a>).</li><li>In the series window add it as an additional table: <b>New row → when column “miesiac” changes</b> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"16.5\">Additional tables</a>).</li><li>Insert an image on the page and in <b>File name column</b> choose the <code>obraz</code> column of the Miesiące table (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"17.5\">Image from data</a>).</li></ol>"},{"id":"18.3","tytul":"Personalised invitations","html":"<ol class=\"kroki\"><li>A project in invitation format, e.g. 148 × 105 mm.</li><li>A <b>Goście</b> table with an <code>imie</code> column — e.g. pasted from a spreadsheet (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"14.3\">Editing data in the app</a>).</li><li>Design the invitation and type <code>Dear {imie}!</code> in the text.</li><li>A series with the Goście table — one invitation for each guest.</li><li>A <b>For print</b> PDF split into files or as a single file (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"21.1\">Export window</a>).</li></ol>"}]},
  {"nr":"19","tytul":"Preview","czesc":"Finished publication","ikona":"pdf","wstep":"The preview shows the whole publication as it will come out of the printer.","sekcje":[{"id":"19.1","tytul":"Opening the preview","html":"<p>The preview includes data and repeated pages, with no guides and no risk of accidental changes. Open it with the <span class=\"ui\">Preview</span> button on the editor's top bar (<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>W</kbd>) or from the <span class=\"ui\">⋯</span> menu on the project card — then the preview opens straight away, without the editor.</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-podglad.png\" alt=\"Preview on the top bar\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Preview on the top bar</figcaption></figure><p>Pages follow in order, spreads stand side by side. Series pages have a caption, e.g. \"3 of 31\". Clicking a page opens it in the editor; <span class=\"ui\">Editor</span> or <kbd>Esc</kbd> takes you back to editing.</p><figure><img src=\"img/instrukcja/podglad.png\" alt=\"Preview\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Preview</figcaption></figure>"},{"id":"19.2","tytul":"Zooming","html":"<ul class=\"punkty\"><li><span class=\"ui\">−</span> and <span class=\"ui\">+</span> on the bar; clicking the percentage goes back to 100%, i.e. the whole spread in the window.</li><li><kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + mouse wheel zooms in at the cursor.</li><li>On a phone or tablet: pinch with two fingers; a double tap zooms in or back out.</li></ul>"},{"id":"19.3","tytul":"Presentation","html":"<p><span class=\"ui\">Presentation</span> shows one page or spread at a time, full screen. Arrow keys and the space bar turn the pages, <kbd>Esc</kbd> or <span class=\"ui\">×</span> closes it. On a phone, swipe sideways to turn the page, swipe down to close the presentation.</p>"},{"id":"19.4","tytul":"3D mockup","html":"<p><span class=\"ui\">3D mockup</span> next to <span class=\"ui\">Separations</span> on the preview bar shows the project as a printed object — like a product photo in a studio: real page dimensions, thickness from the paper and page count, light with a shadow on a table or wall. You can rotate it and flip through it before it goes to the print shop.</p><p>The full-screen button in the window header (next to close) enlarges the mockup to the whole screen — the placement, page flipping and view stay. The same button or Esc returns to the normal window. It also works for someone viewing the mockup from a link.</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-makieta.png\" alt=\"3D mockup: an open hardcover book\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>3D mockup: an open hardcover book</figcaption></figure><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>What it looks like</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Book – softcover</b></td><td>Lies on a table. The cover comes from the cover section (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"10.6\">Cover with a spine</a>): front, spine and back, at the size of the interior; stiff like card — it bends only at the spine. The interior page by page, open pages curved at the spine, page edges visible from the side. Without a cover section, the first page is the cover.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Book – hardcover</b></td><td>Rigid boards and a spine under a thin cover paper; at the hinge the board slopes smoothly down to the deepest point of the groove, with no sharp corners. When closed it lies against the block; when open the board turns in the hinge groove and lies at the same height as when closed. The cover spine (rounded or flat) is as wide as the block plus two boards and stays in place. Hinge width and board thickness come from the cover settings (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"10.6\">Cover with a spine</a>). Endpaper: a sheet folded in half, glued to the board only where it lies on it; the piece between the fold and the board edge is loose (of fixed length). The free leaf of the endpaper is glued to the first page of the block along 3 mm at the spine: when open it curves back onto the board and slightly lifts the first page, and turning the first page lays it on the endpaper and flattens it. The block always has the same thickness. Nothing passes through the block or goes under the table.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Saddle-stitched booklet</b></td><td>Sheets folded in half, nested one inside another and stapled with two staples in the fold. No spine — all leaves come out of one place and only spread to their own height at the fold; the front edge is trimmed flush. Opens almost completely flat. Chosen for a cover with saddle-stitch binding.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Spiral notepad</b></td><td>Wire-O binding: two parallel wires at each hole. The ring diameter is chosen from the block thickness as manufacturers do (from 1/4″ to 1¼″, pitch 3:1 or 2:1); a block thicker than about 28 mm gets the largest ring and a note below the mockup. The notepad lies on the wire.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Wall calendar</b></td><td>Hangs on the wall; the Wire-O spiral at the top has a gap in the middle with a semicircular cut-out for the hanger. A turned leaf goes to the back.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Desk calendar</b></td><td>Stands on a desk on a thick cardboard stand; leaves turn over the top to the other side.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Single sheet</b></td><td>Flyer, poster, invitation — lies on a table, front and back.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Type matched to the project.</b> The <b>Type</b> list shows at the top only what the project really is — usually one type, at most two: hard cover — <b>Book – hardcover</b>; soft — book or spiral notepad; saddle-stitched — saddle-stitched booklet; no cover with 12–14 leaves, or a calendar — wall and desk calendar; one or two pages — sheet; no cover, up to 64 pages in a multiple of 4 — notepad or saddle-stitched booklet; other without a cover — notepad or book. The saddle-stitched booklet warns you when the page count is not a multiple of 4 or when it is too thick for staples (over about 64 pages). The other types are lower down, in the <b>Doesn't match the project</b> group: if you pick one of those, the mockup tells you what is missing and suggests what to do (e.g. change the binding to hardcover, add a cover or start from a project template; missing leaves are shown as white).</p><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th><b>Folded flyer</b></th><th>From a flyer-type section (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"10.11\">Special sections: dust jacket, flyer, calendars, die lines, imposition</a>): folded with the cover on top, it unfolds panel by panel (roll fold or accordion), then turns over to the outside.</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Die-cut box</b></td><td>From a box die line: first a flat printed sheet, then the walls, flaps and lid fold into a solid.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Three-month calendar</b></td><td>Hangs on the wall: a backboard with a header, three calendar pads and a red window on the middle one.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>When a section of the interior has a different format from the rest (e.g. an A4 endpaper with an A5 interior, with no type set), the mockup can't assemble it into a book correctly and says so below the image – <span class=\"ui\">Set section type…</span> opens that section's settings (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"10.4\">Section settings</a>).</p><p>The <b>Type</b> list shows only what fits the project's sections: flyer, envelope, box and three-month calendar — when the project has such a section; books, notepad, calendars and sheet — when it has regular pages. When the project has a <b>dust jacket</b> section, the hardcover book shows it on the cover (front, spine and back from the jacket, flaps folded inside); the <span class=\"ui\">Show dust jacket</span> switch hides it and reveals the cover.</p><p>The type is chosen automatically (with a cover — book; calendar from the name or from 12–14 leaves; one page — sheet); you change it with the <b>Type</b> list. For a book or notepad, set the <b>Placement</b> list: <b>Lying</b> or <b>Standing</b> — closed, it stands on its bottom edge; open, it spreads into a \"V\" like on a display shelf (the V opens smoothly together with the cover). After you open a standing book, the view turns to face it head-on so both pages look the same, and after closing it returns to an angle; if you rotate the view with your finger or mouse, it stays where you put it. The choice is remembered for the project.</p><p>On a phone or tablet the mockup keeps fewer pages in memory at once and draws them at a lower resolution (without the shine of laminate and varnish in the light) – phones with little memory used to close the browser tab. If the tab still closes with the mockup open, the next time you open it the mockup runs in <b>power-saving mode</b> and says so above the preview.</p><p>The interior format is the format of most pages in the project. Endpaper-type sections (front or back) are not part of the interior: in a book the front endpaper lies on the inside of the front cover and as a loose leaf before page 1, the back one — the other way round, just as after binding (an A4 landscape sheet with an A5 book splits into two halves). A hardcover without endpapers in the project gets a blank endpaper. Any other page twice as wide as the interior — e.g. a spread on a single sheet — also splits into two pages.</p><ul class=\"punkty\"><li><b>Rotating</b> — drag with the mouse or your finger. The light moves with the view, and the mockup can't be turned under the table or behind the wall.</li><li><b>Zooming</b> — mouse wheel, two fingers or <span class=\"ui\">−</span> and <span class=\"ui\">+</span>; <span class=\"ui\">View</span> goes back to the front view. After zooming in, the pages load again at a higher resolution — they're sharp up close.</li><li><b>Page flipping</b> — the arrows below the mockup, the <kbd>←</kbd> <kbd>→</kbd> keys or a tap on the right or left half of the image. Below it you can see what is open, e.g. \"Pages 2–3\" or \"Leaf 3 of 13\".</li><li><span class=\"ui\">Download mockup image</span> saves a PNG image in the same framing as on screen — e.g. for a quote or a post online. You choose the size in the <b>Export</b> part (below). In the dark theme the studio background is dark.</li></ul><p>Someone with a preview link (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"23.1\">Creating a link</a>) also sees the mockup — in one variant, without settings. The link follows your changes by itself: after you save a shot it shows the new variant, and a link created earlier gets the mockup the next time the project is opened.</p><p class=\"ramka-instrukcji uwaga\"><b>Note.</b> The mockup is drawn by the graphics card. A device without 3D graphics (an old phone, a blocked graphics card, e.g. Chrome on an older Mac) shows a <b>simplified mockup</b>: the same book, notepad or calendar, with the same pages, rotating with finger and mouse and page flipping, just without cast shadows, shine and reflections (there's a short note about it above the mockup). The mockup image (PNG) and saved shots work the same way. You can save a picture of the mockup only by downloading it — <span class=\"ui\">Download mockup image</span> (PNG), also in a mockup from a link.</p><h4>Preset shots</h4><p>Above the mockup there's a row of tiles — one tap sets the camera, placement and leaves: <b>Front</b>, <b>¾ (classic)</b>, <b>From above (flat)</b>, <b>Spine</b>, <b>Standing</b>, <b>Spread from above</b>, <b>Fanned pages</b>, <b>Stack of copies</b>, <b>Front and back</b> (two copies side by side, the second one flipped) and <b>On the wall</b> for wall calendars (a plaster wall, light from a window). You only see the shots that fit the type (a desk calendar has no stack or view from above). In the front, ¾ and stack shots the box is folded, and from above – flat. After choosing a shot you can keep rotating and zooming.</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-makieta-ujecia.png\" alt=\"Preset shots: fanned pages on a wooden tabletop\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Preset shots: fanned pages on a wooden tabletop</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-makieta-stos.png\" alt=\"Stack of copies on wood\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Stack of copies on wood</figcaption></figure><h4>Shot and lighting</h4><p>The collapsed <b>Shot and lighting</b> part below the mockup gathers all the scene settings (the same on a phone):</p><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>What you set</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Camera</b></td><td>rotation, tilt, distance and <b>lens</b> — a narrow angle is a telephoto lens (flat, no distortion), a wide one — wide angle with strong perspective</td></tr><tr><td><b>Book</b></td><td>opening angle (180° — flat), cover ajar, fanned pages and choice of spread from a list</td></tr><tr><td><b>Light</b></td><td>direction (from the left, from the right, from above, window — low from the side), tone (neutral, warm, cool), softness and shadow strength</td></tr><tr><td><b>Surface and background</b></td><td>studio gradient, solid color, wood, paper, concrete, marble, fabric, plaster or transparent; background color (with textures it changes their tint)</td></tr><tr><td><b>Copies</b></td><td>one, two or three side by side, a stack of 3 or 5, front and back</td></tr><tr><td><b>Materials</b></td><td>cover finish (as in the project, matte, gloss, soft-touch), white or cream paper, color of the spiral and stand; <b>Project foil and varnish in the light</b> — elements with a print finish (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"22.2\">Print finish: varnish, foiling, embossing, white</a>) shine like foil and varnish as you rotate the mockup</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><span class=\"ui\">Restore defaults</span> returns to the starting settings. Light and background are remembered for the project on this device.</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-makieta-swiatlo.png\" alt=\"Shot and lighting: camera, book, light, background, copies, materials\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Shot and lighting: camera, book, light, background, copies, materials</figcaption></figure><h4>Export and animation</h4><p>In the <b>Export</b> part you choose the image size: <b>Web</b> (longer side 1600 px), <b>Print</b> (4000 px), <b>Instagram</b> (square 1080 × 1080) or <b>Custom</b>, plus <b>Transparent background</b> — only the object remains, with its shadow, ready to paste into a website or a presentation.</p><p><span class=\"ui\">Record animation</span> makes a short clip: <b>Turntable</b> or <b>Page flipping</b> (from the current page), as <b>video</b> (MP4 if the browser can do it, otherwise WebM) or <b>GIF</b>. A progress bar shows the frames; <span class=\"ui\">Cancel</span> stops the recording.</p><h4>Saved shots</h4><p>In the <b>Saved shots</b> part, type a name and tap <span class=\"ui\">Save shot</span> — the type, camera, light, background, copies and page positions are saved in the project (also on other devices and for the people you work with; saving can be undone). Tapping a name in the list restores the shot, the bin deletes it.</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-makieta-specjalne.png\" alt=\"Special section mockups: a flyer unfolding and a folded box\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Special section mockups: a flyer unfolding and a folded box</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-makieta-kalendarz.png\" alt=\"3D mockup: wall calendar\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>3D mockup: wall calendar</figcaption></figure><p class=\"ramka-instrukcji wskazowka\"><b>Tip.</b> the mockup shows the shape and thickness of the publication from the paper and page count (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"10.6\">Cover with a spine</a>) — it's a preview, not a proof print. Check the colors in <span class=\"ui\">Separations</span> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"22.6\">Separations preview</a>).</p>"},{"id":"19.5","tytul":"Live preview","html":"<p>An open preview refreshes by itself when the project changes elsewhere — on another device, by a collaborator or by AI. It stays at the same place and zoom; only the changed pages are redrawn.</p>"}]},
  {"nr":"20","tytul":"Preflight check","czesc":"Finished publication","ikona":"pdf","wstep":"A list of things to check before you send the file to the print shop.","sekcje":[{"id":"20.1","tytul":"List of issues","html":"<p><span class=\"ui\">⋯</span> → <span class=\"ui\">Preflight check</span> goes through the whole project — including every repeated page.</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-menu-kontrola.png\" alt=\"Preflight check in the ⋯ menu\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Preflight check in the ⋯ menu</figcaption></figure><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Issue</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Text doesn't fit in the frame</b></td><td>On the listed pages the text sticks out of a fixed-height frame — e.g. with a longer value from the table.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Field without a value</b></td><td>A field with a typo or from a deleted column.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Missing image file</b></td><td>The table contains the name of an image that hasn't been uploaded.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Duplicates</b></td><td>The same value in a column marked <b>≠</b>, with page numbers.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Values outside the list</b></td><td>Values outside the list of a column marked <b>☰</b>, with row numbers.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Missing data</b></td><td>An additional table ran out of rows before the end of the series.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Series pages in spreads</b></td><td>Where blank pages are, or from which page the block lands on the opposite side.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Table goes off the page</b></td><td>The rows grew with the content and the table went off the sheet.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Incomplete signature</b></td><td>A section is short of pages for a full signature (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"10.7\">Signatures</a>); with saddle-stitch binding — the page count isn't divisible by 4.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Ink coverage</b></td><td>An element's color, converted with the printer profile, gives over 300% ink — after you open the separations preview with a profile or export PDF/X-4 (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"22.6\">Separations preview</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Element in the margin or outside the bleed</b></td><td>As in the warnings list in the editor (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"12.2\">Warnings</a>).</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><span class=\"ui\">Show</span> next to an issue opens the preview at the first page it concerns, with that sheet highlighted. <span class=\"ui\">Open template</span> opens the template page in the editor. Issues don't block export — the same list is at the bottom of the PDF export window.</p><figure><img src=\"img/instrukcja/kontrola.png\" alt=\"List of issues\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>List of issues</figcaption></figure>"}]},
  {"nr":"21","tytul":"Export to PDF and PNG","czesc":"Finished publication","ikona":"pdf","wstep":"A file for the print shop, for viewing on screen or for printing at home. The AI assistant can also create the file for you (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"25.5\">PDF file through AI</a>).","sekcje":[{"id":"21.1","tytul":"Export window","html":"<p>Choose <span class=\"ui\">⋯</span> → <span class=\"ui\">Export to PDF…</span>.</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-menu-pdf.png\" alt=\"Export to PDF in the ⋯ menu\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Export to PDF in the ⋯ menu</figcaption></figure><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>File type</b></td><td><b>Vector – sharp text and shapes</b>: text with embedded fonts, sharp at any zoom, selectable. <b>Page images – page as a picture</b>: each page is a single image; it looks the same everywhere, but the file is larger.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Range</b></td><td><b>Whole project</b>, <b>Selected sections</b> (tick one or several; each shows its page count) or <b>Selected pages</b> – numbers as in Word: <code>1-5, 8, 11-</code> (a comma or semicolon between parts, <code>11-</code> – from 11 to the end, <code>-3</code> – from the start to 3). Numbers count across the whole printout, including series repeats. The summary below tells you right away how many pages you'll get and hints when something can't be read.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Purpose</b></td><td><b>For print – with bleed and crop marks</b> or <b>For viewing – trimmed to format</b>. Crop marks are added to sections that have them switched on (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"10.4\">Section settings</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Sheet layout</b></td><td><b>Regular pages</b> or pages laid out on sheets for printing yourself (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"21.6\">Booklet and several pages per sheet</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Quality</b></td><td>Photo resolution: 300 dpi — the standard for print, 150 dpi — a small file for a test, 600 dpi — very sharp.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>At the bottom of the window there's the <b>To check before printing</b> list (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"20\">Preflight check</a>). <span class=\"ui\">Export</span> saves the file; you can stop a long export with the <span class=\"ui\">Cancel</span> button on the progress card.</p><p>A large file (from 150 pages, single file) is made by the Makietka server, not your device — the progress card shows <b>Export to PDF – the Makietka server is making the file</b>, and the finished file downloads by itself. It looks the same, because the server uses the same code. Exception: PDF/X with your own ICC profile (the profile file is only on your device) is always made by the device. Without internet, or if the server has trouble, the device makes the file, as before.</p><figure><img src=\"img/instrukcja/pdf.png\" alt=\"Export window\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Export window</figcaption></figure>"},{"id":"21.2","tytul":"Advanced","html":"<p>The <b>Advanced</b> part:</p><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Convert text to outlines (no fonts in file)</b></td><td>Letters become shapes. Some print shops require this; the text can no longer be selected afterwards.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Text and shape colors in CMYK (black as pure K)</b></td><td>Colors saved as the four printing inks; black text is printed with black ink only, so it's sharp.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Print standard</b></td><td><b>No standard – plain PDF</b>, <b>PDF/X-4 – coated paper (FOGRA39)</b>, <b>PDF/X-4 – uncoated paper (FOGRA47)</b> or <b>PDF/X-4 – printer's profile (.icc file)</b> — <span class=\"ui\">Choose profile file…</span>.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Photos in CMYK too (larger file)</b></td><td>With PDF/X-4: photos converted to printing inks with the same profile.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Split into files</b></td><td><b>Single file</b>, <b>Separate file for each section</b> or <b>Parts of a set number of pages</b> (<b>Pages per part</b>). You get several files in a ZIP archive.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Include font files used in the project</b></td><td>The download is a ZIP with the PDF file and a <code>czcionki</code> folder.</td></tr><tr><td><b>PDF for on-screen reading: bookmarks and clickable links</b></td><td>Bookmarks and links for on-screen readers (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"21.7\">PDF for on-screen reading</a>). On by default; not available with PDF/X-4 and sheet layouts.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Print finishes</b></td><td>When the project has them: <b>Separate plates in the same PDF</b>, <b>Separate PDF for each finish</b> or <b>Skip print finishes</b> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"22.4\">Print finishes in the PDF file</a>).</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},{"id":"21.3","tytul":"How to choose settings for the print shop","html":"<p>Ask the print shop what it needs:</p><ul class=\"punkty\"><li>it says \"PDF/X-4\" — choose that standard and the paper it will print on,</li><li>it says \"everything in CMYK\" — also tick both CMYK boxes,</li><li>it says \"no fonts\" or \"outlines\" — tick <b>Convert text to outlines</b>,</li><li>it needs nothing special — <b>Vector</b>, <b>For print</b>, 300 dpi.</li></ul><p class=\"ramka-instrukcji wskazowka\"><b>Tip.</b> A file from a project in screen colors looks slightly different in print. The safest option is to order a proof print.</p>"},{"id":"21.4","tytul":"What can't be saved as vectors","html":"<p>An element with a shadow, an element clipped by a mask and an SVG drawing with a filter go into the file as an image of that one element — the rest of the page stays vector. Text in a font that can't be embedded goes into the file as outlines. After export the app tells you if this has happened.</p>"},{"id":"21.5","tytul":"Page as PNG","html":"<p><span class=\"ui\">⋯</span> → <span class=\"ui\">Download current page as PNG</span> saves an image of the open page at 300 dpi, with bleed — e.g. to show someone in a message.</p>"},{"id":"21.6","tytul":"Booklet and several pages per sheet","html":"<p><b>Sheet layout</b> in the export window places pages on sheets — for printing on an ordinary printer or at a small print shop:</p><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Layout</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Booklet to fold (saddle-stitched)</b></td><td>Pages in pairs, so that after double-sided printing and folding the sheets in half you get a booklet. The page count is padded with blank pages to a multiple of 4.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Booklet from signatures</b></td><td>The same, but separately for each signature of <b>Signature (pages)</b> — 4, 8, 16 or 32 (by default as in the section settings, <span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"10.7\">Signatures</a>). The signatures are sewn or glued together.</td></tr><tr><td><b>2 pages per sheet</b>, <b>4 pages per sheet</b></td><td>Consecutive pages side by side — e.g. A6 flyers, four to an A4 sheet.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Sheet</b>: <b>Fit to pages</b> (two A5 pages → a 296 × 210 mm sheet) or <b>A4</b> / <b>A3</b> — pages are centered and shrink if they don't fit. <b>Creep (mm)</b>: in a thicker booklet the middle leaves stick out after folding; the pages on them shift by part of this value towards the spine (e.g. 0.5–1 mm for 40 pages).</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-pdf-arkusze.png\" alt=\"Booklet: sheet plan in the export window\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Booklet: sheet plan in the export window</figcaption></figure><p>With <b>For print</b> the sheets have crop marks, and a booklet — dashed fold marks. Pages on the sheets are trimmed to format, without bleed.</p><p class=\"ramka-instrukcji wskazowka\"><b>Tip.</b> It's easier to print a booklet on a home printer with <span class=\"ui\">Print at home…</span> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"21.8\">Print at home</a>).</p>"},{"id":"21.7","tytul":"PDF for on-screen reading","html":"<p>A regular PDF file comes with navigation for on-screen readers:</p><ul class=\"punkty\"><li><b>bookmarks</b> in the reader's side panel — the project's sections, with headings under them (book heading styles, <span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"13\">Book: table of contents, running head, footnotes, cross-references, index</a>),</li><li><b>clickable table of contents</b> — a contents line takes you to its page,</li><li><b>cross-references</b> like \"p. 12\" take you to that page,</li><li><b>element link</b> — in the element panel, in the <b>Other</b> part, the <b>Link in PDF (web address, email)</b> field: clicking the element in the PDF opens the address; a QR code with a web address is clickable on its own.</li></ul><p>Links are in vector files; bookmarks — in both file types. There's no navigation in PDF/X-4 for the print shop.</p>"},{"id":"21.8","tytul":"Print at home","html":"<p>Printing on an ordinary printer: <span class=\"ui\">⋯</span> → <span class=\"ui\">Print at home…</span> or <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>P</kbd> in the editor and preview. The window lays out the pages on sheets by itself — don't print the project from the browser menu.</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-druk.png\" alt=\"Print at home window\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Print at home window</figcaption></figure><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>What to print</b></td><td><b>Regular print</b> (one page per sheet), <b>Booklet to fold</b> (sheets folded in half and stapled in the middle – the page order is arranged for you), <b>2</b> or <b>4 pages per sheet</b>.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Paper</b></td><td>A4, A3 or Letter – the sheet rotates by itself.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Page size</b></td><td><b>Actual</b> (pages shrunk only when they don't fit) or <b>Fit to sheet</b>.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Pages</b></td><td>All, <b>selected sections</b> (one or several), <b>selected pages</b> (as in Word: <code>1-5, 8, 11-</code>) or just the current one.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Grayscale</b></td><td>Black-and-white print.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Crop and fold marks</b></td><td>For a booklet you'll trim after stapling.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Below the settings you can see how the pages will be laid out on the sheets. <span class=\"ui\">Print</span> opens the print dialog with the finished file (on a phone the file opens in a new tab — choose <b>Share → Print</b> there), and <span class=\"ui\">Download PDF for printing</span> saves the same file.</p><p>The browser's print dialog may change the scale its own way, add margins or a header with the date and address. Makietka prints a finished PDF file, not the page itself, so there are no headers or footers in it; 100% scale and double-sided printing are saved in the file. Not every browser follows these settings, so the window shows what to check in the printer dialog:</p><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>In the printer dialog</th><th>Set</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Scale</b></td><td><b>100% / Actual size</b> — not \"Fit\" or \"Shrink to page\".</td></tr><tr><td><b>Margins</b></td><td>Default. The file already has margins, and the pages are laid out on the sheet.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Headers and footers</b></td><td>Off. If you see the date and address in the preview, untick this option.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>A printer can't print the few millimeters at the edge of the sheet. So a page the size of the sheet is slightly reduced to leave a 5 mm margin; the window tells you by how much.</p><p class=\"ramka-instrukcji wskazowka\"><b>Tip.</b> The most reliable print: <span class=\"ui\">Download PDF for printing</span> and print from a PDF program. The first time, print a <span class=\"ui\">test page</span>: the 100 mm ruler should measure exactly 100 mm, and the 3, 5 and 10 mm frames show how much your printer leaves at the edge.</p><ol class=\"kroki\"><li>First print a single sheet as a test.</li><li>Check that the scale in the printer dialog is <b>Actual size / 100%</b>.</li><li>Print a booklet double-sided, <b>flip on short edge</b>; without double-sided printing — first the odd pages, then put the stack back in and print the even ones.</li><li>Fold the sheets in half (first one on top) and staple them in the fold.</li></ol>"}]},
  {"nr":"22","tytul":"Project colors, inks and finishes","czesc":"Finished publication","ikona":"pdf","sekcje":[{"id":"22.1","tytul":"Spot colors","html":"<p>Printing isn't just the four CMYK inks. Makietka knows spot colors (e.g. Pantone), finishes — varnish, foiling, embossing, white — and printing with black ink only or black plus one color. Everything goes into the PDF file the way the print shop expects: each ink on a separate plate.</p><p>A spot color is a separate, ready-mixed ink — in exactly the shade you need (e.g. a logo color). The easiest way is to add it at an element:</p><ol class=\"kroki\"><li>Select an element and open its color (e.g. <b>Fill color</b>).</li><li>At the bottom of the color picker: <span class=\"ui\">Make this color a spot color…</span>.</li><li>Type the ink name exactly as at the print shop or in the swatch book, e.g. \"PANTONE 186 C\", and <span class=\"ui\">Add</span>.</li></ol><p>You can do the same in Properties → <b>Print finish</b> → <b>Spot ink</b> → <b>New from current color…</b>. You then pick the added color in any color field — fill, stroke, text, part of a text — in the <b>Spot colors</b> part, together with a <b>Tint</b> (e.g. 40%).</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-kolor-dodatkowy.png\" alt=\"Spot color in the color picker\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Spot color in the color picker</figcaption></figure><p>You'll find the list of spot colors in <span class=\"ui\">⋯</span> → <span class=\"ui\">Project colors…</span>: there you can rename a color, enter <b>C</b>, <b>M</b>, <b>Y</b>, <b>K</b> (this is only the on-screen preview — the print shop will use its own ink) or delete it. Changing a spot color's CMYK changes all elements in that color. Deleting a color leaves the elements in an ordinary CMYK color.</p>"},{"id":"22.2","tytul":"Print finish: varnish, foiling, embossing, white","html":"<p>You set the finish at the element — nothing needs to be added beforehand. Select an element → Properties → <b>Print finish</b> and tap an effect:</p><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Effect</th><th>What it is</th><th>Plate in PDF</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Varnish</b></td><td>Gloss varnish only in that spot, e.g. a title on a matte cover.</td><td>Spot_UV</td></tr><tr><td><b>Foiling</b></td><td>Metallic foil; <b>Foil color</b>: gold, silver or copper.</td><td>Foil_Gold, Foil_Silver, Foil_Copper</td></tr><tr><td><b>Embossing</b></td><td>A raised pattern pressed into the paper.</td><td>Emboss</td></tr><tr><td><b>Debossing</b></td><td>A recessed pattern pressed into the paper.</td><td>Debossing</td></tr><tr><td><b>White</b></td><td>White ink underneath — on colored, kraft or transparent stock.</td><td>White</td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-uszlachetnienie.png\" alt=\"Print finish in the element panel\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Print finish in the element panel</figcaption></figure><p>Above the tiles you choose <b>how</b> to add the effect:</p><ul class=\"punkty\"><li><b>Color + effect</b> — the element prints in its own colors, and the effect goes on a copy of it exactly on top. This is how you put varnish on a photo or make colored text with foiling. This is the default for photos.</li><li><b>Effect only</b> — the element doesn't print with any ink, it's only the effect, e.g. text in pure gold. This is the default for shapes and text.</li></ul><p>In the same part there's <b>Spot ink</b> — filling the element with a spot color (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"22.1\">Spot colors</a>).</p><p class=\"ramka-instrukcji wskazowka\"><b>Tip.</b> A finish on very small text (below 6 pt) and thin lines (below 0.3 mm) may not reproduce well. <b>Preflight check</b> will remind you about it (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"20\">Preflight check</a>).</p><p><b>Cut-outs and folding pieces — die lines.</b> The same tiles also include technical lines. The element (shape, line, closed pen shape, a whole group) stops being a drawing and becomes a line for the bindery or a plotter:</p><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Tile</th><th>What it is</th><th>In the editor</th><th>Plate in PDF</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Cutting</b></td><td>A contour to cut with a knife or plotter. For a group — each of its shapes.</td><td>red line</td><td>CutContour</td></tr><tr><td><b>Crease</b></td><td>A fold line — pressed in so the card folds evenly.</td><td>blue dashed</td><td>Crease</td></tr><tr><td><b>Perforation</b></td><td>A tear-off line (coupon, ticket).</td><td>orange dotted</td><td>Perforation</td></tr><tr><td><b>Glue</b></td><td>An area where glue goes (box or envelope flap).</td><td>green area</td><td>Glue</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The lines are always visible on the page but don't print with ink — in a vector PDF they go onto separate plates (CutContour is recognized by most print & cut plotters). The <b>⋯</b> menu → <span class=\"ui\">Plotter file (SVG, current page)</span> downloads just the lines of the current page in millimeters — for Cricut, Silhouette or a print shop plotter (the line color says what to do). <b>Preflight check</b> makes sure it can be cut and folded: a cut from an open line, a crease or glue with no cut line, a crease or glue outside the cut-out piece. Ready-made die lines for envelopes, folders and boxes, with a 3D mockup, come from a section of the <b>Die line</b> type (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"10.4\">Section settings</a>).</p>"},{"id":"22.3","tytul":"Print finish view","html":"<p>In the editor and preview a print finish appears as an effect: the shine of varnish, metallic foil, the shadow of embossing, white on a checkerboard. View menu → <b>Show print finishes</b> turns the effect off (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"3.6\">Bottom-right corner and the view menu</a>).</p>"},{"id":"22.4","tytul":"Print finishes in the PDF file","html":"<p>In a vector file each print finish is a separate plate with <b>overprint</b> — it doesn't knock out the ink underneath. In the export window, in the <b>Advanced</b> part, choose <b>Print finishes</b>:</p><ul class=\"punkty\"><li><b>Separate plates in the same PDF</b> — the most common,</li><li><b>Separate PDF for each finish</b> — the main file without finishes and a separate file for each plate, in one ZIP,</li><li><b>Skip print finishes</b>.</li></ul><p>A page-image file has no separate plates — choose a vector file.</p>"},{"id":"22.5","tytul":"Project colors and color printing — for the whole project or for a section","html":"<p><span class=\"ui\">⋯</span> → <span class=\"ui\">Project colors…</span> gathers everything about colors in one window. At the top, in the <b>Applies to</b> field, you choose <b>Whole project</b> or a single section — e.g. a full-color cover with a black-and-white interior.</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-kolory-druku.png\" alt=\"The &quot;Project colors&quot; window for the &quot;Interior&quot; section\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>The &quot;Project colors&quot; window for the &quot;Interior&quot; section</figcaption></figure><p><b>Colors in the project</b> (or <b>in the section</b>) are the colors you use, with the number of uses. Tap a color and pick a new one — it changes everywhere in that scope: for the whole project on pages, in masters and in styles, for a section only on its pages. You can reverse the change with the usual <span class=\"ui\">Undo</span>.</p><p><b>Color printing</b> says which inks the print will use:</p><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>When</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Full color</b></td><td>Regular CMYK printing (default).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Grayscale</b></td><td>Printing with black ink only — colors and photos come out in shades of black.</td></tr><tr><td><b>B/W + 1 color</b></td><td>Black and one spot color, e.g. a calendar with red Sundays. You choose the <b>Mode spot color</b> below.</td></tr><tr><td><b>As in the project</b></td><td>Sections only: the section prints the same way as the whole project (default).</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>In the PDF each page is printed in its section's mode. In the color picker on a page of such a section, only shades of black and the mode color remain, and the section's colors are shown separately — <b>In this section</b>. The window shows colors outside the mode — <span class=\"ui\">Replace with nearest allowed</span> changes them to black or the mode color. The button in <b>Preflight check</b> does the same.</p><p>At the bottom of the window, in the <b>Print finishes</b> part, you can see which effects are in the project and on how many elements.</p><p class=\"ramka-instrukcji wskazowka\"><b>Tip.</b> Extended CMYK (CMYK with extra inks, e.g. orange, green and violet) is done by the print shop itself from your file — you don't need a separate palette.</p>"},{"id":"22.6","tytul":"Separations preview","html":"<p>In the project preview, <span class=\"ui\">Separations</span> shows the page split into the plates the print shop will print it with: <b>Cyan</b>, <b>Magenta</b>, <b>Yellow</b>, <b>Black</b>, spot colors and print finishes.</p><figure><img src=\"img/instrukcja/separacje.png\" alt=\"Separations preview: a spot color and spot UV varnish with overprint\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Separations preview: a spot color and spot UV varnish with overprint</figcaption></figure><ol class=\"kroki\"><li>Use the arrows above the image to move between pages.</li><li>Untick a plate to see the page without it; leave one and tick <b>Single plate in grayscale</b> — you'll see just that plate's ink (black = 100%).</li><li><b>Show print finish overlay</b> — print finishes as a hatched overlay on top of the ink (they print with overprint and don't knock out the ink underneath).</li><li><b>Highlight ink coverage above</b> <b>Coverage limit (%)</b> — places where the total ink exceeds the limit (usually 300% for coated paper) turn red. Below it is the highest coverage on the page.</li></ol><p><b>CMYK conversion</b>: <b>Simple</b> — like a vector file in CMYK without a profile (black and grays with black ink only; doesn't exceed 300%); <b>Coated paper (FOGRA39)</b> or <b>Uncoated paper (FOGRA47)</b> — the way the printer profile will convert it. After conversion with a profile, <b>Preflight check</b> will also point out element colors with ink coverage above 300% (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"20\">Preflight check</a>).</p>"}]},
  {"nr":"23","tytul":"Sharing via link","czesc":"Finished publication","ikona":"pdf","wstep":"A preview link for people without an account — a client, family, the print shop.","sekcje":[{"id":"23.1","tytul":"Creating a link","html":"<p>Someone with the link sees the pages as in the preview; they can't change anything and can't see your other projects. They can also open <span class=\"ui\">3D mockup</span> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"19.4\">3D mockup</a>): rotate it, flip through it and download an image — in one variant, the same as yours (the first saved shot, or with no shots — the type, placement and light from your mockup), without settings and without changing the type. The mockup updates in the link by itself, also in links created before it was added.</p><ol class=\"kroki\"><li>In the preview click <span class=\"ui\">Share</span> — or in the project list, <span class=\"ui\">⋯</span> on the card → <span class=\"ui\">Share via link…</span> (the project doesn't open, the pages are prepared in the background).</li><li><span class=\"ui\">Create link</span> — the link is ready right away; page images are prepared in the background (you can close the window). Anyone who opens the link in the meantime will see \"The preview is being prepared\", and the pages will appear for them by themselves.</li><li>Copy the link and send it.</li></ol><p>The link is short: <b>makietka.app/</b> and six characters, e.g. <code>makietka.app/k7m2xq</code>. Links created earlier get a short address by themselves the next time the project is opened; the old, long address still works.</p><div class=\"galeria\"><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-udostepnij.png\" alt=\"Share in the preview\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Share in the preview</figcaption></figure><figure><img src=\"img/instrukcja/udostepnianie.png\" alt=\"Share preview window\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Share preview window</figcaption></figure></div><p>A project with a link has a <b>Shared</b> badge in the project list — clicking it opens the same window.</p>"},{"id":"23.2","tytul":"Updating","html":"<p>The link updates by itself: a few seconds after your changes the app uploads the page images that have changed. The address stays the same. <span class=\"ui\">Update now</span> does it right away. Someone who has the link open doesn't need to refresh the page — new pages appear by themselves.</p><p>The page images are prepared by the app, so the link keeps up with changes while the project is open — in the editor or the preview, on any device. Meanwhile a bar is shown on screen: <b>Updating the link preview – don't close Makietka</b>, with a page counter. You can close the Share window — the pages keep being created, and the bar stays on screen. Just don't close the Makietka tab: the browser will ask if you're sure. If the tab does close, the link will finish by itself the next time you open the project.</p><p>When there are many pages to draw at once (from 40, e.g. the first link to a thick book or a watermark change), the images are drawn by the Makietka server, not your device. The bar then changes to <b>The link preview is being drawn on the server – you can close Makietka</b>: the link will finish by itself, even after you close the app. The server draws the same images as the app. Small changes (a few pages) are still drawn by the device, because it only takes a moment.</p>"},{"id":"23.3","tytul":"Link protection","html":"<p>The <b>Link protection</b> part:</p><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Password – no one can see the preview without it</b></td><td>Someone with the link first enters the <b>Link password</b>. After 10 wrong attempts the link stops accepting passwords for a quarter of an hour.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Valid until a chosen date</b></td><td>After the <b>Last day of validity</b> the link stops working.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>You can set protection when you create the link or at any time later: change the password, move the expiry date, add protection to an ordinary link or remove it. The link address doesn't change. You can also open the same window from the project list by clicking the link badge on the project card.</p><p><span class=\"ui\">Save protection</span> saves the settings. An empty password field with the password option ticked keeps the current one; unticking both boxes removes protection. A date change alone takes effect immediately. A new password, or turning protection on or off, prepares the link pages again from scratch — this takes a while, also from the project list (the project doesn't open).</p>"},{"id":"23.4","tytul":"Watermark","html":"<p>The <b>Watermark</b> part in the Share window: tick <b>Diagonal text on every page of the link</b>, type the <b>Watermark text</b> (empty field — \"PREVIEW\"), set the <b>Opacity</b> slider (5–50 %, default 12 % — faint text; a small preview under the slider shows it on a piece of the page right away) and click <span class=\"ui\">Save watermark</span>. The text is baked into the link's page images, also in the viewer's 3D mockup, so it can't be turned off on the viewer's side. The setting is saved immediately, and the link pages are then prepared in the background (a bar on screen, see 23.2) — you can close the window; unticking and saving removes the mark. The same applies to changing link protection. The watermark also works from the project list and doesn't change the printout or the PDF file.</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-znak-wodny.png\" alt=\"Watermark: text, Opacity slider and preview\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Watermark: text, Opacity slider and preview</figcaption></figure>"},{"id":"23.5","tytul":"3D mockup in the link","html":"<p>The <b>3D mockup in the link</b> part of the Share window decides what the viewer sees after tapping <b>3D mockup</b> in the link preview:</p><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Viewer can see the 3D mockup</b></td><td>Unticked — the link has only pages, no mockup.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Type</b></td><td><b>As in the project mockup</b> (type matched to the project) or a chosen type, e.g. a notepad instead of a book. Types that don't fit are lower down, in the <b>Doesn't match the project</b> group.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Shot</b></td><td>The first saved shot or one chosen from the list of saved shots (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"19.4\">3D mockup</a>): type, camera, light, background and page positions from that shot.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><b>Save mockup in the link</b> saves your choice in the project; an existing link updates in the background. Also works from the project list.</p>"},{"id":"23.6","tytul":"Revoking a link","html":"<p><span class=\"ui\">Revoke link</span> deletes the shared pages — the old address stops working immediately. Sharing again gives a new address.</p><p class=\"ramka-instrukcji uwaga\"><b>Note.</b> The link is long and random, so it can't be guessed, but anyone who gets it can pass it on. Set a password for confidential projects.</p>"}]},
  {"nr":"24","tytul":"Collaboration","czesc":"Together with others","ikona":"uzytkownik","wstep":"Working on a project together: invitations, roles and comments.","sekcje":[{"id":"24.1","tytul":"Inviting","html":"<p>Each person works in their own account, and changes show up for everyone live. Choose <span class=\"ui\">⋯</span> → <span class=\"ui\">Collaboration…</span> in the editor or on the project card.</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-menu-wspolpraca.png\" alt=\"Collaboration in the ⋯ menu\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Collaboration in the ⋯ menu</figcaption></figure><p>In the <b>Invite someone</b> section:</p><ol class=\"kroki\"><li>In the <b>Email or username</b> field, type an address or a name — after two letters, matching usernames appear.</li><li>Choose the <b>Role</b> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"24.2\">Roles</a>).</li><li>Click <span class=\"ui\">Invite</span>.</li></ol><p>The app sends an email with an invitation link. You can also copy the link and send it yourself. When you invite by name, you don't see that person's email address. If that person has turned off emails about invitations (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"27.6\">Notifications</a>), the app tells you — they will see the invitation in the app.</p><figure><img src=\"img/instrukcja/wspolpraca.png\" alt=\"The Collaboration window\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>The Collaboration window</figcaption></figure>"},{"id":"24.2","tytul":"Roles","html":"<div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Role</th><th>What they can do</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>can edit</b></td><td>Changes pages, data, masters and styles; exports PDF.</td></tr><tr><td><b>can view</b></td><td>Opens the project in preview only.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Owner</b></td><td>All of the above, plus invites, changes roles, revokes access, shares a preview link and deletes the project.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},{"id":"24.3","tytul":"Accepting an invitation","html":"<p>Click the link in the message. If you have an account, you'll also see the invitation in the app: an <span class=\"ui\">Invitations</span> button with a number appears in the project list. An accepted project is on your list with the owner's badge.</p><p>The invitation is accepted by whoever has the link — even from a different address than the one it was sent to. The Collaboration window always shows who is really in the project.</p><p>With notifications turned on (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"27.6\">Notifications</a>), you'll learn about an invitation right away.</p>"},{"id":"24.4","tytul":"Working live","html":"<ul class=\"punkty\"><li>Changes are merged element by element: when you fix the title and someone moves a photo, both changes stay. For the same element, the app will ask (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"2.5\">Several devices and undo</a>).</li><li>Circles with initials on the editor and preview bar show who else has the project open; point to a circle to see which page they're on.</li><li><span class=\"ui\">Undo</span> undoes only your own changes.</li></ul>"},{"id":"24.5","tytul":"Managing people","html":"<p>In the Collaboration window, the owner can, for each person:</p><ul class=\"punkty\"><li>change the <b>role</b> in the drop-down list,</li><li><span class=\"ui\">Revoke</span> — revokes access immediately; changes that person has already saved stay,</li><li>for a pending invitation: <span class=\"ui\">Copy invitation link</span>, <span class=\"ui\">Resend invitation email</span> and <span class=\"ui\">Revoke invitation</span>.</li></ul>"},{"id":"24.6","tytul":"Leaving a project and making a copy","html":"<p>A collaborator can <span class=\"ui\">Leave project</span> — in the <span class=\"ui\">⋯</span> menu on the card or in the Collaboration window. <span class=\"ui\">Make my own copy</span> creates your own project with the same content.</p>"},{"id":"24.7","tytul":"Comments","html":"<p>A comment is a note pinned to a spot on the page or to an element — like a sticky note on a printout — shown as an orange pin with a number. Everyone in the project can comment, including people with the <b>can view</b> role (in preview). Comments are not included in the PDF.</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-komentarz-narzedzie.png\" alt=\"The Comment tool and the Comments button with a counter\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>The Comment tool and the Comments button with a counter</figcaption></figure><p><b>Adding a comment</b></p><ol class=\"kroki\"><li>Click <span class=\"ui\">Comment</span> on the toolbar (in preview: <span class=\"ui\">Comments</span> → <span class=\"ui\">New comment</span>).</li><li>Click a spot on the page. If you select an element first, the comment is pinned to it right away.</li><li>Type the text and click <span class=\"ui\">Add comment</span> (or Ctrl+Enter). Esc cancels.</li></ol><p>Type <b>@</b> and the start of a name to mention a person in the project — the app suggests names.</p><p><b>The comment list</b></p><p><span class=\"ui\">Comments</span> on the editor's top bar and in preview shows the number of unresolved threads. Clicking a pin opens its thread.</p><figure><img src=\"img/instrukcja/komentarze.png\" alt=\"The Comments panel with a thread and a reply\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>The Comments panel with a thread and a reply</figcaption></figure><ul class=\"punkty\"><li>Filters: <b>Unresolved</b>, <b>All</b>, <b>Mine</b> (my threads, my replies and mentions of me).</li><li>A thread contains the replies, <span class=\"ui\">Reply</span> and <span class=\"ui\">Show on page</span>.</li><li><span class=\"ui\">Resolve</span> closes a finished thread and hides the pin; <span class=\"ui\">Reopen</span> (the <b>All</b> filter) brings it back.</li><li>You can edit your own comment with the <span class=\"ui\">Edit</span> pencil (marked \"(edited)\"); <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Enter</kbd> saves and sends.</li><li>A comment can be deleted by its author or the project owner — it disappears for everyone at once, along with the replies.</li><li>A comment on an element follows the element; if you delete the element, the pin stays in that spot. A thread from a deleted page stays in the list, labeled \"page deleted\".</li><li>Comments are added on pages, not on masters.</li></ul><p>Comments appear for everyone at once; a reply you're writing doesn't disappear when someone adds something. You'll also learn about new ones from notifications (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"27.6\">Notifications</a>).</p><p>An AI connected with a key also reads comments, replies to them and can resolve them after a fix (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"25.1\">What AI can do</a>).</p>"}]},
  {"nr":"25","tytul":"Working with AI","czesc":"Together with others","ikona":"uzytkownik","wstep":"An AI assistant can build and change your projects together with you.","sekcje":[{"id":"25.1","tytul":"What AI can do","html":"<p>An AI assistant (Claude, ChatGPT and others) connects to Makietka through a <b>connector</b>: an address with your key, which you add in the AI app.</p><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>AI can</th><th>AI cannot</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>create, copy and delete projects (with full access)</td><td>see projects outside the key</td></tr><tr><td>add, change and copy pages; add, change, align and evenly distribute elements; change styles and masters</td><td>change your account or password</td></tr><tr><td>fill in data tables and set up series</td><td>make a PDF file without Makietka — your browser renders it (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"25.5\">PDF file through AI</a>)</td></tr><tr><td>upload, insert and replace photos</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>read comments, reply to them and add its own (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"24.7\">Comments</a>)</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>request a PDF file or a PNG of a page and give you a download link (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"25.5\">PDF file through AI</a>)</td><td>draw pictures – only the <span class=\"ui\">AI assistant</span> in Makietka draws them (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"25.6\">AI Assistant in Makietka (own key)</a>); an AI app can upload a picture it drew itself</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>share the project or invite people (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"24\">Collaboration</a>)</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>upload fonts, use the Library or undo changes – undo them in the editor (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd>)</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>draw comic panels or place bubbles with one command – the <span class=\"ui\">AI assistant</span> in Makietka does that</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Write to the AI normally, as you would to a graphic designer: what you want to make, in what format, how many pages, what mood. If you have the project open, the changes appear in the editor after a moment. The AI's changes merge with yours like a collaborator's changes (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"24.4\">Working live</a>).</p><p>A project copy made through the connector has all pages, data and pictures. It gets its own copies of the account pictures the first time it is opened in Makietka; until then it uses the original's files, so deleting the original does not remove them.</p>"},{"id":"25.2","tytul":"A key for AI","html":"<p><span class=\"ui\">Account settings</span> → <b>Artificial intelligence</b> starts with the <b>Roughly how much AI costs</b> table (approximate prices with Google Gemini: improving and translating text and chatting – a fraction of a grosz, a comic script – about 1 grosz, an image – about 0.14 PLN as a draft, 0.41 PLN for print, 0.55 PLN best; a sample 3 × 3 panel comic – about 1.50 PLN) and the <b>Monthly limit for AI images and texts</b> field. The windows and the assistant don't ask for consent for every image – you see the cost next to the button and in the chat, and the limit makes sure you don't spend too much.</p><p>Below is a switch between four ways of working with AI: <b>Chat in Makietka</b>, <b>Connector in an AI app</b>, <b>Copy and paste</b> and <b>Chat that opens web pages</b>. Each tab explains how that way works, what you need and how much it costs, and below it shows your keys with an <span class=\"ui\">Edit</span> button and an <span class=\"ui\">Add key</span> button. You always add and edit a new key in a small window.</p><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>What for</th><th>Cost</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Chat in Makietka</b></td><td>Provider API keys (Anthropic, OpenAI, Gemini, DeepSeek, Mistral, xAI, OpenRouter, OpenAI-compatible) for the AI Assistant in the editor (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"25.6\">AI Assistant in Makietka (own key)</a>). In the window you choose the provider, paste the key (<span class=\"ui\">Where do I get a key?</span> leads to the provider's page) and click <span class=\"ui\">Save key</span>. The keys are encrypted with your keys password – nobody but you can read them, not even Makietka's administrators. This is also where you see the one-day \"Chat in Makietka\" keys the assistant uses to read the open project.</td><td>You pay the provider. Without a key: \"copy and paste\" mode (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"25.6\">AI Assistant in Makietka (own key)</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Connector in an AI app</b></td><td>The access key and connector address for Claude, ChatGPT and other apps with MCP (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"25.3\">Connecting in the AI app</a>). Sign-in connections also end up here (\"Connector: app name\").</td><td><b>Most likely a paid plan</b> of the AI app (ChatGPT – paid plans; Claude – one custom connector on the free plan).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Copy and paste</b></td><td>Any chat in the browser, including free ChatGPT, without a key: you choose a project and <span class=\"ui\">Open with the AI Assistant</span> – the project opens with the AI Assistant in \"copy and paste\" mode (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"25.6\">AI Assistant in Makietka (own key)</a>). The chat gets no key or address.</td><td>Nothing.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Chat that opens web pages</b></td><td>An access key and a ready-made prompt with addresses for a chat that opens any web pages by itself (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"25.7\">AI that can open web pages (no connector)</a>). ChatGPT doesn't do that – use \"Copy and paste\" then.</td><td>Makietka charges nothing.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-konto-ai.png\" alt=\"Artificial intelligence in account settings\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Artificial intelligence in account settings</figcaption></figure><p>The window for a new access key (connector and regular chat):</p><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Description (for you)</b></td><td>E.g. \"Claude – calendar\".</td></tr><tr><td><b>AI can</b></td><td><b>read and edit</b> or <b>read only</b>.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Valid for</b></td><td>1 day, 7 days, 30 days, 90 days, a year or no expiry.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Selected projects</b></td><td>The projects the key gives access to — yours and those shared with you.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Full access – all projects, including creating and deleting</b></td><td>Also projects added later. The AI always confirms deleting a project by its name.</td></tr><tr><td><b>When editing, AI may also</b></td><td>Five switches (all on by default): add, delete and reorder pages; change masters and styles; change data tables and series; upload, replace and delete images; make PDF and PNG files. When off – the AI gets a refusal with an explanation; this is enforced by the Makietka server, not the AI app. Shown only for a \"read and edit\" key.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><span class=\"ui\">Create key</span> shows the <b>Connector address</b> (or a ready-made prompt for a regular chat). When you have a keys password, Makietka remembers the key encrypted with that password; without a password – <span class=\"ui\">Remember key</span> in the window. <span class=\"ui\">Done</span> closes the window.</p><p>Each key in the list has <span class=\"ui\">Edit</span> – editing opens in a window, just like adding:</p><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>In the window</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><span class=\"ui\">Show key</span></td><td>Shows the connector address or the prompt for a regular chat (and the key itself) again – for copying. Works for keys remembered in the vault.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Range</b></td><td>Selected projects or full access — the address doesn't change.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Permissions</b></td><td>The same five switches as for a new key. The key list shows what a key can't do (e.g. \"without: pages, images\").</td></tr><tr><td><b>Validity</b></td><td>A new period starting today (1 day … a year or no expiry), also for a key that has already expired; \"no change\" keeps the current one.</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ui\">Save changes</span></td><td>Saves the range and validity.</td></tr><tr><td><span class=\"ui\">Revoke key</span></td><td>Turns the key off immediately.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>A provider API key in the edit window: <span class=\"ui\">Show key</span>, <b>Replace key</b> (you paste a new one and click <span class=\"ui\">Save</span>) and <span class=\"ui\">Delete key</span>. The <b>Model and connection</b> section (also when adding): <b>Default model</b> – empty means the provider's recommended model (after <span class=\"ui\">Test connection</span> you can see which; the list also suggests the cheapest and the best), for \"Other – OpenAI-compatible\" also the <b>API address</b>, and <span class=\"ui\">Test connection</span> – Makietka asks the provider for the list of models and tells you whether the key works, and if not – why (wrong key, no funds, limit, model unavailable, no internet).</p><p>When the keys are encrypted on this device, the Chat in Makietka section shows <span class=\"ui\">Unlock</span> – you enter the keys password once per device.</p><figure><img src=\"img/instrukcja/dostep-ai.png\" alt=\"Artificial intelligence settings\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Artificial intelligence settings</figcaption></figure>"},{"id":"25.3","tytul":"Connecting in the AI app","html":"<p><b>The simplest way – with sign-in (no copying of the key).</b> In the AI app, add a custom connector at the address <code>https://api.makietka.app/mcp</code> (without a key) and choose sign-in (OAuth). The app opens the Makietka page <b>Connect to Makietka</b>: you sign in with your password, see who is asking (e.g. \"Claude\"), choose projects (or all of them), reading only or changes too, and the validity – <span class=\"ui\">Allow</span> returns to the AI app, <span class=\"ui\">Deny</span> ends the connection. The connection is an ordinary access key \"Connector: app name\" – you can see it in the Artificial intelligence settings and revoke it. The AI app doesn't get your password. The second way – the address with a key from the window – works as before:</p><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>App</th><th>How to connect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Claude</b> (claude.ai, desktop and mobile app)</td><td><b>Settings → Connectors → Add custom connector</b>, name \"Makietka\", paste the address. In a conversation, turn on the connector in the tools menu.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Claude Desktop</b></td><td>In the Artificial intelligence window: <span class=\"ui\">Download the Claude Desktop extension</span>, double-click the downloaded file and paste the key. The extension also reads files from your disk.</td></tr><tr><td><b>ChatGPT</b></td><td><b>Settings → Apps → Add custom MCP server</b>, paste the address, authentication: none.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Claude Code</b> and other MCP programs</td><td>The command from the <b>Advanced</b> section in the Artificial intelligence settings.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>To check: write to the AI \"Show my projects in Makietka\".</p><p>The easiest way is to copy the \"Connect Makietka\" prompt and paste it into the conversation — the AI will recognize which app you're in and tell you what to click:</p><div class=\"prompt-instrukcji\"><div class=\"prompt-naglowek\"><b>The &quot;Connect Makietka&quot; prompt</b><button type=\"button\" class=\"przycisk\" data-kopiuj-prompt data-ikona=\"kopiuj\">Copy</button></div><p class=\"ciche\">Paste it into a conversation with the AI – it will guide you through connecting in your app.</p><pre>Pomóż mi podłączyć Makietkę do Ciebie jako łącznik (serwer MCP), krok po kroku.\n\nCo to jest Makietka: aplikacja w przeglądarce (https://makietka.app) do składania publikacji do druku – kalendarzy, planerów, notesów, zeszytów, komiksów. Ma strony, wzorce, style tekstu, tabele danych, serie powielanych stron, obrazy i eksport PDF.\n\nCo wiem o łączniku Makietki:\n- To zdalny serwer MCP (transport Streamable HTTP) z logowaniem OAuth: adres https://api.makietka.app/mcp (bez klucza) – aplikacja AI otworzy stronę Makietki, gdzie się loguję i wybieram projekty. Druga droga: adres z kluczem https://api.makietka.app/mcp/&lt;klucz&gt; (łącznik „bez logowania”).\n- Klucz tworzę sam w Makietce: Ustawienia konta → Sztuczna inteligencja. Wybieram: które projekty (albo pełny dostęp – wtedy AI może też tworzyć i usuwać projekty), czy AI tylko czyta, czy też zmienia, i ważność. Adres z kluczem widać tylko raz.\n- Narzędzia łącznika: lista projektów, nowy projekt i usunięcie projektu (przy pełnym dostępie), przegląd projektu, czytanie i zmiana stron, elementów, wzorców, stylów, tabel danych i serii, wgrywanie obrazów (adres https, link z Dysku Google albo Dropboxa, kod SVG, małe pliki kawałkami), wstawianie obrazów na strony, link do wgrania zdjęć przez przeglądarkę, kontrola przed drukiem.\n- Opis dla AI: https://makietka.app/ai/api.md, model danych projektu: https://makietka.app/ai/format-makietka.md.\n- Dla Claude Desktop jest też rozszerzenie https://makietka.app/ai/mcp/makietka.mcpb – działa na komputerze i czyta pliki oraz foldery z dysku.\n- Claude Code: claude mcp add --transport http makietka https://api.makietka.app/mcp/&lt;klucz&gt;\n\nTwoje zadanie:\n1. Ustal, w jakiej aplikacji teraz rozmawiamy (claude.ai w przeglądarce, aplikacja Claude na telefonie, Claude Desktop, Claude Code, ChatGPT, Cursor albo inna) i czy na moim planie da się w niej dodać własny łącznik MCP. Jeśli nie wiesz – zapytaj mnie.\n2. Poprowadź mnie po kolei, co kliknąć, żeby dodać łącznik o nazwie „Makietka” z moim adresem. Nie proś mnie o wklejenie klucza do tej rozmowy – wkleję go sam w ustawieniach.\n3. Jeśli ta aplikacja albo mój plan nie obsługuje łączników, powiedz to wprost i zaproponuj najprostszą drogę (inna aplikacja, Claude Desktop z rozszerzeniem, Claude Code).\n4. Po podłączeniu sprawdź, czy widzisz narzędzie „projekty”, i pokaż mi listę moich projektów.\nPisz prosto, po polsku, jeden krok na raz i czekaj, aż powiem, że zrobione.</pre></div><p class=\"ramka-instrukcji wskazowka\"><b>Tip.</b> Connectors are usually part of paid AI app plans. On a phone you can usually only use connectors added earlier on a computer.</p>"},{"id":"25.4","tytul":"Photos through AI","html":"<ul class=\"punkty\"><li><b>ChatGPT</b> takes photos dropped into the conversation directly.</li><li><b>Claude</b> uploads small graphics from the conversation by itself; <b>Claude Desktop with the extension</b> — files and folders from your disk.</li><li><b>An upload link always works:</b> ask the AI for a link — it opens this project with the Project images window, you drop photos or a ZIP there, and the AI sees them and carries on.</li><li>The AI can also download an image from a web address or from a Google Drive or Dropbox link shared with \"anyone with the link\", and draw its own SVG graphic.</li></ul><p>The AI doesn't delete a photo that's placed on a page — it replaces it with a new file in the same spot.</p>"},{"id":"25.5","tytul":"PDF file through AI","html":"<p>Ask the AI e.g. \"Make a print-ready PDF\" or \"Show the cover as an image\". Makietka renders the file with the same code as <span class=\"ui\">Export to PDF…</span> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"21\">Export to PDF and PNG</a>):</p><ul class=\"punkty\"><li><b>Makietka is open</b> (any tab, on a computer or a phone) — it takes the request by itself. A bar \"AI requested a PDF export…\" appears at the top, and after a moment a notification with a <span class=\"ui\">Download</span> button. The AI gets a download link for the file and the notes from the preflight check.</li><li><b>Makietka is closed</b> — the Makietka server makes the file (in about a minute), and the AI gives you the download link when you ask again. You don't have to open anything. If the server can't manage (e.g. the daily limit), the AI gives you a link to the app — once you open it, the file is made automatically.</li></ul><p>The download link works without signing in for 7 days, then the file disappears from the account. The AI can request the whole project, one section or selected pages, a print file (with bleed and crop marks, also PDF/X) or one for reading on screen, a booklet for folding, or a PNG of a single page.</p>"},{"id":"25.6","tytul":"AI Assistant in Makietka (own key)","html":"<p>No separate AI app needed: in the editor, <span class=\"ui\">AI assistant</span> on the top bar (next to <span class=\"ui\">Preview</span>; on a phone <span class=\"ui\">AI ▾</span> → <b>AI assistant…</b>, and also the <b>⋯</b> menu → <span class=\"ui\">AI assistant (own key)…</span>) opens a chat next to the page (on a phone — a window). Write what to do, e.g. “lay this page out in two columns” or “add a title and a frame for a photo”.</p><ol class=\"kroki\"><li><b>Provider</b> — Anthropic (Claude), OpenAI (ChatGPT), Google Gemini, DeepSeek, Mistral, xAI (Grok), OpenRouter (hundreds of models from one account) or <b>Other – OpenAI-compatible API</b> (with its own address). Next to the name you see what it can do: <b>chat and project changes</b> – every provider reads and changes the project with Makietka's tools; <b>and images</b> – Google Gemini also draws images.</li><li><b>API key</b> — from the chosen provider (<span class=\"ui\">Where do I get a key?</span> leads to their page). With OpenRouter, instead of pasting a key, <span class=\"ui\">Sign in with OpenRouter</span> is enough — you sign in and approve access on their side.</li><li><b>Model</b> — leave it empty: Makietka picks the provider's recommended model by itself (fast and sensible; the field shows which). The list also suggests <b>Cheapest</b> and <b>Best – more expensive</b>, and advanced users can type any name.</li><li><b>In this project, AI can</b> — <b>read and edit</b> or <b>read only</b>.</li></ol><p>Write what to do (Enter sends, Shift+Enter — new line). The AI uses the same tools as the connector (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"25.1\">What AI can do</a>); the chat shows which ones (🔧), and the changes appear in the editor right away. <span class=\"ui\">Stop</span> halts after the current step. The sliders icon in the header shows and hides the settings.</p><ul class=\"punkty\"><li><b>The keys are encrypted and stored in your account.</b> With the first key, Makietka asks for an <b>AI keys password</b> (at least 8 characters, preferably different from your account password). The browser encrypts the key with it (AES-256) and only then saves it to your account – the server holds only the encrypted record. Without your password nobody can read it, <b>not even Makietka's administrators</b>. On each new device you enter the password once; after that the device remembers it. Lost password: in the password window, <span class=\"ui\">Forgot it – delete saved keys</span> and paste the keys again.</li><li>The conversation goes from your browser straight to the provider, and you settle the cost with them.</li><li>The AI sees only the open project — through a one-day \"Chat in Makietka\" access key, visible in <span class=\"ui\">Account settings</span> → <b>Artificial intelligence</b> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"25.2\">A key for AI</a>); you can revoke it there.</li><li>Changing the provider or model starts a new conversation.</li></ul><p><b>No key and no fees – \"copy and paste\".</b> The provider <b>Any chat without a key – copy and paste</b> works with any AI chat in the browser, including free ones (ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini, Copilot…), without an API key:</p><ol class=\"kroki\"><li>Write what to do in the Makietka chat. Makietka copies a ready-made prompt to the clipboard: rules, a list of tools, the project's state and your task (you can also see it in the conversation, with a <span class=\"ui\">Copy</span> button).</li><li>Paste it into the AI chat. It will answer briefly and add a <code>makietka</code> code block with commands.</li><li>Paste its whole answer into the Makietka chat and send it – Makietka runs the commands (🔧) and copies the results.</li><li>Paste the results to the AI chat – it will see what worked and, if needed, send further commands.</li></ol><p>The permissions are the same as with your own key (a one-day \"Chat in Makietka\" key, reading or changes).</p><p>The answer appears in the conversation live, as the AI writes it (Anthropic, OpenAI and compatible, Gemini).</p><p><b>Photos in the conversation.</b> The paper clip next to the message field attaches photos (PNG, JPEG, WebP, GIF, up to 5 MB). The AI sees them (the model must support images), and Makietka immediately uploads the original to the project (the \"Project images\" window), so the AI can put it on a page. In \"copy and paste\" mode the photo also goes to the project, but the browser chat won't see it.</p><p><b>When something doesn't work,</b> the assistant tells you in plain words what happened: the provider rejected the key, no funds in the provider account, too many requests, model unavailable or without tool support, no connection. When the provider is overloaded, Makietka retries the request twice by itself before reporting an error.</p><p><b>Consent for risky changes.</b> Before the assistant deletes pages, 3 or more elements at once, images or the project, changes 3 or more pages in one save, or fully replaces masters, styles, tables or series, a question appears in the conversation: \"AI wants to … Allow?\" with the buttons <span class=\"ui\">Allow</span>, <span class=\"ui\">Always allow in this conversation</span> and <span class=\"ui\">Don't allow</span>. After a refusal nothing changes, and the AI is told you didn't allow it and asks what to do instead. The same goes for \"copy and paste\" mode: the question appears when the pasted chat answer contains such a change.</p><p><b>Images and comics in the chat.</b> The assistant can also draw: an image from a description into a frame or onto the page (like <span class=\"ui\">AI ▾</span> → <b>Image from a description…</b>, <span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"7.11\">AI image</a>), comic page panels from the script and cards (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"10.13\">Comic</a>), bubbles from the script and a cost estimate. Google Gemini draws the images with the Gemini key from your vault, even when you chat with another provider. The assistant doesn't ask for consent for every image – in the chat it writes what it is drawing and roughly how much it costs, e.g. “🎨 Drawing the image “Tulips in the garden” – about 0.14 PLN”. The safety net is the <b>monthly spending limit</b> in the AI settings: once it is exceeded, the assistant draws nothing. The assistant draws in <b>Draft</b> quality unless you ask for better.</p><p>Example requests:</p><ul class=\"punkty\"><li>“Add the title Spring in the garden on this page, a frame below it, and draw tulips in it.”</li><li>“Draw the panels of page 2 of the comic and place the bubbles.”</li><li>“How much will it cost to draw 10 images for print?”</li><li>“Translate all the texts on this page into English.”</li><li>“In the picture on page 2, give the girl a red hat.” – the assistant fixes the current picture; the rest stays.</li></ul><p>Changes and images appear in the editor during the conversation – the chat doesn't have to be closed.</p>"},{"id":"25.7","tytul":"AI that can open web pages (no connector)","html":"<p>The connector address (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"25.2\">A key for AI</a>) <b>opened like an ordinary web page</b> shows a description of the access and a list of tools in plain text. An AI that can read pages from the web (e.g. free ChatGPT or Claude with search) can therefore work without adding a connector: in the <b>Artificial intelligence</b> settings, right after creating a key, <span class=\"ui\">Copy for a regular chat</span> gives you a ready-made prompt with the address – paste it into the conversation; the AI will show your projects and ask what to do. It calls tools by opening the addresses <code>…/n/&lt;tool&gt;?parameter=value</code> (complex parameters as <code>?json=…</code>); the response is the result or a description of the error. The permissions come from the key – a <b>read only</b> key won't change anything.</p><p class=\"ramka-instrukcji uwaga\"><b>Note.</b> Some chats open only addresses they got from the user or found themselves – not the ones they build. Then reading works (links from the description), and for changes use \"copy and paste\" mode or the connector.</p><p class=\"ramka-instrukcji uwaga\"><b>Note.</b> ChatGPT opens only addresses you paste (not the ones it builds itself, nor links found on a page). That's why the prompt contains ready-made addresses for reading: projects, project overview and format description – it will read, but usually won't make changes. For changes without a paid plan, use the AI Assistant in \"copy and paste\" mode (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"25.6\">AI Assistant in Makietka (own key)</a>).</p>"},{"id":"25.8","tytul":"Security","html":"<p>The AI gets at most your permissions: in a project you can only view, the key only reads, and when the owner revokes your access, the key loses it immediately.</p><p class=\"ramka-instrukcji uwaga\"><b>Note.</b> The connector address contains the key — whoever has it has access to the selected projects. Don't publish it, give the shortest validity that's enough, and revoke the key once the work is done.</p>"}]},
  {"nr":"26","tytul":"Example: a comic with AI","czesc":"Together with others","ikona":"uzytkownik","sekcje":[{"id":"26.1","tytul":"From idea to file","html":"<p>An A5 booklet with a cover: the AI lays out the panels and speech bubbles, you provide the drawings and make the decisions.</p><ol class=\"kroki\"><li>Create a key with <b>full access</b> and <b>read and edit</b> — the AI will create a new project (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"25.2\">A key for AI</a>).</li><li>Copy the \"Print-ready comic\" prompt (below), add your idea and send it. The AI first shows a page plan — correct it before it starts.</li><li>Open the new project — you'll see pages, panels and speech bubbles being added.</li><li>Ask the AI for an upload link and drop in your drawings (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"25.4\">Photos through AI</a>). The AI will put them into the panels.</li><li>Fix whatever you like: the text in the bubbles (the \"Speech bubble\" style changes all of them at once), the cropping, the page order.</li><li>Make the cover as a separate section with a spine (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"10.6\">Cover with a spine</a>).</li><li><span class=\"ui\">Preflight check</span>, then a PDF <b>For print</b> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"20\">Preflight check</a>, <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"21\">Export to PDF and PNG</a>).</li></ol><div class=\"prompt-instrukcji\"><div class=\"prompt-naglowek\"><b>The &quot;Print-ready comic&quot; prompt</b><button type=\"button\" class=\"przycisk\" data-kopiuj-prompt data-ikona=\"kopiuj\">Copy</button></div><p class=\"ciche\">Copy it, add your idea at the end and send it to an AI with Makietka connected.</p><pre>Korzystając z łącznika Makietka, przygotuj ze mną komiks do druku.\n\n1. Najpierw zaproponuj plan: tytuł, liczbę stron (domyślnie 8 stron środka + okładka), na każdej stronie liczbę kadrów i co się w nich dzieje. Poczekaj, aż zatwierdzę.\n2. Potem utwórz nowy projekt „Komiks – &lt;tytuł&gt;”: format A5 (148×210 mm), spad 3 mm, marginesy 8 mm. Środek w rozkładówkach, okładka jako osobna sekcja.\n3. Na stronach ułóż kadry: prostokąty z czarnym obrysem 0,6 mm i odstępem 4 mm między kadrami, w obrębie marginesów. W kadrach dymki (elipsy albo prostokąty z zaokrągleniem, białe wypełnienie, czarny obrys) z tekstem 9 pt, wyśrodkowanym. Narrację umieść w prostokątnych ramkach u góry kadru.\n4. Zrób styl tekstu „Dymek” i „Narracja”, żebym mógł je potem zmienić naraz.\n5. Rysunków nie wymyślaj na siłę: jeśli nie możesz ich narysować, w każdym kadrze zostaw szary prostokąt z krótkim opisem sceny. Ja wgram rysunki – daj mi link do wgrania obrazów, a potem wstaw je w kadry z dopasowaniem „wypełnij”.\n6. Dodaj numery stron we wzorcu (pole {nr}) na dole, po zewnętrznej stronie.\n7. Na koniec uruchom kontrolę przed drukiem i powiedz, co poprawić.\n\nMój pomysł na komiks: &lt;opisz tutaj bohaterów, historię i nastrój&gt;</pre></div><p class=\"ramka-instrukcji wskazowka\"><b>Tip.</b> A saddle-stitched booklet has a page count divisible by 4 (8, 12, 16…). Prepare the drawings for print at no less than 300 dpi at the panel size.</p>"}]},
  {"nr":"27","tytul":"Account","czesc":"Account, devices and help","ikona":"motyw","wstep":"Your account stores your projects, fonts and elements, so you have them on every device.","sekcje":[{"id":"27.1","tytul":"Creating an account","html":"<p>On the sign-in screen, choose <span class=\"ui\">New account</span> and enter:</p><ul class=\"punkty\"><li><b>Username</b> — 3–30 characters: letters, digits, <code>_</code>, <code>.</code> and <code>-</code>. Others use this name to invite you to a project (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"24.1\">Inviting</a>).</li><li><b>Email</b>.</li><li><b>Password</b> — at least 8 characters, with a lowercase and an uppercase letter, a digit and a special character.</li></ul><p>If you get a “Confirm your account” email, click the button in it — only then can you sign in.</p><div class=\"galeria\"><figure><img src=\"img/instrukcja/rejestracja.png\" alt=\"New account\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>New account</figcaption></figure><figure><img src=\"img/instrukcja/rejestracja-wyslano.png\" alt=\"Check your inbox\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Check your inbox</figcaption></figure></div>"},{"id":"27.2","tytul":"Signing in and password","html":"<p>In the <b>Email or username</b> field, enter your account address or your username (upper or lower case doesn't matter), then your password. After 10 failed sign-in attempts with the same username, you have to wait fifteen minutes — with your email address you can sign in right away.</p><p><span class=\"ui\">Forgot your password?</span> sends a link for setting a new password. The link works once; when it expires, ask for a new one.</p><figure><img src=\"img/instrukcja/logowanie.png\" alt=\"Sign in\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Sign in</figcaption></figure>"},{"id":"27.3","tytul":"Account settings","html":"<p>Click the circle with your initial in the top-right corner of the project list and choose <span class=\"ui\">Account settings</span>.</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-menu-konta.png\" alt=\"Account menu\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Account menu</figcaption></figure><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Row</th><th>What you change</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Username</b></td><td>The name others use to invite you to projects.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Email</b></td><td>Your account address. <span class=\"ui\">Change email</span> sends a confirmation link to the new address; until you click it, you sign in with the old one. <span class=\"ui\">Enter a different address</span> replaces the pending address with a new one.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Password</b></td><td><span class=\"ui\">Change password</span>.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Theme</b></td><td>How the app looks (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"27.4\">Theme</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Notifications</b></td><td>Notifications on this device (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"27.6\">Notifications</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Artificial intelligence</b></td><td>Keys for AI assistants (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"25.2\">A key for AI</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Brand kit</b></td><td>Brand colors, fonts, text styles and logo — available in every project (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"27.9\">Brand kit</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Storage</b></td><td>How much space your files and projects take up (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"27.5\">Storage and limits</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Delete account</b></td><td>Deleting your account (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"27.7\">Deleting your account</a>).</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><span class=\"ui\">Sign out everywhere</span> (in the <b>Sign in</b> view) signs out all devices at once — e.g. after working on someone else's computer.</p><figure><img src=\"img/instrukcja/konto.png\" alt=\"Account settings\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Account settings</figcaption></figure>"},{"id":"27.4","tytul":"Theme","html":"<p>The theme button is at the top of the screen (in the project list, in account settings, on the sign-in screen) and in the editor's <span class=\"ui\">⋯</span> menu. It shows the icon of the selected mode and opens a menu: <b>System</b>, <b>Light</b> or <b>Dark</b>. You can choose the same in <span class=\"ui\">Account settings</span> → <b>Theme</b>. The theme only changes how the app looks — publication pages are always on white paper.</p><figure><img src=\"img/instrukcja/motyw.png\" alt=\"Dark theme\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Dark theme</figcaption></figure><h4>Language</h4><p>Next to the theme button there is a button with a flag – the <b>app language</b>: Polish (Polski), English or Ukrainian (Українська). In the editor, the same choice is in the <span class=\"ui\">⋯</span> menu → <b>Język / Language / Мова</b>. The first time, Makietka uses your browser's language, then it remembers your choice on this device. The language only changes the app's labels — project content (text on pages, names, data in tables) stays as it was entered. The help (book button) and its screenshots also follow the chosen language.</p>"},{"id":"27.5","tytul":"Storage and limits","html":"<p><span class=\"ui\">Account settings</span> → <b>Storage</b> shows bars for your account limits:</p><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Limit</th><th>How much</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Account files (images, fonts, link previews)</td><td>2 GB</td></tr><tr><td>A single file</td><td>50 MB</td></tr><tr><td>Projects</td><td>100</td></tr><tr><td>Images in one project</td><td>1000</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Below is a list of your projects: how much space each takes, how many images and pages it has, when it was last changed. <span class=\"ui\">Open</span> opens the project, <span class=\"ui\">Show images</span> — its images window (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"7.5\">The Project images window</a>). Other people's projects that you have access to are listed separately and don't count toward your limits.</p><p>From 80% of a limit, the app warns you — here and when you upload images. <span class=\"ui\">Clean up…</span> shows how much space it will free and deletes only files no project uses any more: old versions of replaced photos, files of deleted projects and of disabled links. Files from the last two days stay.</p>"},{"id":"27.6","tytul":"Notifications","html":"<p>Notifications tell you right away about invitations and comments — even when the app is closed. In <span class=\"ui\">Account settings</span> → <b>Notifications</b>, turn on <b>On this device</b> (the browser will ask for permission) and choose what you want to hear about: invitations, replies to your invitations, role and access changes, comments and mentions (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"24.7\">Comments</a>). Clicking a notification opens the right project or thread.</p><p>You turn notifications on separately on each device. On iPhone and iPad they only work in the installed app (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"28.4\">Installing the app</a>).</p><p><b>By email</b> — a switch for the whole account: an email when someone invites you to a project or shares it with you. When it's off, invitation emails don't arrive, and the invitation waits in the app (<span class=\"ui\">Projects</span> → <span class=\"ui\">Invitations</span>). Account emails (address confirmation, new password) can't be turned off.</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-powiadomienia.png\" alt=\"Notifications in account settings\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Notifications in account settings</figcaption></figure>"},{"id":"27.7","tytul":"Deleting your account","html":"<p><span class=\"ui\">Account settings</span> → <b>Delete account</b> deletes your account and all your projects. Before you do that, export the projects you want to keep (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"2.6\">Project file</a>).</p><p class=\"ramka-instrukcji uwaga\"><b>Note.</b> Deleting your account can't be undone.</p>"},{"id":"27.8","tytul":"Contact","html":"<p>A question, an idea or a bug? <span class=\"ui\">Contact</span> is in the account menu and below the sign-in form. <span class=\"ui\">Attach files</span> adds a screenshot, a project file or a PDF (up to 5 files, 10 MB in total). You can also write to kontakt@makietka.app.</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-kontakt.png\" alt=\"Contact form with an attachment\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Contact form with an attachment</figcaption></figure>"},{"id":"27.9","tytul":"Brand kit","html":"<p>You save the colors, fonts and logo of your company (or school, parish, club) once — in your account — and have them in every project: <span class=\"ui\">Account settings</span> → <b>Brand kit</b> → <span class=\"ui\">Edit brand kit…</span> (or Library → <b>Brand</b>).</p><p><b>Several kits</b> — e.g. one for each client, school or publisher. At the top of the brand window is the <b>Kit</b> list: <span class=\"ui\">New kit…</span> (empty), <span class=\"ui\">Copy…</span> (with the current kit's colors, fonts, styles and logo), <span class=\"ui\">Rename…</span>, <span class=\"ui\">Delete</span>. Changes to a kit are saved when you switch to another one. The choice in account settings is the <b>kit in use</b> — the brand of projects that don't have their own. A choice made with a project open (the brand window from the Library or from the ⋯ menu) is remembered by the project: its colors, fonts and Library show that kit, while other projects keep theirs. <span class=\"ui\">Apply brand to project…</span> does the same — in its window you choose which kit to apply.</p><ul class=\"punkty\"><li><b>Brand colors</b> — with a name, e.g. “Brand navy”. The <b>Pantone / CMYK</b> switch turns a color into a spot color, printed with a separate ink (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"22.1\">Spot colors</a>).</li><li><b>Brand fonts</b> — one for headings, one for body text.</li><li><b>Text styles</b> — <span class=\"ui\">Add preset styles</span> adds Title, Subtitle, Heading 1–4, Paragraph, Highlighted paragraph, Caption, Quote, List, Footnote, Footer / running head and Label in the brand fonts and color; <span class=\"ui\">Custom style</span> — more, with no limit. Each style has a font, size, weight, italic, line spacing, letter spacing, color and letter case.</li><li><b>Logo</b> — PNG, JPG, WebP or SVG; you insert it from the Library.</li></ul><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-marka-style.png\" alt=\"Brand text styles\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Brand text styles</figcaption></figure><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-marka.png\" alt=\"Brand kit\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Brand kit</figcaption></figure><p>The brand is at hand in the color window (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"6.7\">Choosing a color</a>), at the top of the font list (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"5.8\">Fonts</a>) and in the Library (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"4.8\">Library</a>).</p><ul class=\"punkty\"><li><b>color picker</b> — the <b>Brand</b> part above “Used in project” (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"6.7\">Choosing a color</a>),</li><li><b>font list</b> — the <b>Brand</b> group at the very top (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"5.8\">Fonts</a>),</li><li><b>Library</b> — the <b>Brand</b> category: logo, <span class=\"ui\">Apply brand to project…</span> and <span class=\"ui\">Edit brand kit…</span> (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"4.8\">Library</a>),</li><li><span class=\"ui\">⋯</span> → <span class=\"ui\">Apply brand to project…</span> — replaces the colors and fonts of the whole project with brand ones (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"3.3\">The ⋯ menu</a>).</li></ul><p><b>Apply brand to project.</b> The window shows every color and every font in the project with a suggestion: a color gets the closest brand color (black, white and grays stay), the most common font gets the body font, the others get the heading font. In the lists you can change a suggestion or choose “no change”. The <b>Text styles</b> part copies the brand styles into the project styles (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"5.4\">Styles</a>): a project style with the same name (e.g. “Title”) gets the brand look and stays linked to its texts — they change right away — and missing styles are added. <span class=\"ui\">Apply</span> changes pages, masters and styles at once; you undo it all with a single <span class=\"ui\">Undo</span> (<kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Z</kbd>).</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-marka-zastosuj.png\" alt=\"Apply brand to project\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Apply brand to project</figcaption></figure>"}]},
  {"nr":"28","tytul":"Phone and tablet","czesc":"Account, devices and help","ikona":"motyw","wstep":"How to work on a touch screen and how to install the app.","sekcje":[{"id":"28.1","tytul":"Gestures","html":"<p>On a touch screen:</p><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Gesture</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>One finger</b></td><td>Selects and moves elements.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Two fingers</b></td><td>Move the view and zoom.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Quick swipe sideways</b></td><td>On an empty part of the page, when the whole page fits on the screen: left – next page, right – previous page (with spreads, the whole spread). A slow drag selects with a frame as usual.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Double tap</b></td><td>Like a double click: typing in text and table cells, entering a group, editing points.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Press and hold</b></td><td>Shows a tip, e.g. the description of the save cloud.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>In the preview, pinching zooms, and in a presentation, swiping sideways turns the page (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"19\">Preview</a>).</p>"},{"id":"28.2","tytul":"Editor on a phone","html":"<p>On a phone, the editor works in portrait. You open the <b>Pages</b>, <b>Properties</b>, <b>Layers</b> and <b>Warnings</b> panels with buttons at the bottom of the screen. A panel slides up from the bottom, and the view moves by itself so the selected element is visible above the panel.</p><p><b>Handle</b> (the bar at the top of the panel): drag it up to enlarge the panel, down to shrink or close it. Tapping the handle shrinks an enlarged panel to its normal height, and closes a normal one. Tapping the panel's button again also closes it.</p><p>Less common tools — polygon, drawing, pen, comment, copy formatting — are under <span class=\"ui\">⋯</span> at the end of the insert bar (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"3.4\">Insert bar</a>). There are no rulers or frame cropping on a phone: you enter guides as numbers (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"12.4\">Rulers and guides</a>), and crop images with numbers in the properties (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"7.2\">Cropping</a>).</p><p>A phone held sideways shows a “Not enough vertical space” screen — turn it to portrait.</p><figure class=\"telefon\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/telefon.png\" alt=\"Editor on a phone\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Editor on a phone</figcaption></figure>"},{"id":"28.3","tytul":"Bottom sheets","html":"<p>On a phone and tablet, windows (Data, Masters, Export to PDF, Project images and others) slide up from the bottom of the screen. Instead of an X they have a <b>handle</b> — drag it down or tap it to close; tapping the dimmed background also closes them. The window's main button is at the bottom, always visible. Dropdown lists and color and date pickers, which also slide up from the bottom on a phone, have the same handle — tapping outside them or on the handle hides them. Small menus (⋯, view) drop down below their button and hide when you tap next to them.</p><figure class=\"telefon\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/telefon-zaslonka.png\" alt=\"Window on a phone\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Window on a phone</figcaption></figure>"},{"id":"28.4","tytul":"Installing the app","html":"<p>Installed Makietka opens from the home screen like a regular app — full screen, without the browser bar.</p><div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>How to install</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Android</b> (Chrome)</td><td><span class=\"ui\">Install the app</span> in the account menu or in the browser menu ⋮.</td></tr><tr><td><b>iPhone and iPad</b> (Safari)</td><td><span class=\"ui\">Install the app</span> shows the steps: <b>Share → Add to Home Screen</b>.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Computer</b> (Chrome, Edge)</td><td>The install icon in the address bar or <span class=\"ui\">Install the app</span>. In Safari on a Mac: <b>File → Add to Dock</b>.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The installed app on a computer opens <code>.makietka</code> files with a double click.</p>"},{"id":"28.5","tytul":"Full screen","html":"<p><span class=\"ui\">Full screen</span> (four corners) in the project list, in the editor and in the preview hides the browser's bars; <kbd>Esc</kbd> or the same button brings them back. On iPhone the browser doesn't allow full screen — there you get it by installing the app (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"28.4\">Installing the app</a>).</p>"},{"id":"28.6","tytul":"Longer tasks","html":"<p>When something takes more than a moment — e.g. opening a large project, exporting a PDF, printing at home, uploading images — a card with a progress bar appears at the top, and when it's known how much is left, with a counter, e.g. “17 of 48”. You can stop a PDF export with the <span class=\"ui\">Cancel</span> button. Page thumbnails of a large project are drawn in the background (a thin bar in the Pages panel) — you can keep working normally in the meantime.</p><figure class=\"kadr\"><img src=\"img/instrukcja/k-postep.png\" alt=\"Progress card during PDF export\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Progress card during PDF export</figcaption></figure>"}]},
  {"nr":"29","tytul":"Keyboard shortcuts","czesc":"Account, devices and help","ikona":"motyw","sekcje":[{"id":"29.1","tytul":"List of shortcuts","html":"<p>On a Mac, press <kbd>⌘</kbd> instead of <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>. In the editor, the <kbd>?</kbd> key shows this list. While you're typing text, only the shortcuts from the “Text” group work.</p><p><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>K</kbd> opens the command palette: type a few letters of a command's name and press <kbd>Enter</kbd>.</p><div data-wstaw=\"skroty\"></div>"}]},
  {"nr":"30","tytul":"Glossary","czesc":"Account, devices and help","ikona":"motyw","sekcje":[{"id":"30.1","tytul":"Printing and print-ready files","html":"<div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>CMYK</b></td><td>The four printing inks: cyan, magenta, yellow, black. The print shop converts on-screen colors (RGB) to CMYK.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Dpi</b></td><td>The number of image dots per inch at print size. A photo for print should have at least 300 dpi.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Imposition</b></td><td>Arranging pages on a print sheet so that after folding and trimming they're in the right order (e.g. a booklet).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Spot color (Pantone)</b></td><td>A ready-mixed ink of an exact shade, printed separately alongside CMYK.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Overprint</b></td><td>Ink printed on top of the ink below without knocking it out — important for black text and finishes.</td></tr><tr><td><b>PDF/X-4</b></td><td>A PDF variant for print shops: embedded fonts, a stored color profile, transparency without flattening.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Ink coverage</b></td><td>The total of CMYK inks in one spot (in %). Too much (usually over 300%) means the ink won't dry.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Color profile (ICC)</b></td><td>A description of the print shop's press and paper; when converting to CMYK it gives colors as close as possible to the intended ones.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Resolution</b></td><td>See Dpi.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Separations</b></td><td>Separate printing plates for each ink (C, M, Y, K, spot colors, finishes).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Bleed</b></td><td>A few millimeters beyond the edge of the page; backgrounds and photos extend all the way there, and the print shop trims it off.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Imposed copy (n-up)</b></td><td>One copy of a small item (business card, sticker) on a print sheet – many of them fit on one sheet.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Vector</b></td><td>A drawing described by shapes, not pixels — sharp at any zoom (text, shapes, vector PDF).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Crop marks</b></td><td>Lines in the PDF file that show the print shop where to cut.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},{"id":"30.2","tytul":"Print finishing","html":"<div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>White underprint</b></td><td>A layer of white ink under the colors — needed when printing on transparent, colored or metallic material.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Score, scoring</b></td><td>A crease pressed into card (e.g. a cover) so that it folds evenly and doesn't crack.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Fold</b></td><td>The line along which a sheet is folded (e.g. a trifold leaflet); on card it's preceded by a score.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Spot varnish</b></td><td>Glossy varnish only on selected areas of the page, e.g. on the title.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Perforation</b></td><td>A line of small cuts that makes it easy to tear off part of a sheet, e.g. a calendar page.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Embossing</b></td><td>A raised or recessed pattern pressed into the paper.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Section type</b></td><td>What a section is in the publication (book block, cover, endpaper, insert, leaflet…) – it sets its format, numbering and place in the spine.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Finish</b></td><td>Extra print finishing: varnish, foil, embossing, white underprint.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Die line</b></td><td>The shape along which the print shop cuts and scores the sheet (envelope, box, sticker); in the PDF a separate CutContour color.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Foil stamping</b></td><td>Shiny foil (gold, silver, colored) transferred onto paper in selected places.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},{"id":"30.3","tytul":"Paper and binding","html":"<div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Book block</b></td><td>All the inside pages of a book together, without the cover.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Booklet</b></td><td>A few sheets folded in half and stapled at the spine; the pages have to be arranged on the sheets accordingly (imposition).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Format</b></td><td>The page size after trimming, e.g. A5 = 148 × 210 mm.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Paper weight</b></td><td>Paper thickness given as the weight of a square meter (g/m²) — among other things, the spine width depends on it.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Spine</b></td><td>The part of the cover between the front and the back; its width depends on the number of pages and the paper. In a spread — where the pages are bound.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Headband</b></td><td>A strip of fabric at the top and bottom of the spine of a hardcover book block.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Dust jacket</b></td><td>A paper cover put on a hardcover, with two flaps folded inside (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"10.11\">Special sections: dust jacket, flyer, calendars, die lines, imposition</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Binding</b></td><td>How the pages are joined: saddle-stitched (staples), perfect bound (softcover), hardcover, spiral.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Spread</b></td><td>The left and right pages side by side, as in an open book.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Signature</b></td><td>A sheet folded into several leaves — a book is sewn from signatures of 4, 8, 16 or 32 pages.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Flap</b></td><td>The part of a dust jacket or cover folded inside behind the cover board.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Ribbon (bookmark)</b></td><td>A narrow ribbon glued into the spine of a hardcover, for marking your place in the book.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Insert</b></td><td>Pages on different paper bound into the middle of a book, e.g. photos on coated paper – without page numbers.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Endpaper</b></td><td>A sheet joining the book block to the hard cover: one half glued to the inside of the cover, the other half is the first (or last) loose leaf.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},{"id":"30.4","tytul":"Text and typography","html":"<div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Line spacing</b></td><td>The distance between consecutive lines of text.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Justification</b></td><td>Aligning text to both edges of the frame — the lines are of equal length.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Baseline</b></td><td>The invisible line the letters “stand” on. A baseline grid aligns lines in neighboring columns and across pages.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Margin</b></td><td>The strip along the edge of the page where no text is placed.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Style</b></td><td>A saved text look, changed in all texts at once.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Widow and orphan</b></td><td>A single line of a paragraph left alone at the end or the beginning of a column or page.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Running head</b></td><td>Text at the top or bottom of the page repeated on many pages, e.g. the chapter title.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},{"id":"30.5","tytul":"Editing and tools","html":"<div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Element</b></td><td>Anything that sits on a page: text, shape, line, image, table, group.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Gradient</b></td><td>A smooth transition between colors in a fill, also into transparency.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Cropping</b></td><td>Choosing the part of a photo that is visible in the frame.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Opacity</b></td><td>The transparency of an element or color: 100% — fully visible, 0% — invisible.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Mock-up</b></td><td>A page-by-page design of a publication; 3D mock-up — a preview of the printed item (book, calendar).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Soft edges</b></td><td>The edge of a photo or shape smoothly fading into transparency.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Guide</b></td><td>A helper line on the page for aligning elements; it doesn't print.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},{"id":"30.6","tytul":"Project and data","html":"<div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>Block (of a series)</b></td><td>A page or a few neighboring pages of a series, repeated together.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Field</b></td><td>A name in braces, e.g. <code>{dzien}</code>; on the finished page it turns into a value.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Document field</b></td><td>A field that comes from the project: page number, section, date.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Section</b></td><td>A part of the publication with its own format, margins and numbering, e.g. the cover and the inside.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Series</b></td><td>Repeating a page or block for consecutive table rows.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Data table</b></td><td>A sheet in the project that fields and series take their values from — not to be confused with a table on a page.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Skipped row</b></td><td>A <code>[skip]</code> row: it takes up a place in the block, but its page isn't created.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Master</b></td><td>Elements shared by many pages: background, footer, page number.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},{"id":"30.7","tytul":"AI","html":"<div class=\"tabela-instrukcji\"><table><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><b>AI assistant</b></td><td>A chat program (e.g. Claude, ChatGPT) that, once the connector is added, reads and changes your projects (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"25.1\">What AI can do</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><b>AI key</b></td><td>Permission for the assistant: which projects it can access, whether it can only read, and how long it's valid (<span class=\"zobacz\">→</span> <a href=\"#\" data-cel=\"25.2\">A key for AI</a>).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Connector</b></td><td>An address with your key through which the AI assistant works in your projects. You see it only once, when you create the key.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Key scope</b></td><td>Selected projects or full access to all of them, including creating and deleting projects.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"}]},
];
